\begin{abstract}
A relativistic mean-field model with a crossing term of isovector-scalar and isoscalar mesons, the Cracow crossing terms
(CCT) model, has previously been shown to be capable of explaining the light and compact object HESS J1731-347.
Here, the model is supplemented with a correct treatment of the phase transition. 
Applying the thermodynamically consistent Gibbs conditions shows that a mixed-phase system occurs
in the core of the neutron star over a wide range of densities, extending up to the neutron star crust,
leading to an intriguing stellar structure with a thick and massive crust.
\end{abstract}


\keywords{HESS J1731-347, relativistic mean-field model, ultra-compact low-mass neutron star, neutron star crust}

