\section{Introduction}
% Motivate study of multi-agent systems, introduce equilibrium notions as a solution concept. Highlight advantages of CE

%Many real-world systems, including environmental management \cite{madani2017serious}, electricity markets~\cite{visudhiphan1999dynamic}, and transportation systems \cite{mylvaganam2017differential}, involve multiple self-interested agents that interact with each other in a dynamic environment. 
Many real-world systems involve multiple self-interested agents that interact with each other in a dynamic environment. Such multi-agent systems can be framed as Markov games, which extend normal-form games to dynamic settings. %In Markov games, several notions of equilibria have emerged as solution concepts in the analysis of multi-agent decision making, such as the Nash equilibrium \cite{nash1951non}, the correlated equilibrium \cite{aumann1974subjectivity, aumann1987correlated}, and the coarse correlated equilibrium \cite{moulin1978strategically}. 
%
%Compared to the Nash equilibrium, the correlated equilibrium has several appealing properties such as: (i) it can be efficiently computed \cite{cesa2006prediction,bubeck2012regret} whereas the Nash equilibrium is computationally intractable \cite{daskalakis2009complexity}, (ii) in terms of social welfare, it can achieve arbitrarily better outcomes than the Nash equilibrium \cite{koutsoupias1999worst, roughgarden2002bad}, and (iii) the notion of a correlated equilibrium arises from independent no-regret learning dynamics~\cite{blum2007external,foster1997calibrated,erez2023regret}. Additionally, any correlated equilibrium is a coarse correlated equilibrium.
%
% Motivate studying games with constraints. Introduce the concept of constrained equilibria. Need to account for more general notions of equilibria. Disucss literature, GNE, constrained CE for different games settings. Boufous versus Chen motivate us to study notion of constrained CE.Motivate study of constrained correlated equilibrium which compared to GNE was less studied but relevant from motivation before. 
%
%The study of equilibria under constraints on agents’ strategy sets has been largely neglected. 
In many such systems, however, agents are subject to constraints, where the feasibility of an individual agent’s strategy depends on the joint strategies of others. Prominent examples arise in environmental management~\cite{madani2017serious}, where countries must jointly ensure that greenhouse emissions are below some threshold, in electricity markets~\cite{visudhiphan1999dynamic}, where transmission capacity constraints must be satisfied, or in transportation systems~\cite{mylvaganam2017differential}, where vehicles must avoid collisions with other vehicles. Such multi-agent systems can be framed as Markov games with coupling constraints \cite{debreu1952social}. To analyze constrained multi-agent decision making, it is crucial to address constrained equilibrium notions. \looseness-1
\\
\\
The \emph{generalized Nash equilibrium} was first introduced in \cite{rosen1965existence} for normal-games as a natural extension of its unconstrained counterpart, the Nash equilibrium \cite{nash1951non}.\footnote{A generalized Nash equilibrium is also referred to as a constrained Nash equilibrium.} Several works have since emerged to study the generalized Nash equilibrium, including its characterization and existence in various game settings, ranging from constrained normal-form games~\cite{rosen1965existence,facchinei2007generalized, dutang2013existence,fischer2014generalized, braouezec2023economic,tian1992existence} to constrained Markov games \cite{altman2000constrained, alvarez2006existence,dufour2022stationary,dufour2024nash,zhang2018continuous}. However, computing a generalized Nash equilibrium is intractable \cite{daskalakis2009complexity}, a property it inherits from the Nash equilibrium.  To circumvent this issue in the unconstrained setting, past works have turned to the weaker correlated equilibrium \cite{aumann1974subjectivity, aumann1987correlated}, which can be computed efficiently \cite{cesa2006prediction,bubeck2012regret,zhang2025learning}. This motivates studying the constrained correlated equilibrium, which was recently introduced by \cite{chen2022finding,boufous2024constrained,bernasconi2023constrained,jaskiewicz2023approximate} and to date, has received less attention. \looseness-1
\\
\\
In constrained Markov games, a Markovian constrained correlated equilibrium is a feasible policy such that any \emph{unilateral modification} is either unprofitable or leads to an infeasible outcome. Different classes of modifications—deterministic vs. stochastic in normal-form games, and additionally, Markovian vs. non-Markovian in Markov games—lead to distinct equilibrium notions. Among these, non-Markovian stochastic modifications form the most general class and define the strongest notion of equilibrium. Understanding whether restricted classes of modifications—such as deterministic or Markovian—can yield equivalent notions of constrained correlated equilibria is a fundamental question. This has practical significance for both computation and learning \cite{dagan2023externalswapregret20,chen2022finding}, as limiting the modification set can enable efficient algorithms \cite{blum2007external,erez2023regret}. For instance, in unconstrained settings, restricting to deterministic modifications enables efficient no-swap regret learning algorithms \cite{blum2007external}. However, in the presence of constraints, such restrictions to deterministic modifications have been shown to yield a weaker equilibrium notion \cite{chen2022finding}. As the structure and characterization of constrained correlated equilibria in terms of possible modifications remain only partially understood, we are led to the following question:
\begin{center}
    \textit{Which classes of modification yield equivalent notions of constrained correlated equilibria?\looseness-1}
\end{center}
Apart from characterization, an important question deals with conditions on the existence of constrained correlated equilibria. In general, in constrained games, agents have to satisfy their own constraints, which may depend on other agents' policies, a setting known as playerwise coupling constraints. For Markov games with playerwise coupling constraints, \cite{altman2000constrained} shows the existence of a constrained Nash equilibrium under a \emph{strong} Slater's condition. The existence of a constrained Nash equilibrium implies the existence of a constrained correlated equilibrium. The strong Slater's condition demands that every agent can modify its policy so that any joint policy is strictly feasible. This is often unrealistic, for example, in transportation systems, it implies that each vehicle can adjust its policy to guarantee collision avoidance with other vehicles.\looseness-1 %From the computation side, it has been shown that in the playerwise coupling constraints setting, computing a constrained correlated equilibrium is intractable, as was recently shown in~\cite{bernasconi2025complexity}. \looseness-1
\\
\\
A possible path to circumvent this issue is to restrict to a setting with common coupling constraints, which are a special case of playerwise coupling constraints, where all agents share the same constraints. Such settings arise in many applications, such as budget caps and safety requirements in environmental management, power grids, and road networks. For normal-form games with common coupling constraints, \cite{boufous2024constrained} proves the existence of a constrained correlated equilibrium assuming a jointly feasible policy exists, which is a much weaker condition than the strong Slater's condition. However, they only consider deterministic modifications and, as discussed above, this leads to a weaker notion of a constrained correlated equilibrium. Thus, it remains unclear whether the relaxation of the strong Slater's condition is due to the use of common coupling constraints, the restriction to normal-form games, or the limitation to deterministic modifications. Motivated by the above, the second question we address in this paper is the following:
\begin{center}
    \textit{In Markov games with common coupling constraints, does a constrained correlated equilibrium exist without the strong Slater's condition?}
\end{center}
\paragraph{Contributions} In this paper, we consider constrained finite-horizon Markov games with finite state and action spaces and provide answers to the two above questions for this setting. We summarize our contributions as follows:\looseness-1


\begin{enumerate}
    \item  We consider constrained correlated equilibria defined with respect to the most general set of non-Markovian stochastic modifications \cite{chen2022finding}. We show that it is sufficient to restrict to (i) Markovian stochastic modifications or (ii) convex combinations of Markovian deterministic modifications, thereby characterizing equivalent notions of Markovian constrained correlated equilibria (Theorem~\ref{cor:equinotion}). 
    \item For Markov games with \emph{playerwise} coupling constraints, we show that the strong Slater's condition is necessary for the existence of constrained correlated equilibria (Example \ref{example1}).\looseness-1

    \item For Markov games with \emph{common} coupling constraints, we prove the existence of a constrained correlated equilibrium under a significantly weakened Slater-type condition (Theorem \ref{thm_main}). This, together with the second point, shows that the strong Slater's condition is an artifact of having playerwise coupling constraints.
\end{enumerate}



