The computational study of equilibria involving constraints on players’ strategies has been largely neglected. However, in real-world applications, players are usually subject to constraints ruling out the feasibility of some of their strategies, such as, e.g., safety requirements and budget caps. Computational studies on constrained versions of the Nash equilibrium have lead to some results under very stringent assumptions, while finding constrained versions of the correlated equilibrium (CE) is still unexplored.

\subsection{Related work}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \cite{altman2000constrained} established the existence of a constrained Nash equilibrium in \emph{infinite-horizon} Markov games with \emph{playerwise} coupling constraints under the \emph{strong Slater's condition}, assuming finite state and action spaces. Subsequent works have extended this result to infinite state and action spaces; see, e.g., \cite{alvarez2006existence, zhang2018continuous, dufour2022stationary, dufour2024nash}.

    \item \cite{boufous2024constrained} established the existence of a constrained correlated equilibrium in \emph{normal-form games} with \emph{common} coupling constraints. Note that normal-form games are a special case of Markov games. While their result guarantees the existence of a constrained correlated equilibrium assuming the existence of a feasible policy, their definition of a correlated equilibrium is restricted to \emph{deterministic} deviations, making it a weaker notion than the one considered in our work \cite[Theorem 1]{chen2022finding}.

    \item In summary, our work focuses on \emph{weakening} the Slater-type assumption while ensuring the existence of constrained correlated equilibria in \emph{finite-horizon Markov games} with \emph{common} coupling constraints, under the more general framework of \emph{stochastic} modifications.
\end{itemize}
