For shorthand, we refer to \emph{Markov games with coupling constraints} as \emph{constrained Markov games}. \looseness-1
\paragraph{Game dynamics} %For each timestep $t$, define history $h_t=(s_1,a_1,\ldots s_{t-1}, a_{t-1},s_t)$ to be a sequence of previous states and actions, as well as the current state and let $\mc H_t=\prod_{h=1}^{t-1}(\mathcal{S}_h \times \mathcal{A}) \times \mathcal{S}_t$ be the set of all histories of length $t$. A policy of player $i$ is a sequence $\pi^i=\{\pi_t^i\}_{t=1}^H$, where $\pi_t^i:\mc H_t\rightarrow\Delta(\mc A_i)$ maps any history of length $t$ to a probability distribution over player $i$'s action space $\mc A_i$. The joint policy is a sequence $\pi=\{\pi_t\}_{t=1}^H$, where $\pi_t:\mc H_t\rightarrow\Delta(\mc A)$ maps any history of length $t$ to a probability distribution over the joint action space $\mc A$. A joint policy $\pi$ is a product policy if for all $t\in[H]$ and all $h_t\in\mc H_t$, $\pi_t(\cdot\lvert h_t)=\pi_t^1(\cdot\lvert h_t)\times\ldots\times\pi_t^N(\cdot\lvert h_t)$ is a product distribution. We denote by $\Pi^i$ the set of player $i$'s policies and by $\Pi$ the set of joint policies.
For \( t \in[H] \), let \( \pi_t : \mathcal{S} \rightarrow \Delta(\mathcal{A}) \) denote a mapping from each state \( s \in \mathcal{S} \) to a distribution over the joint action space \( \mathcal{A} \) at timestep \( t \). We define \( \pi := \{\pi_t\}_{t \in [H]} \) as a {Markovian policy}, and denote the set of all such policies by \( \Pi_M \).

