While recent work has focused on the characterization and computation of constrained correlated equilibria \cite{chen2022finding,bernasconi2023constrained,bernasconi2025complexity}, existence arguments relied on the framework developed by \cite{altman2000constrained}. However, \cite{altman2000constrained} establishes the existence of constrained correlated equilibria in Markov games with \emph{playerwise} coupling constraints, under the following assumption:\footnote{In \cite{altman2000constrained}, the authors prove existence of constrained Nash equilibria for infinite-horizon Markov games, which implies the existence of constrained correlated equilibria. We believe their results can be transferred to the finite-horizon setting by adapting their sensitivity analysis accordingly.}\looseness-1

\begin{assumption}[Strong Slater’s condition]\label{ass:strong_slater}
For any player $i \in \mc N$ and any policy $\pi \in \Pi_M$, there exists a modification \( \phi^i\in\Phi_{M}^i\) such that:
\begin{align*}
V^{g^{i,j}}(\phi^i \circ \pi) > c^{i,j},\,\forall j\in[J].
\end{align*}
\end{assumption}

The next example illustrates that even in normal form games the above assumption is necessary for the existence of a constrained correlated equilibrium in settings with \emph{playerwise} coupling constraints.  

\begin{example}\label{example1}
Consider a 2-player normal-form game, where each player's action space is \(\mA^1 = \mA^2 = \{1,2\}\). Denote the two players as \(P1\) and \(P2\), respectively. The reward functions and the playerwise coupling constraint functions for both players are defined as follows:
\begin{align*}
    &r^1(a^1,a^2) = r^2(a^1,a^2) = \mathds{1}_{(a^1,a^2) = (1,2)},\\
    &g^{1,1}(a^1,a^2) = \mathds{1}_{(a^1,a^2) = (1,1)},\quad g^{2,1}(a^1,a^2) = \mathds{1}_{(a^1,a^2) = (1,2)},
