In this paper, however, we show that strong Slater's condition can be relaxed when considering \emph{common} coupling constraints---where $g^j=g^{i,j}$ and $c^j=c^{i,j}$ for all $i\in\mc N$ and all $j\in[J]$. 
Observe that the terms $i$-feasible and feasible defined in Equation \eqref{eq:i-feasible} are equivalent, and the set of feasible policies $\mc C_\pi$ defined in Equation \eqref{eq:feasible_set} reduces to:
\begin{align}
\mathcal{C}_{\pi} := \{\pi \in \Pi_M \mid V^{g^j}(\pi) \geq c^j, \, \forall j \in [J]\}.\label{eq:cpi}
\end{align}
%
%Furthermore, we define the finite set of non-stationary, non-Markovian deterministic modifications as: $$\Phi_{det}^i=\{\phi^i = \{\phi_t^i\}_{t=1}^{H}\mid \phi_t^i: (\mc S \times \mc A)^{t-1} \times \mc S \times \mAi \to \mAi\}.$$
