Now, we present a relaxation of Assumption \ref{ass:strong_slater}. 

\begin{assumption}\label{ass:weakened_Slater}
    For any player $i \in \mc N$ and any policy $\pi\in\Pi_M$ on the boundary of $\mc C_\pi$, i.e., $\pi \in \{\pi \in \Pi_M \mid \exists j \in [J]\,\,s.t.\,\, V^{g^j}(\pi) = c^j\}$, at least one of the following conditions hold:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item There exists a modification \( \phi^i\in\Phi^i_{M} \) such that $V^{g^{j}}(\phi^i \circ \pi) > c^j,\,\forall j\in[J].$
        \item 
        \begin{enumerate}
        \item For all $j\in[J]$, it holds that:
        $\min_{\phi^i\in\Phi^i_{M}} V^{g^{j}}(\phi^{i}\circ\pi)<c^j$.
        \item There exists an $\alpha\in\Delta(K^i)$ with positive weights, namely $\alpha_k>0$ for all $k\in[K^i]$, such that:
            $$\sum_{k=1}^{K^i}\alpha_kV^{g^j}(\phi^i(k)\circ\pi)\ge c^j,\,\forall j\in[J],$$
             \noindent where $\phi^i(k)\in\Phi^i_{M,det}$.

        \end{enumerate}
    \end{enumerate}
\end{assumption}
Note that the above assumption indeed weakens Assumption \ref{ass:strong_slater}. Specifically, the first condition only requires existence of a strictly feasible modification for policies on the \emph{boundary} of the feasible set $\mc C_\pi$, rather than for all policies $\Pi_M$ as required by the strong Slater's condition. The second condition complements the first and imposes an additional requirement in (b). For illustration, in normal-form games, (b) is equivalent to the existence of a feasible modification in the interior of $\Delta(\mc A^i)$. The next example shows that there exists a Markov game with common coupling constraints admitting a constrained correlated equilibrium, in which the second condition of the above assumption is satisfied, but strong Slater's condition is violated.\looseness-1

