Next, we establish our existence result under our relaxed assumption.


\begin{theorem}\label{thm_main}
    Consider a Markov game with common coupling constraints. Let Assumption \ref{ass:weakened_Slater} hold. Then, there exists a policy $\pi\in\Pi_M$ which is a constrained correlated equilibrium.\looseness-1
\end{theorem}


\paragraph{Proof outline} Equilibrium existence proofs typically rely on fixed-point theorems~\cite{nash1951non,rosen1965existence,altman2000constrained}. The key challenge in our proof lies in constructing a point-to-set map \( f \) that satisfies the conditions of \cite[Kakutani’s fixed point theorem]{kakutani1941generalization} (see Appendix \ref{app:supporting_results}), namely, convexity and compactness of the domain and upper semi-continuity of $f$. We then demonstrate that such a fixed point corresponds to a constrained correlated equilibrium.

Considering \emph{common} coupling constraints ensures that the search for a feasible modification can be restricted to \( \mathcal{C}_\pi \) for every player $i\in\mc N$, rather than $\mc C_\pi^i$ in the case of \emph{playerwise} coupling constraints. However, in Markov games \( \mathcal{C}_\pi \) is generally non-convex \cite[Lemma~3]{ding2020natural}. To overcome this, we consider the set of feasible state-action occupancy \( \mathcal{C}_d \) given by:
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{C}_d = \{ d^\pi \in \Delta(\mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A}) \mid \pi \in \mathcal{C}_\pi \},\label{eq:c_d}
\end{align}
which is convex and compact. This allows us to consider the point-to-set map \( f: \mathcal{C}_d \to 2^{\mathcal{C}_d} \) with domain \( \mathcal{C}_d \), which is crucial for relaxing the strong Slater's condition (Assumption \ref{ass:strong_slater}). We provide the full proof in the next section. 


% Impact and comparison with existing literature
\paragraph{Comparison to related work} 
\citet{altman2000constrained} shows the existence of a constrained Nash equilibrium in Markov games with playerwise coupling constraints. Their proof relies on a fixed-point mapping over the entire policy space $\Pi_M$. Firstly, we consider the common coupling constraint setting, which allows us to restrict the search for feasible modifications to \( \mathcal{C}_d \) in Equation \eqref{eq:c_d}. Secondly, we focus on the constrained correlated equilibrium which is crucial for the convexity of \( \mathcal{C}_d \), a property that does not hold for constrained Nash equilibria \cite[Theorem~1]{chen2024hardness}. 

In the special of normal-form games with common coupling constraints, under the assumption that a feasible policy exists, a relaxation of our Assumption \ref{ass:weakened_Slater}, \cite{boufous2024constrained} establishes the existence of a weaker notion of constrained correlated equilibria by considering only the finite set of deterministic modifications. Note that in normal-form games, there is no horizon, so distinctions between non-Markovian and Markovian modifications are meaningless. In contrast, our setting addresses the more complex case of (i) Markov games with (ii) stochastic modifications. This introduces two key challenges: due to (i) the set $\mc C_d$ in Equation~\eqref{eq:c_d} no longer reduces to $\mc C_\pi$ in Equation~\eqref{eq:cpi}, and (ii) requires searching over infinitely many stochastic modifications. To address this challenge, we leverage the equivalent formulation of the constrained correlated equilibria in Theorem \ref{cor:equinotion} and construct a linear program to find the best feasible $\alpha\in\Delta(K^i)$.

%deterministic modificationsConsidering finitely many deterministic modifications allows the authors in \cite{boufous2024constrained} to define \( f \) as a continuous point-to-point mapping, whereas in our setting, defining $f$ is more challenging as it requires searching over infinitely many convex combinations of deterministic modifications (see Proposition \ref{cor:equinotion}). %We address this challenge by constructing and solving an adequate linear program in the proof presented hereafter.



