\section{Conclusion and future work}\label{sec:conclusion}

% Conclusion
In this paper, we studied constrained correlated equilibria in finite-horizon Markov games with coupling constraints. We addressed two key questions: (i) which classes of modifications yield equivalent definitions of constrained correlated equilibria, and (ii) under what conditions such equilibria exist. Our characterization results show that it suffices to consider either Markovian stochastic modifications or convex combinations of Markovian deterministic modifications, both of which result in equivalent definitions. For existence, we proved that the strong Slater’s condition is necessary in the general setting with \emph{playerwise} coupling constraints, but can be significantly weakened under \emph{common} coupling constraints.\looseness-1

% Future 
A challenging future direction is to establish the existence of constrained Nash equilibria under weakened Slater-type condition in games with common coupling constraints. While our analysis relies on the convexity of the feasible set of state-action occupancy measures, this property does not hold for constrained Nash equilibria requiring a novel analysis to prove existence. Another interesting future direction is to address whether common coupling constraints can bypass the computational intractability found in the computation of constrained correlated with playerwise coupling constraints \cite{bernasconi2025complexity}.
