% \tcr{\paragraph{Proof outline} The proof relies on establishing an equivalence between the state-action occupancy measures induced by non-Markovian stochastic modifications $\Phi^i_{\text{NM}}$, convex combinations of non-Markovian deterministic modifications $\Phi^i_{\text{NM,det}}$, and Markovian stochastic modifications $\Phi^i_{\text{M}}$. To establish such equivalence, the core technical challenge lies in constructing a framework that enables a meaningful comparison between general non-Markovian (potentially stochastic) modifications and their non-Markovian deterministic counterparts for the second condition, and Markovian stochastic counterparts for the third condition. We address this by designing a finite-horizon Markov decision problem (MDP) tailored to player~$i$ (see Equation \eqref{eq:loop-free_MDP} in Appendix \ref{app:thm:MCE}), which captures the joint behavior of all other players under a fixed policy $\pi$. In particular, player $i$'s modification $\phi^i$ is the policy of the constructed MDP, and the induced state-action occupancy measure can be mapped to that of the original Markov game (see Lemma \ref{lemma:eq_occ_ori}). For the second condition, within this MDP, we leverage a known result, namely, there is an equivalence between the state-action occupancy measure induced by any Markovian stochastic policy and the convex combination of state-action occupancy measures induced by non-Markovian deterministic policies (see Lemma \ref{prop:deterministic_modification}). For the third condition, within the MDP, we show that any general modification $\phi^i\in\Phi^i_{NM}$—where $\phi^i$ depends on the full trajectory history—can be mapped to a Markovian stochastic modification $\bar \phi^i \in\Phi^i_{M}$ such that the composed policy $\bar{\phi}^i \circ \pi$ induces the same marginal state-action occupancy over $(s, a)$ as $\phi^i \circ \pi$ (see Lemma \ref{thm:MCE}). The rest of the proof follows by the linearity of the reward $V^{r^i}(\phi^i \circ \p