\section{Equilibrium notions in Markov games}\label{app:equilibrium_notions}

Common solution concepts for an finite-horizon Markov game include the Nash equilibrium, the correlated equilibrium, and the coarse correlated equilibrium. We hereafter define each equilibrium notion.

\begin{definition}[Nash equilibrium (NE)]\label{def:NE}
    A Markovian product policy $\pi =\pi^1\times\ldots\times\pi^N\subseteq\Pi_M$ is a Nash equilibrium if for any player $i \in \mc N$ the following holds:
    \begin{align}
       V^i(\pi) \ge V^i((\ov \pi^i,\pi^{-i})),\quad  \forall\ov\pi^i\in\Pi_M^i,
    \end{align}
    where $(\ov\pi^i,\pi^{-i})= \pi^1\times\ldots\times\pi^{i+1}\times\ov\pi^i\times\pi^{i+1}\times\ldots\times\pi^N$ is the modified product policy.
\end{definition}

In a Nash equilibrium $\pi$, no player can improve its value function by deviating from its policy $\pi^i$ to any other Markovian policy $\ov \pi^i$, and therefore none of the players have an incentive to unilaterally deviate. Next, we define a correlated equilibrium.

\begin{definition}[Correlated equilibrium (CE)] \label{def:CE}
    A joint Markovian policy $\pi\subseteq\Pi_M$ is a correlated equilibrium if for any player $i \in \mc N$ and for any stochastic modification $\phi^i = \{\phi_t^i\}_{t=1}^{H}$ with $\phi_t^i: \mc S \times \mAi \to \Delta(\mAi)$ the following holds:
    \begin{align}
       V^i(\pi) \ge V^i(\phi^i \circ \pi),
    \end{align}
    where the modified policy $\phi^i\circ\pi=\{\phi_t^i\circ\pi_t\}_{t=1}^H$ is given by:
    \begin{align*}
        (\phi_t^i\circ\pi_t)((\ov a_t^i,a_t^{-i})\lvert h_t) =\sum_{a_t^i\in\mc A^i} \phi_t^i(\ov a_t^i\lvert s_t, a_t^i)\pi_t(a_t\lvert s_t),\quad
        \forall (\ov a_t^i,a_t^{-i})\in\mc A,
        \forall h_t\in\mc S.
    \end{align*}
\end{definition}
In a correlated equilibrium $\pi$, no player can improve its value function by employing a Markovian stochastic modification $\phi^i$ to its action induced by the joint Markovian policy $\pi$. In \cite[Theorem~1]{chen2022finding} it was shown that replacing stochastic modifications with deterministic modifications $\phi^i=\{\phi_t^i\}_{t=1}^H$ with $\phi_t^i:\mc S\times\mc A^i\rightarrow \mc A^i$, does not result in a weaker notion of a correlated equilibrium. In other words, a joint Markovian policy $\pi$ is a correlated equilibrium regardless of whether deterministic or stochastic modifications are considered. %It is furthermore sufficient to consider only Markovian modifications of the form $\phi^i=\{\phi_t^i\}_{t=1}^H$, where $\phi_t^i:\mc S\times\mc A^i\rightarrow\Delta(\mc A^i)$. Namely, a joint Markovian policy $\pi$ is a correlated equilibrium if it is robust to any player employing a deterministic Markovian modification $\phi^i$ to its action induced by the joint policy $\pi$. We provide the full statement and a proof in the Appendix \ref{app:equivalent_ce_notion} as it is not the main focus of this paper.
Lastly, we define a coarse correlated equilibrium.

\begin{definition}[Coarse correlated equilibrium (CCE)]\label{def:CCE}
    A joint Markovian policy $\pi\subseteq\Pi_M$ is a coarse correlated equilibrium if for any player $i \in \mc N$ the following holds:
    \begin{align}
       V^i(\pi) \ge V^i((\ov \pi^i,\pi^{-i})),\quad \forall\ov \pi^i\in\Pi_M^i,
    \end{align}
    where $(\ov \pi^i,\pi^{-i})=\ov \pi^i\times\pi^{-i}$ is the modified joint policy.
\end{definition}

In a coarse correlated equilibrium $\pi$, no player can improve its value function by deviating from its policy $\pi^i$ to any other Markovian policy $\ov \pi^i$, and therefore none of the players have an incentive to unilaterally deviate. The main difference between a Nash equilibrium and a coarse correlated equilibrium is that the former is a product policy whereas the latter is a joint policy. Compared to a correlated equilibrium, a coarse correlated equilibrium merely protects against unconditional unilateral deviations $\ov \pi^i$.

A Nash equilibrium is guaranteed to exist for finite-horizon Markov games with finite state- and action spaces \cite{filar2012competitive}. However, computing a Nash equilibrium even in a two-player game is PPAD-complete \cite{daskalakis2009complexity, chen2009settling}. Thus, two relaxations of the Nash equilibrium were introduced - the correlated and the coarse correlated equilibrium - each more permissive and more computationally tractable than the previous one \cite{daskalakis2023complexity}. Specifically, the following inclusion holds in finite-horizon Markov games:
\begin{align*}
    NE \subseteq CE \subseteq CCE,
\end{align*}
where NE, CE, and CCE denote the set of all Nash equilibria, correlated equilibria, and coarse correlated equilibria defined in Definitions \ref{def:NE} - \ref{def:CCE}, respectively. This inclusion implies the existence of a correlated and a coarse correlated equilibrium in finite-horizon Markov games. 


\section{Equivalent definition of a CE in finite-horizon Markov games}\label{app:equivalent_ce_notion}

We further prove that it is sufficient to consider only Markovian modifications of the form $\phi^i=\{\phi_t^i\}_{t=1}^H$, where $\phi_t^i:\mc S\times\mc A^i\rightarrow\Delta(\mc A^i)$. Namely, a joint Markovian policy $\pi$ is a correlated equilibrium if it is robust to any player employing a deterministic Markovian 
modification $\phi^i$ to its action induced by the joint policy $\pi$.

\begin{theorem}
A joint Markovian policy $\pi\subseteq\Pi_M$ is a correlated equilibrium if for any player $i \in [N]$ and for any deterministic Markovian modification $\phi^i = \{\phi_t^i\}_{t=1}^{H}$ with $\phi_t^i: \mc S \times \mAi \to \mAi$ the following holds:
    \begin{align}
       V^i(\pi) \ge V^i(\phi^i \circ \pi).
    \end{align}
\end{theorem}


Previous works claim that restricting modifications to Markovian modifications is in general limiting \citep{foster2023hardness, cai2024near}, since a player's best response may be non-Markovian even if all other players use Markovian policies. Furthermore, \cite[Proposition B.1]{foster2023hardness} provides an example showing that all Markovian modifications yield a value that is suboptimal compared to the value attained by the best non-Markovian modification. However, their example does not contradict our theorem as they assume that players' policies are independent while a correlated equilibrium is a joint policy. In particular, our theorem establishes that restricting to the set of Markovian modifications does not yield a weaker notion of a correlated equilibrium; instead, it defines an equivalent notion. This invalidates the claim made in \cite{cai2024near}. \tcr{Think if here is the right place: More interestingly, in the context of designing an efficient algorithm that, when adopted independently by all players in a Markov game, ensures sublinear regret for each individual player, \cite{erez2023regret} achieves a no-regret guarantee by considering Markovian modifications, while V-learning \citep{jin2021v,cai2024near} and SPoCMAR \citep{daskalakis2023complexity} fail to achieve such guarantees when considering non-Markovian deviations. These results highlight the advantages of considering Markovian modifications not only for designing efficient decentralized algorithms but also for ensuring no-regret guarantees.}

