\begin{abstract}
Neural network (NN)-based Digital Predistortion (DPD) stands out in improving signal quality in wideband radio frequency (RF) power amplifiers (PAs) employing complex modulation. However, NN DPDs usually rely on a large number of parameters for effective linearization and can significantly contribute to the energy consumption of the digital back-end in RF systems. This paper presents \texttt{OpenDPDv2}, a unified framework for PA modeling, DPD learning, and model optimization to reduce power consumption while maintaining high linearization performance. The optimization techniques feature a novel DPD algorithm, TRes-DeltaGRU, alongside two energy-efficient methods. The top-performing 32-bit floating-point (FP32) TRes-DeltaGRU-DPD model achieves an Adjacent Channel Power Ratio (ACPR) of -59.4\,dBc and Error Vector Magnitude (EVM) of -42.1\,dBc. By exploiting fixed-point quantization and dynamic temporal sparsity of input signals and hidden neurons, the inference energy of our model can be reduced by 4.5$\times$ while still maintaining -50.3\,dBc \ac{ACPR} and -35.2\,dB in \ac{EVM} with 56\% temporal sparsity evaluated with a TM3.1a 200MHz-BW 256-QAM OFDM signal applied to a 3.5 GHz GaN Doherty RF PA. \texttt{OpenDPDv2} code, datasets, and documentation are publicly accessible at \textcolor{red}{\url{https://github.com/lab-emi/OpenDPD}}.
\end{abstract}


\begin{IEEEkeywords}
digital predistortion (DPD), temporal sparsity, power amplifier (PA), recurrent neural network (RNN), digital signal processing (DSP)
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} exponential growth of wireless data traffic has driven growing demands for broader spectrum usage, higher data rates and fewer error occurrences in modern wireless communication systems. However, wideband \ac{RF} \ac{PAs}, as an essential component in TX systems, degrade the signal quality by inherent distortions. These distortions raise out-of-band emissions power and in-band symbol errors, which negatively affect both the reliability of communications and energy efficiency. To address these challenges, \ac{DPD} has become a promising method.

\ac{DPD} aims to derive an inverse function of \ac{PAs} transfer function to compensate for nonlinearity before the signal passes through the \ac{RF} transmitter. Conventional \ac{DPD} methods based on Volterra-series deliver effective linearization in narrowband scenarios but struggle with wideband and high-constellation modulated signals to satisfy communication standards~\cite{3GPP}. In contrast, \ac{ML} techniques, such as \ac{NN}-based \ac{DPD}, outperform \ac{GMP} model for \ac{OFDM} signal with bandwidth higher than 200\,MHz and meet the requirement of communication standard~\cite{PGJANET,DVRJANET,PNRNN,BOJANET}. 

However, the DPD module consumes a substantial portion of power in wideband radio digital back-ends~\cite{wesemann2023energy}. The incorporation of \ac{NNs} could further intensify this power issue. The power usage in digital signal processors, contrary to the purpose of the efficient implementation for wideband transmitters in basestations and Wi-Fi routers, where power resources are constrained.

Previous approaches to reduce DPD energy consumption include lowering the sample rate~\cite{Li2020SampleRate}, employing a sub-Nyquist feedback receiver in the observation path~\cite{Hammler2019}, dynamically adjusting model cross-terms based on input signal characteristics~\cite{Li2022}, simplifying computational pathways for DPD algorithms~\cite{Beikmirza2023}, and pruning less critical weights in fully connected layers to achieve static spatial weight sparsity~\cite{Liu2022}. 

This article presents \texttt{OpenDPDv2}, a unified open-source \ac{NN}-based DPD framework for accurate PA modeling, DPD learning, and in-situ model optimization towards reduced arithmetic operations and energy-expensive memory accesses, and goes beyond our previous works~\cite{wu2024opendpd,Wu2024IMS,WuIMS2025,Duan2025TCN-DPD}. \texttt{OpenDPDv2} incorporates a new high-performance hybrid-\ac{NN}-based DPD algorithm with high linearization performance and optimized energy efficiency through dynamic temporal sparsity and quantization-aware training. The main contributions are:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item We present \texttt{OpenDPDv2}, an open-source, end-to-end (\textbf{E2E}) learning framework crafted in \texttt{PyTorch}~\cite{Paszke2019PyTorch} to enable fast prototyping of accurate and power-efficient \ac{NN}-based DPD algorithms by streamlining learning and optimization through DPD quantization and temporal sparsity exploitation.

    \item \texttt{OpenDPDv2} comes with a new TM3.1a (256\,QAM-\ac{OFDM}) 5-channel $\times$ 40\,MHz (200\,MHz) test signal dataset, measured from a 3.5\,GHz Ampleon GaN \ac{PA} @ 41.5\,dBm average output power, named \texttt{APA\_200MHz}, for free access of the community to precise \ac{PA} measurement data for modeling, \ac{DPD} learning and potential development of other digital signal processing algorithms and systems.

    \item \texttt{OpenDPDv2} embeds a new \ac{TRes}-DeltaGRU \ac{NN} \ac{DPD} algorithm, achieving decent measured linearization performance at -59.4\,dBc \ac{ACPR} with 1000 32-bit floating-point (FP) parameters.

    \item We provide energy estimation results derived from a simulated ARM CPU platform using \texttt{Gem5}, a widely-adopted open-source computer architecture simulator, providing accurate statistics of committed instructions and memory access patterns of a practical processor. \texttt{OpenDPDv2} managed to reduce 4.5$\times$ the inference energy consumption of a 1000-parameter \ac{TRes}-DeltaGRU \ac{DPD} model while still maintain -50.3\,dBc \ac{ACPR} and -35.2\,dB \ac{EVM} for the TM3.1a 200MHz test signal.

\end{enumerate}

\section{NN-Based DPD's Power Issue}
\subsection{Overview}
This section provides a comprehensive analysis of power consumption in \ac{NN}-based \ac{DPD} systems to prepare for later discussions and identifies pathways toward energy-efficient implementations suitable for mass deployment.

Figure~\ref{fig:dpd_power} (a) illustrates the operational cycle of a practical \ac{DPD} system, where periodic IQ data collection and model parameter updates (adaptation) interleave with continuous DPD inference. This cyclic operation reveals that the total power consumption of an \ac{NN}-based \ac{DPD} engine, $P_\text{DPD}$, can be decomposed into three principal components:

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
P_{\text{DPD}} &= P_{\text{INF}} + P_{\text{SAM}} + P_{\text{ADA}} \\
&= \frac{E_{\text{INF}}}{T_\text{DPD}} + \frac{E_{\text{SAM}}}{T_\text{DPD}} + \frac{E_{\text{ADA}}}{T_\text{DPD}} \\
\label{eq:dpd_pwr_total}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\noindent where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $T_\text{DPD}$: cyclic period of \ac{DPD} operation, spanning between the starting point of data sample collections from the \ac{TOR}~\cite{hammler2019receiver} in neighboring \ac{DPD} operation cycles, which includes high-speed \ac{ADC}, down-conversion, and associated processing for online adaptation.
\item $E_{\mathrm{INF}}$: energy consumed during DPD inference (INF), encompassing per‐sample forward pass of the \ac{NN} DPD model throughout $T_\text{DPD}$.
\item $E_{\mathrm{SAM}}$: energy drawn by the \ac{TOR} during IQ data sample (SAM) collection.
\item $E_{\mathrm{ADA}}$: energy expended in updating the \ac{DPD} model parameters for online adaptation (ADA).
\end{itemize}


\subsection{Inference Power ($P_{\text{INF}}$): The Dominant Factor}
In a streaming transmitter, DPD inference operates continuously throughout $T_\text{DPD}$, processing each incoming I/Q sample at the sampling rate $f_s$. The inference energy consumption is fundamentally tied to the computational complexity of the \ac{NN} model:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
  E_{\text{INF}} &= E_\text{F} \cdot N_\text{F} = E_\text{F} \cdot f_{s} \cdot T_\text{DPD},
\label{eq:dpd_pwr_inf}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\noindent where: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item $E_\text{F}$: energy per forward pass, encompassing arithmetic operations (multiplications, additions, nonlinear activations) and memory accesses.
    \item $N_{F}$: total number of forward passes during $T_\text{DPD}$.
\end{itemize}

Consequently, the \ac{DPD} inference power consumption becomes:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    P_{\text{INF}} &= \frac{E_{\text{INF}}}{T_\text{DPD}} = E_\text{F} \cdot f_{\text{s}}
  \label{eq:power}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

As wireless systems push toward multi-GHz sampling rates for wider bandwidths, inference power will scale proportionally and potentially reach prohibitive levels.
%-------------------------------------
\begin{figure}[!t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{figures/DPDpower2.pdf}
    \caption{(a) Qualitative Analysis of EVM with DPD vs. Time. (b) A practical case study of power breakdown across different adaptation periods.}
    \label{fig:dpd_power}
\end{figure}
%-------------------------------------
\subsection{Data Collection Power ($P_{\text{SAM}}$): The Periodic Overhead}
Maintaining accurate linearization requires periodic acquisition of high-quality I/Q samples from the \ac{TOR}. The power contribution from this sampling process is:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
P_{\text{SAM}}
  &= \frac{P_{\text{TOR}} \cdot T_{\text{SAM}}}{T_{\text{DPD}}} \\
  &\approx \frac{P_{\text{ADC}} \cdot N_{\text{SAM}}}{T_{\text{DPD}} \cdot f_{s}},
\label{eq:dpd_pwr_dat}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\noindent where $P_{\text{TOR}}$ represents the \ac{TOR}'s active power consumption approximating the \ac{ADC} power, which is the dominant part. $T_\text{SAM}=N_{\text{SAM}}/f_{s}$ denotes the data acquisition duration (as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:dpd_power} (a)), and $N_\text{SAM}$ is the number of collected I/Q samples.

Notably, this component exhibits an inverse relationship with the adaptation period $T_\text{DPD}$, allowing system designers to trade off adaptation frequency against power consumption based on PA characteristics.

\subsection{Adaptation Power ($P_{\text{ADA}}$): The Online Learning Cost}
While most existing \ac{NN}-based \ac{DPD} studies focus on offline implementations, practical deployments must address long-term PA variations, including thermal drift, electron trapping, and bias-point shifts that evolve over microseconds to milliseconds~\cite{Arne2025MWTL}. Online adaptation via backpropagation~\cite{rumelhart1986learning} incurs significant computational overhead, with each epoch requiring both forward (F) passes and backward (B) passes. Given that each backward pass typically requires 2–3$\times$ the computational resources of a forward pass, the adaptation power consumption can be estimated as:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
P_{\text{ADA}}
  &= \frac{\left(E_{\text{F}}+E_{\text{B}}\right) \cdot N_{\text{EPO}}\cdot N_{\text{SAM}}}{T_\text{DPD}} \\
  &\approx \frac{4E_{\text{F}} \cdot N_{\text{EPO}} \cdot N_{\text{SAM}}}{T_\text{DPD}}.
\label{eq:dpd_pwr_ada}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $E_{\text{B}}$ is the energy per backward pass and $N_{\text{EPO}}$ is the number of online adaptation epochs.

\subsection{DPD Power Breakdown: A Practical Case Study}


Synthesizing Eqs.~\ref{eq:dpd_pwr_total}, \ref{eq:dpd_pwr_inf}, \ref{eq:dpd_pwr_dat}, and~\ref{eq:dpd_pwr_ada}, the total DPD power consumption becomes:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
P_{\text{DPD}}
  &= E_\text{F} \cdot f_{s} + \frac{P_{\text{ADC}} \cdot N_{\text{SAM}}}{T_\text{DPD} \cdot f_{s}} + \frac{4E_{\text{F}} \cdot N_{\text{EPO}}\cdot N_{\text{SAM}}}{T_{\text{DPD}}}
\label{eq:dpd_pwr_final}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

To ground this analysis in practical numbers, consider a TM3.1a test signal with 200 MHz baseband bandwidth sampled at 983.04 MHz (approximated to 1 GHz for clarity). Below are the assumptions:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Forward pass energy}: $E_\text{F}=10$\,nJ per forward pass for a 1000-parameter \ac{GRU}-based DPD~\cite{WuIMS2025};
\item \textbf{Data collection}: assuming $P_{\text{ADC}} \approx 1$\,W for 12-bit precision at 1\,GHz sample rate~\cite{adc12sj1600};
\item \textbf{Adaptation parameters}: $N_{\text{EPO}} = 10$ epochs, $N_\text{SAM}=6600$ samples (normalized from 2000 samples at 300\,MHz~\cite{Li2021pretrain});
\item \textbf{Update period}: $T_\text{DPD}$ ranging from 1\,ms to 1\,s;
\end{itemize}

Figure~\ref{fig:dpd_power} (b) presents the power breakdown across different adaptation periods. For $T_\text{DPD}=1$\,ms, the total power consumption reaches:
\begin{align*}
P_{\text{DPD}} &= P_{\text{INF}} + P_{\text{SAM}} + P_{\text{ADA}} \\
&= 10.0 + 0.0066 + 2.64 = 12.65 \quad \text{(W)}
\end{align*}


This analysis reveals a critical insight: inference power dominates the total consumption (>79\%), even with aggressive adaptation rates. For $T_\text{DPD}=10$\,ms, the total power consumption is approximately 10.26\,W. As $T_\text{DPD}$ increases toward more practical values (e.g., 1\,s), the inference component exceeds 99\% of total power, while adaptation and sampling contributions become negligible. The reason this $P_{\text{DPD}}$ is way higher than common \ac{DPD} power numbers showing up in industrial product datasheets is because here we assume a completely real-time streaming large complex \ac{NN} \ac{DPD} @ 1\,GHz sample rate under 100\% duty cycle, which does not exist in any physical product yet.

\subsection{Implications for Power-Efficient Wideband DPD Design}
As the inference power dominates and scales linearly with model complexity and sampling rate, the primary path towards energy-efficient \ac{NN} DPD lies in aggressively reducing $E_\text{F}$ through model compression, such as quantization~\cite{Wu2024IMS} and model sparsification methods~\cite{WuIMS2025, Liu2022,li2024gpu}, dedicated hardware accelerators on FPGAs~\cite{versluis2025sparsedpd} or even in ASICs~\cite{Li2025ISCAS}, and low-sampling-rate \ac{DPD}~\cite{Li2020SampleRate} or sub-Nyquist \ac{TOR} techniques~\cite{hammler2019sub}.

Therefore, in this work, we integrate the DPD learning and optimization through model quantization and temporally sparsification to reduce \ac{DPD} forward pass complexity, which contributes to the reduction of $P_\text{INF}$ and $P_\text{ADA}$. 

% Complementary strategies include intelligent adaptation that extends $T_{DPD}$ by updating only when linearization metrics degrade, and system-level optimizations such as bandwidth-aware processing and multi-rate architectures. The convergence of these approaches—algorithmic efficiency, hardware optimization, and adaptive intelligence—will enable practical deployment of \ac{NN}-based DPD in future wideband transmitters while maintaining acceptable power budgets.

