\section{OpenDPDv2}
\label{seq:DeltaDPDv2}
%----------------------------------------
\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/OpenDPDv2.pdf}
    \caption{Learning and optimization flow of \texttt{OpenDPDv2}.}
    \label{fig:opendpdv2}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure*}[!t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/DeltaDPD.pdf}
    \caption{(a) Temporal sparsity in RNN-based DPD. (b) Schematic of TRes-DeltaGRU. (c) Unfolded TRes-DeltaGRU DPD at $t$ and $t+1$. (d) Layers of TCN Residual Path.}
    \label{fig:DeltaDPD}
\end{figure*}

\texttt{OpenDPDv2} extends \texttt{OpenDPDv1}~\cite{wu2024opendpd} with the new \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU} algorithm and two optimization methodologies, including temporal sparsity through delta threshold mechanisms and quantization-aware training for hardware-efficient deployment. As depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:opendpdv2}, the architecture of \texttt{OpenDPDv2} comprises four primary steps. Steps 1 and 2 maintain the foundational framework of \texttt{OpenDPDv1}, while steps 3 and 4 introduce the temporal sparse DPD learning and quantization-aware learning methodologies:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Data Acquisition \& PA Modeling (Fig.~\ref{fig:opendpdv2}a):} The baseband input I/Q signal is represented as $\mathbf{X} = \{\mathbf{x}_t | \mathbf{x}_t = I_\mathbf{x_t} + jQ_\mathbf{x_t}, I_\mathbf{x_t}, Q_\mathbf{x_t} \in \mathbb{R}, t \in 0,\dots ,T-1\}$. Transmitting it through the \ac{PA}, the baseband output signals are captured as $\mathbf{Y} = \{\mathbf{y}_t | \mathbf{y}_t = I_\mathbf{y_t} + jQ_\mathbf{y_t}, I_\mathbf{y_t}, Q_\mathbf{y_t} \in \mathbb{R}, t \in 0,\dots ,T-1\}$. A behavioral PA model, $\pi_{PA}$, is trained via \ac{BPTT} to minimize the \ac{MSE} between predicted $\hat{\mathbf{y}}_{PA}[n]$ and ground truth \ac{PA} output $\mathbf{y}[n]$.

\item \textbf{Temporally-Sparse DPD Learning (Fig.~\ref{fig:opendpdv2}b):} Building upon the DPD learning step of \texttt{OpenDPDv1}, \texttt{OpenDPDv2} introduces the \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU} algorithm with Delta threshold mechanisms. The Delta \ac{NN}-based DPD model employs temporal sparsity through delta thresholds $\Theta_{\phi}$ and $\Theta_h$ to achieve computational efficiency. The Delta DPD model $\pi_{DPD}$ is cascaded before the pre-trained, frozen $\pi_{PA}$, to form the cascaded model $\pi_{CAS}$. Using BPTT, $\pi_{CAS}$ is trained to approximate a linear transfer function with $\mathbf{x}[n]$ as input and $G\mathbf{x}[n]$ as output, enabling $\pi_{DPD}$ to learn the inverse of the PA behavior.

\item \textbf{Quantization-Aware Optimization (Fig.~\ref{fig:opendpdv2}c):} \texttt{OpenDPDv2} implements quantization-aware training as the fourth step~\cite{Nagel2021} to optimize the \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU} DPD model for hardware deployment. During training, full-precision variables are updated during gradient descent while quantized values are used in forward propagation. This methodology reduces computational complexity and enables energy-efficient hardware implementation while maintaining model performance.
\end{enumerate}

%--------------------------------------
\subsection{The TRes-DeltaGRU Algorithm}
%--------------------------------------
Besides the learning and optimization architecture, \texttt{OpenDPDv2} is embedded with a novel high-performance hybrid-\ac{NN} DPD model, \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU}, leveraging light-weight temporal convolution residual path and temporal sparsity of \ac{GRU}.
%--------------------------------------
\subsubsection{Dynamic Temporal Sparsity in DPD input signals and hidden neurons}
%--------------------------------------

 The delta processing concept emerges from analyzing the temporal characteristics of \ac{DPD} signals. We present the input feature set $\begin{bmatrix} I_{x_t},\,Q_{x_t},\,|x_t|,\,|x_t|^3 \end{bmatrix}$, as an example for the \ac{DPD} task. When processing continuous sequential signals using \textbf{NNs}, the changes between consecutive time steps ($\Delta$) in input neurons, denoted as $\Delta\phi$, and hidden state vectors, denoted as $\Delta h$, are depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:DeltaDPD} (a). The Delta data in Figure~\ref{fig:DeltaDPD} (a) are obtained from a 200\,MHz OFDM signal and a 15-hidden-neuron vector employed in our proposed \ac{GRU}-based model for the \ac{DPD} task. In Figure~\ref{fig:DeltaDPD} (a), white pixels indicate Delta values close to zero, suggesting a redundant representation over time. Consequently, the \ac{DPD} model can exploit this temporal stability in both the $\Delta\phi$ and $\Delta h$ sequences to transform dense-matrix-dense-vector multiplication \textbf{M$\times$V} into dense-matrix-sparse-vector multiplication (\textbf{M$\times$SV}). 
 % This approach facilitates a reduction in computational complexity and memory access while preserving accuracy with minimal loss of performance.

%--------------------------------------
\subsubsection{Temporal Residual Path}
%--------------------------------------

In the initial version of DeltaDPD, the \ac{GRU} layer processes the input feature and its memory effect with a recurrent structure. The predicted DPD outputs are generated by a final \ac{FC} layer:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{\hat{u}}_{t} &= \mathbf{W}_{\hat{y}}\boldsymbol{h}_{t} + \mathbf{b}_{\hat{y}}
\end{align}
However, with this output layer, the sparsity of the pre-distorted \ac{DPD} output $\mathbf{\hat{u}}_{t}$ directly depends on the sparsity of the hidden state $\boldsymbol{h}_{t}$. For instance, a 52\% sparsity in DeltaDPD~\cite{WuIMS2025} reduces the average sampling rate of $\mathbf{\hat{u}}_{t}$ from 983.04\,MHz to approximately 471.86\,MHz, potentially degrading linearization performance. To address this dependency, we introduce a residual connection between the input and output layers. While a simple \ac{FC} layer with the current input step as the residual path could suffice, prior studies~\cite{PNRNN, ARVTDNN} suggest that incorporating a memory window enhances \ac{DPD} performance. However, a large memory window increases the \ac{FC} layer’s number of parameters. To mitigate this, we employ a \ac{TCN} as the residual path, balancing efficiency and performance, as detailed in Figure~\ref{fig:DeltaDPD} (b). 

%--------------------------------------
\subsubsection{TRes-DeltaGRU}
%--------------------------------------

To summarize, the schematic of the expanded \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU} approach over time is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:DeltaDPD} (c). \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU} approach employs Delta thresholds $\Theta_{\phi}$ and $\Theta_{h}$ to bypass multiply-accumulate (MAC) operations and memory accesses associated with $\Delta$ vector elements below these thresholds, along with their corresponding weight columns. In the \ac{GRU} block of Fig.~\ref{fig:DeltaDPD} (c), all gray elements represent those skipped during processing.

The sequential \textbf{M$\times$V} operation can be converted to a delta operation as follows:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{y}_t &= \mathbf{W} \mathbf{v}_t, \label{eq1} \\
\mathbf{y}_t &= \mathbf{W} \Delta\mathbf{v}_t + \mathbf{y}_{t-1} = \mathbf{W} (\mathbf{v}_t - \mathbf{v}_{t-1}) + \mathbf{y}_{t-1}, \label{eq2}
\end{align}
where $\mathbf{v}_t$ represents either the \ac{RNN} input $\boldsymbol{\phi}_t$ or the hidden state $\mathbf{h}_t$ vector at time $t$, $\mathbf{W}$ denotes the weight matrices, and $\mathbf{y}_{t-1}$ is the result of the \textbf{M$\times$V} operation from the previous time step $t-1$. In Eq.~\ref{eq2}, the term $\mathbf{W} \Delta\mathbf{v}_t$ transitions to a dense-matrix-sparse-vector multiplication (\textbf{M$\times$SV}) when only computations corresponding to $\Delta\mathbf{v}_t$ elements exceeding the threshold are retained, as described by:
\begin{align}
\Delta\mathbf{v}_t &= \begin{cases}
\mathbf{v}_t - \tilde{\mathbf{v}}_{t-1}, & |\mathbf{v}_t - \tilde{\mathbf{v}}_{t-1}| > \Theta_{v}, \\
0, & |\mathbf{v}_t - \tilde{\mathbf{v}}_{t-1}| \leq \Theta_{v}, \label{eq4}
\end{cases}
\end{align}
where $\tilde{\mathbf{v}}$ serves as a memory buffer to store the previous state. To mitigate error accumulation over time by tracking only the most recent significant change, each $k$-th scalar element $\tilde{v}^{k}$ of the vector $\tilde{\mathbf{v}}$ is updated only when the corresponding $\Delta v^{k}$ surpasses the threshold. This updating rule is defined as:
\begin{align}
\tilde{v}^{k}_{t-1} &= \begin{cases}
v^{k}_{t-1}, & |v^{k}_t - \tilde{v}^{k}_{t-1}| > \Theta_{v}, \\
\tilde{v}^{k}_{t-2}, & |v^{k}_t - \tilde{v}^{k}_{t-1}| \leq \Theta_{v}, \label{eq5}
\end{cases}
\end{align}
Compared our initial stage work, we replace the $\,sin\theta_t,\,cos\theta_t $ in DeltaGRU input feature with a small memory window of input. Therefore, the input feature becomes $\boldsymbol{\phi}_t = \begin{bmatrix} I_t,\,Q_t,\,I_{t+1}, Q_{t+1},|x_t|,\,|x_t|^3 \end{bmatrix}$, leading to DeltaGRU equations in their delta forms as follows Eqs.~\ref{eq10}$\sim$\ref{eq13}:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{r}_t &= \sigma\left(\mathbf{M}_{r,t}\right), \label{eq10}\\
\mathbf{z}_t &= \sigma\left(\mathbf{M}_{z,t}\right), \\
\mathbf{n}_t &= \tanh\left(\mathbf{M}_{n\phi,t} + \mathbf{r}_t \odot \mathbf{M}_{nh,t}\right), \\
\mathbf{h}_t &= \left(1 - \mathbf{z}_t\right) \odot \mathbf{h}_{t-1} + \mathbf{z}_t \odot \mathbf{n}_t\label{eq13}
\end{align}
The terms $M_{r}$, $M_{z}$, $M_{n}$ are the pre-activation accumulation of DeltaGRU's reset gate $r$, update gate $z$ and new gate $n$, initialized by the biases of gates $\mathbf{M}_{r,0} = \mathbf{b}_{ir}$, $\mathbf{M}_{z,0} = \mathbf{b}_{iz}$, $\mathbf{M}_{n\phi,0} = \mathbf{b}_{in}$, $\mathbf{M}_{nh,0} = \mathbf{b}_{hn}$ and defined by:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{M}_{r,t} &= \mathbf{W}_{ir} \Delta\boldsymbol{\phi}_t + \mathbf{W}_{hr} \Delta\mathbf{h}_{t-1} + \mathbf{M}_{r,t-1}, \label{eq15}\\
\mathbf{M}_{z,t} &= \mathbf{W}_{iz} \Delta\boldsymbol{\phi}_t + \mathbf{W}_{hz} \Delta\mathbf{h}_{t-1} + \mathbf{M}_{z,t-1}, \label{eq16}\\
\mathbf{M}_{n\phi,t} &= \mathbf{W}_{in} \Delta\boldsymbol{\phi}_t + \mathbf{M}_{n\phi,t-1}, \label{eq17}\\
\mathbf{M}_{nh,t} &= \mathbf{W}_{hn} \Delta\mathbf{h}_{t-1} + \mathbf{M}_{nh,t-1}, \label{eq18}
\end{align}

The final output of the \ac{DPD} module, $\mathbf{U}$, is expressed as:
\begin{align}
\begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{I_u},\mathbf{Q_u}\end{bmatrix}=\mathbf{U} &= \mathbf{\hat{U}} +\text{TCN} (\mathbf{X})
\end{align}
where $\mathbf{\hat{U}} = \{\mathbf{\hat{u}}_t | \mathbf{\hat{u}}_t = \mathbf{W}_{\hat{y}}\boldsymbol{h}_{t} + \mathbf{b}_{\hat{y}}, \, \boldsymbol{h}_{t}\in \mathbb{R}^H, n \in 0,\dots ,T-1\}$. $H$ is the hidden size of DeltaGRU.

The core of the \ac{TCN} is dilated convolutions, which perform convolution on neighboring parameters in the convolutional kernel at intervals of $D$, rather than on consecutive time points of the input, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:DeltaDPD} (d). This approach expands the receptive field of a \ac{CNN} with a kernel size of $K$ from $K$ to $(K-1)\times D + K$ without increasing the parameter count. The \ac{TCN} residual path is defined as: 
\begin{align}
    \text{TCN}= [\text{Dilated Conv1D},\text{Hardswish},\text{Conv1D},\text{Hardswish}]
\end{align}
where the Hardswish activation function is:
\begin{align}
    y=x\cdot \text{clip}(\frac{x+3}{6},0,1)
\end{align}



%-------------------------------
\subsection{Quantization-Aware DPD Learning}
\label{mixed-precision-gru}
%-------------------------------

~\cite{horowitz20141} and ~\cite{jouppi2021ten} demonstrates that \textbf{MAC} operations using 8-bit fixed-point integers (\textbf{INT8}) achieve up to 20$\times$ greater energy efficiency than \textbf{FP32} \textbf{MAC} operations in 45nm and 7nm technology nodes. Although 32-bit floating-point (\textbf{FP32}) arithmetic improves precision, it elevates $E_{\text{INF}}$, reducing energy efficiency. Previous research shows that \ac{DNNs} employing low-precision, fixed-point computations significantly lower memory demands in applications like image recognition and large language models, with negligible accuracy loss. By integrating quantization into \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU}, our framework further optimizes energy consumption while preserving \ac{DPD} performance. The gradient of the $\texttt{Round}$ function is approximated using the straight-through estimator~\cite{bengio2013estimating} to ensure effective training. 

The quantization process from floating-point data $x$ to fixed-point representation $q$ is computed as: 
$$q = s \times \texttt{Round}\left(\texttt{Clip}\left(\frac{x}{s}, Q_{\texttt{min}}, Q_{\texttt{max}}\right)\right) \label{eq_quant}$$
where $s$ is the quantization scale, and $[Q_{\text{min}}, Q_{\text{max}}]$ are quantization range, the \texttt{Clip} function restricts the signed input to the range from $Q_{\texttt{min}} = -2^{n-1}$ to $Q_{\texttt{max}} = 2^{n-1} - 1$ for $n$-bit quantization. The \texttt{Round} function rounds the result to the nearest integer. During training, the quantization scale $s$ for each neural network layer is optimized through gradient descent and adjusted to the nearest power-of-two, ensuring the fixed-point representation captures the densely distributed input range. 

