Previous \ac{DPD} algorithms typically assume a FP32 bit-width for active parameters when evaluating model complexity. However, the number representation format and bit-width of parameters significantly influence energy consumption during hardware implementation. For \ac{NNs}, the forward pass energy consumption $E_{\text{F}}$ can be expressed as:
\begin{equation}
    E_{\text{F}} = E_{\text{MUL}} + E_{\text{ADD}} + E_{\text{MEM}}
    \label{eq:energy}
\end{equation}
where \( E_{\text{MUL}} \), \( E_{\text{ADD}} \), and \( E_{\text{MEM}} \) are the total energy consumption of all multiplications (\textbf{MUL}), additions (\textbf{ADD}), and memory accesses (\textbf{MEM}) during a forward pass, respectively. When mapping from floating-point to fixed-point parameters with identical bit-width, the energy of arithmetic operations and memory access is reduced due to simpler fixed-point arithmetic hardware. For quantized fixed-point models, we define $\alpha$ as the ratio between lower bit widths and 32 bits ($\alpha = 0.5$ for INT16 vs. INT32). Incorporating both quantization and temporal sparsity percentage $\Gamma$, the operation and memory access energy numbers are given by:
\begin{align}
E_{\text{MUL}} &= (1-\Gamma)\cdot \alpha^2 \cdot E_{\text{MUL,INT32}} \\E_{\text{ADD}} & = (1-\Gamma)\cdot \alpha \cdot E_{\text{ADD,INT32}} \\
E_\text{MEM}&=(1-\Gamma)\cdot \alpha \cdot E_{\text{MEM,INT32}}
\label{eq:delta_gain}
\end{align}

\subsection{Hardware Architecture for Energy Simulation}
\begin{figure}[!t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1.0\linewidth]{figures/gem5_arch.png}
    \caption{Microarchitecture of the \texttt{Gem5}-simulated single-core CPU with the 32-bit ARMv7-A instruction set for DPD inference energy estimation under the 7\,nm technology.}
    \label{fig:gem5_arch}
\end{figure}
To provide accurate energy consumption estimates that reflect real-world Von Neumann architecture-based processors, this work employs the timing-accurate \texttt{Gem5}~\cite{gem5} processor microarchitecture simulator to provide detailed statistics on instruction execution, memory hierarchy behavior, and system-level performance metrics.

\subsubsection{Simulated Microarchitecture}
The simulated processor implements a 32-bit ARM architecture targeting embedded computing scenarios suitable for DPD deployment. 
Fig.~\ref{fig:gem5_arch} illustrates the complete microarchitecture, featuring:
\begin{itemize}
    \item An ARM Timing Simple \textbf{CPU core} using the ARMv7-A \ac{ISA} with Thumb support.
    \item A \textbf{cache hierarchy} consists of separate L1 instruction (I-cache) and data caches (D-cache), each configured as 8KB, 4-way set-associative structures with 64-byte cache lines. We omit L2 and L3 caches to model resource-constrained embedded processors typical in the digital back-end modules in RF systems.
    \item A main DDR4 memory of 1GB connected to the CPU through the memory bus.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Energy Modeling Framework}
The energy consumption analysis leverages detailed operation counts and memory access patterns extracted from \texttt{Gem5} simulations, combined with energy characteristics of 7nm CMOS technology~\cite{jouppi2021ten}.

Energy consumption for arithmetic operations varies across data types. For FP32 operations, addition consumes 0.38\,pJ while multiplication requires 1.31\,pJ per operation. Integer operations exhibit quadratic scaling for multiplication and linear scaling for addition relative to bit-width. Specifically, INT16 operations consume approximately 0.015\,pJ (addition) and 0.37\,pJ (multiplication), while INT12 operations require 0.011\,pJ (addition) and 0.21\,pJ (multiplication).

Memory hierarchy energy consumption scales with both access frequency and data width. L1 cache accesses consume 7.5\,pJ per access, while the DDR4 memory accesses require 1.3\,nJ per transfer. The energy model incorporates data-width scaling factors: FP32 (1.0$\times$), INT16 (0.5$\times$), and INT12 (0.375$\times$) to account for reduced data movement in quantized implementations.

