\subsection{Measurement Setup}
Figure~\ref{fig:platform} depicts the experimental setup utilized in this study. The \ac{OFDM} baseband I/Q signal was generated by an \texttt{R\&S-SMW200A} signal generator and amplified by a \ac{PA} with the same average output power, with and without \ac{DPD}. The resulting output signal was digitized using an \texttt{R\&S-FSW43} spectrum analyzer. During the \ac{ACPR} measurements, the resolution bandwidth is set as 100\,Hz to decrease the noise floor of the spectrum analyzer~\cite{SA_RBW}. The \ac{EVM} was computed by comparing the input signal with the digitized output signal instead of the reference grid.

\subsection{Test Signals and Design-Under-Test (DUT)}
The experiment employed \texttt{APA\_200MHz}, a \texttt{TM3.1a} 5$\times$40-MHz (200-MHz) 256-QAM signal with a \ac{PAPR} of 10.0\,dB and sampling rate of 983.04\,MHz. The dataset, consisting of 98304 samples, was split into 60\% for training, 20\% for validation, and 20\% for testing. Additionally, the experiments were conducted on a 3.5\,GHz GaN Doherty \ac{PA} DUT, operating at an average output power of 41.5\,dBm.

\subsection{Neural Network Training}
The end-to-end training process was implemented using \texttt{OpenDPDv2} platform. Models were trained offline for 240 epochs using the ADAMW optimizer, configured with an initial learning rate of 5E-3, a \texttt{ReduceOnPlateau} decay strategy, and a batch size of 64. In the model comparison experiment, the \ac{DVR} decomposition included 3 units,  the \ac{PG}-\ac{JANET} model utilized a window size of 4. Both \ac{JANET} models had a hidden size of 12, while the \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU} model utilized 15 hidden neurons. For the temporal residual layer, the Conv1D hyperparameters are defined as $(C_{in}, C_{out}, K, D)$, where $C_{in}$ and $C_{out}$ represent the input and output channel sizes, respectively, $K$ is the kernel size, $D$ is the dilation length, and $P$ is the padding size. Non-causal convolution was applied with a padding size of $D$. The hyperparameters for the first dilated convolutional layer were set to $(2, 3, 3, 16)$, and for the channel mixer, they were $(3, 2, 1, 0)$. The stride of the Conv1D layer was consistently set to 1.

All experiments were performed on a single NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPU running PyTorch version 2.4.1 with CUDA version 12.4. During \ac{DPD} learning, models achieving the best \ac{ACPR} were saved for final evaluation.

\subsection{\texttt{Gem5} Simulation Setup}
The simulation begins with cross-compilation of DPD algorithms in \texttt{C} programs targeting the ARMv7-A \ac{ISA}. Program binaries are generated using the \texttt{arm-linux-gnueabihf-gcc} cross-compiler with optimization flags \texttt{-O3 -march=armv7-a -mfpu=neon -static} to maximum compilation optimization and make use of the NEON single-instruction-multiple-data (SIMD) vector processing unit.

During simulation, \texttt{Gem5} loads the target binary into the simulated DDR4 memory and executes the DPD algorithms with an I/Q sequence length of 10,000.


