Table~\ref{tab:combine} compares \ac{ACPR} and \ac{EVM} results for the \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU} model across various temporal sparsity and precision levels, alongside counts of operations and memory access. Notably, the W16A16 \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU}-450 model surpasses the FP32 \ac{TRes}-\ac{GRU}-524 model by 0.5\,dB in \ac{ACPR} and 2\,dB in \ac{EVM}. Here, the 450 and 524 refer to the number of active parameters of the corresponding models. Similarly, the FP32 \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU}-288 with fewer active parameters outperforms the \ac{TRes}-\ac{GRU}-311 model in linearization performance. These results validate the efficacy of DeltaDPD methods in achieving better linearization with reduced energy consumption. Additionally, the W12A12 \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU}-288 \ac{DPD} model meets 3GPP spectrum mask of -45\,dBc and \ac{EVM} standards of -30\,dB with the lowest energy cost, attaining an \ac{ACPR} of -46.9\,dBc and an \ac{EVM} of -31.0\,dB.

\subsection{Delta Threshold Scan}
A Delta threshold scan was conducted on a 15-hidden-neuron \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU} model with varying thresholds $\Theta_{\phi}$ from 0 to 0.04 and $\Theta_{h}$ from 0 to 0.4. Figures~\ref{fig:thhscan} (a) and (b) depict the average \ac{ACPR} and \ac{EVM} of FP32 \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU} models, while (c) illustrates the number of activated parameters. Fig.~\ref{fig:thhscan} (d) reflects the influence of \ac{GRU} temporal sparsity on linearization performance. The \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU}-996 model achieves an average \ac{ACPR} of -59.4\,dBc and an \ac{EVM} of -42.1\,dB, surpassing the DeltaGRU-1067 model by 5.1\,dBc in \ac{ACPR}~\cite{WuIMS2025}. This improvement confirms that the \ac{TCN} residual path enhances \ac{DPD} model performance. Furthermore, the \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU}-450 model with $\Theta_{\phi} = 0.01$ and $\Theta_{h} = 0.05$ achieves an \ac{ACPR} 2.9\,dB higher than the DeltaGRU-573 model with $\Theta_{\phi} = 0.0$ and $\Theta_{h} = 0.05$. These results demonstrate that the \ac{TCN} residual path decouples the \ac{DPD} output sampling rate from $\Delta h$, enabling greater sparsity and improved linearization. At higher thresholds ($\Theta_{\phi} = 0.04$, $\Theta_{h} = 0.4$), the number of activated parameters decreases to 222, with the FP32 \ac{TRes-DeltaGRU}-222 model achieving 80.2\% temporal sparsity, yielding an \ac{ACPR} of -46.7\,dBc and an \ac{EVM} of -35.1\,dB, reflecting a trade-off between sparsity and performance. 

