%At the tail of the spheroid, the curvature of the wall in the longitudinal direction increases. This causes additional 2D separations to occur on both the leeward and windward side. The reversal of the axial velocity is discussed in \cite{jiang2014} at low Re, for $\alpha = 45^\circ$. At small angle of attacks, alternate shedding is observed, while at large angles of attack, the shedding only occurs on the windward side. Both windward and leeward vortices are aligned with the z axis, although both have opposite sign. Thus this tail vortices are orthogonal to the primary and secondary vortices if present. The trail is referred to as the region with $x/L > 1.0$. Note that the designation `trail' is used instead of the more commonly used term `wake' to avoid confusion with the recirculating wake, which occurs for $x/L \leq 1$. In the calculated spheroid flows, the trail is composed of a complex interaction between the primary vortex shedding and additional shedding that occurs at the tail (see \ref{sec: tail shedding}). In the near wake of the spheroid the primary vortices and secondary vortices detach from the wall and are orthogonal to the tail vortices. Further downstream, the two vortices merge. In the near field, a single counter--rotating pair of vortices is observed. In the far field, the circulation in the vortex pair decreases.


