Since $\phi_t({\bf c})= \pi_t ^{-1} (\phi^{\min} (\pi_t({\bf c})))$, we have
\beq{fair1111}
\pr (\phi_t({\bf c})=c) =\pr \Big (\phi^{\min} (\pi_t({\bf c}))=\pi_t(c) \Big).
\eeq
 Since $\phi^{\min}$ is the channel selection function that selects the channel with the smallest index from the available channel set
  and $\pi_t$ is uniformly selected from the $N!$ permutations, it follows from symmetry that  each channel $c$ has equal probability of being the smallest one  in $\pi_t({\bf c})$. Thus,  each channel $c$ is selected with equal probability. This proves the second identity.
