\section{Numerical Results for Resonant Leptogenesis}
\label{sec:NAL}

This part of the study is devoted to a numerical exploration of resonant leptogenesis, focusing on the thermal evolution of key dynamical variables. The upper left plot of Figure~\ref{fig:leptogenesis_results} establishes the required mass degeneracy between $M_{N_1}$ and $M_{N_2}$, with viable parameter space concentrated along the diagonal where $|M_{N_1}-M_{N_2}| \ll M_{N_1}$. This near-degeneracy is essential for the resonant enhancement of CP violation.

The upper right plot of Figure~\ref{fig:leptogenesis_results} shows the maximum CP asymmetry $\varepsilon$ as a function of the mass splitting $\Delta M = M_{N_2}-M_{N_1}$. We observe that the CP asymmetry increases sharply as the $\Delta M$ becomes smaller. However, this plot alone doesn’t indicate whether the enhancement reaches the resonance — which depends on $\Gamma$, the decay width.

The bottom panel (Fig.~\ref{fig:leptogenesis_results}) completes the picture by plotting $\varepsilon$ against the dimensionless ratio $\Delta M/\Gamma$. The clear peak at $\Delta M/\Gamma \approx 1$ (vertical dashed line) demonstrates the resonant condition where:

\begin{equation}
|M_{N_1} - M_{N_2}| \approx \frac{\Gamma_1 + \Gamma_2}{2}
\end{equation}

with $\Gamma_i = (h^\dagger h)_{ii}M_{N_i}/8\pi$. The asymmetry falls off symmetrically when moving away from this optimal condition, showing the characteristic narrow resonance behavior of the self-energy contribution. Together, these panels provide comprehensive evidence that our model achieves the necessary conditions for successful resonant leptogenesis.


The left plot of Figure \ref{fig:YB} presents the temperature evolution of different interaction rates relevant for leptogenesis, plotted against the dimensionless parameter $z=M_N/T$, where $M_N$ is the mass of the heavy right-handed neutrino, and $T$ is the temperature of the early universe. This variable serves as a time-like parameter: smaller values of $z$ correspond to earlier times (high temperatures), while larger $z$ values correspond to later times (cooler universe). This plot is crucial for visualizing whether and when the processes that generate lepton asymmetry occur out of thermal equilibrium, which is a core requirement for successful leptogenesis as per the Sakharov conditions.

The plot includes four curves, each representing a key rate in the dynamics of lepton number generation and washout. The solid blue line represents to the decay rate $\Gamma_D(z)$ of the right handed neutrino into a Standard Model lepton and Higgs boson. This is the leading mechanism responsible for generating a lepton asymmetry. When z is small, the high thermal abundance of heavy neutrinos amplifies the decay rate. However, as the universe expands and cools, $\Gamma_D$ falls off due to Boltzmann suppression.

The green dashed line shows the inverse decay rate $\Gamma_{ID}(z)$, which accounts for processes like $\ell_\alpha + H_u \to N$. During the early stages of the universe, this interaction rate is comparable to or greater than the decay rate, suggesting that the processes remain in equilibrium and are effective in erasing any emerging lepton asymmetry. As the universe cools, $\Gamma_{ID}$ drops more rapidly than the decay rate, eventually becoming negligible.

The red dot-dashed line represents $\Gamma_S(z)$, the rate of lepton-number-violating scattering processes such as $\ell H \leftrightarrow \bar{\ell} H^\dagger$. These scatterings also tend to wash out any generated asymmetry if they are active for too long. Like the decay and inverse decay rates, the scattering rate also decreases rapidly with increasing $z$, becoming subdominant after a certain point.

The black dotted line shows the rate of Hubble expansion $H(z)$, which defines the scale of the universe's expansion at each temperature. In the figure, this condition is satisfied at intermediate values of $z$, typically between $z \sim 10-10^2$, where the decay rate crosses below the Hubble rate. This transition enables the generation of a net lepton asymmetry without it being washed out by inverse processes or scatterings.

Overall, the plot confirms that the essential out-of-equilibrium condition for leptogenesis is satisfied in this model. Initially, all processes are in equilibrium, but as the temperature drops, the decay becomes slower than the expansion, while the inverse decay and scattering rates become suppressed. This timing ensures that a lepton asymmetry generated by the decays can survive and be converted into the observed baryon asymmetry of the universe. The plot also illustrates the role of CP violation through the small but crucial difference between the decay and inverse decay rates. These features together support the validity of the leptogenesis scenario in the considered parameter space.

The right plot of Figure \ref{fig:YB} shows how the number density of heavy neutrinos and the resulting lepton asymmetry evolve as the universe cools. The $x$-axis represents the variable z. The number of particles per unit entropy, or comoving number densities, are displayed on the $y$-axis. This is a standard way to track particle populations in an expanding universe.

The solid black line in the figure depicts the comoving abundance of the heavy neutrino species, denoted \( Y_N \), expressed relative to the entropy density. The dashed black curve shows the absolute deviation from equilibrium, that is $|Y_N - Y_N^{\text{eq}}|$. This curve quantifies how much the actual heavy neutrino abundance deviates from its equilibrium value at each point in time. When this quantity is small, the system is close to equilibrium, when it is large, the neutrinos are decaying out of equilibrium which is a necessary condition for generating lepton asymmetry.

At early times (low \( z \)), the deviation is negligible, indicating that the population of heavy neutrinos closely follows the equilibrium distribution. As the universe cools and $z$ increases, the deviation begins to grow typically around $z \sim 1$ to $z \sim 10$ indicating that the neutrinos begin to fall out of equilibrium and start decaying. This is the critical window during which lepton asymmetry can be generated.

All the coloured curves represent viable benchmark points that successfully generate a final \( B\!-\!L \) asymmetry above the observed baryon to photon ratio ($Y_B\simeq 8.6\times10^{-11}$) of the universe, shown by the horizontal red dashed line. While each curve reaches this threshold, the trajectories they follow exhibit subtle but important differences, reflecting the rich interplay between CP-violating decay dynamics and washout processes in the primordial universe. In the high-temperature regime, the system remains close to thermal equilibrium, preventing any net lepton asymmetry from forming. As the universe cools, the decays of the heavy neutrinos gradually depart from equilibrium, initiating the generation of a nonzero lepton asymmetry. Some curves rise gradually, indicating a longer duration of asymmetry generation with relatively weaker washout, while others grow more steeply, suggesting rapid production followed by early freeze-out. This freeze-out behavior ensures that the generated asymmetry remains protected from further dilution. Together, these results demonstrate that within the allowed parameter space, the model can robustly achieve successful thermal leptogenesis, with all selected benchmark points yielding a sufficient baryon asymmetry compatible with observational data. The numerical analysis presented in the figures utilizes consistent benchmark parameters to study leptogenesis in the $B\!-\!L$ symmetric framework. The physical constants include the Planck mass ($1.22 \times 10^{19}$~GeV), $106.75$ relativistic degrees of freedom, and a fixed $Z'$ boson mass of $4$~TeV.  We consider three representative parameter sets (benchmark points), all characterized by a common mass of \( 10^8~\text{GeV} \) for the lightest heavy neutrino. In each case, the second neutrino exhibits a relative mass splitting of approximately \( 1\% \)
and a scalar vacuum expectation value ($v_{NS}$) of $2.35 \times 10^8$~GeV, while varying the CP asymmetry parameter $\varepsilon$ from $2 \times 10^{-6}$ to $1 \times 10^{-7}$. The $B\!-\!L$ gauge coupling $g_{B-L}$ was fixed at $0.023$ for the rate comparison and $\varepsilon$ variation studies, then varied from $0.25$ to $0.0002$ for the coupling strength analysis.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:C}
In summary, our modular \( S_4 \)-based inverse seesaw model with a (2,3) structure provides a highly predictive framework for neutrino masses and mixing. Through detailed numerical analysis, we demonstrate that the model successfully reproduces the observed neutrino oscillation parameters within the \( 3\sigma \) experimental ranges. The predicted values for mixing angles, CP-violating phases and the absolute neutrino mass scale are tightly constrained. In particular, the Dirac CP phase is sharply localized around \( \delta_{\rm CP} \sim 359^\circ \), and the correlation between \( \delta_{\rm CP} \) and \( \sin^2\theta_{23} \) shows excellent agreement with NuFIT global data, highlighting the model’s testability in current and future experiments.

Beyond neutrino oscillation observables, the model offers testable predictions for low-energy phenomena. The effective Majorana mass \( |m_{ee}| \) falls below current experimental bounds but lies within the sensitivity reach of upcoming \( 0\nu\beta\beta \) decay experiments, offering a promising avenue for future verification. Moreover, the model's predictions for the rates of charged lepton flavor violating processes, such as \( \mu \rightarrow e\gamma \), lie well below current experimental bounds, thereby maintaining consistency with existing LFV constraints.


We also investigated the viability of resonant leptogenesis in this framework. The small splitting between the masses of the nearly degenerate heavy neutrinos fulfills the condition necessary for resonant enhancement of CP violation. Resonant enhancement of the CP asymmetry occurs as \( \Delta M \) approaches the scale of the decay width, with a peak observed around \( \Delta M/\Gamma \approx 1 \). The thermal evolution of the relevant interaction rates and the resulting \( B-L \) asymmetry show that this framework is successfully generate the observed matter-antimatter imbalance of the universe across a viable region of parameter space.

In summary, this modular \( S_4 \)-based ISS(2,3) framework offers a unified and testable explanation for neutrino masses, CP violation, rare lepton decays and the baryon asymmetry of the universe. Future experimental data from LFV searches and \( 0\nu\beta\beta \) decay experiments decay experiments, as well as more precise neutrino oscillation measurements, will be critical in further testing and constraining this model.


