\section{Introduction}
The environmental impact of synthetic materials like polyethylene (PE) in packaging waste has driven significant research into biopolymers \cite{Marsh2007}. Thermoplastic starch (TPS) could be used as an alternative due to its biodegradability \cite{babu2013current}, Low cost, wide availability, and excellent film-forming properties (good transparency and low processing temperature) \cite{jimenez2012edible,attaran2017materials}. TPS formulation consisting of linear amylose (1-4 linked $\alpha$-D glucose), amylopectin (amylose with additional 1-6 linked branches), and plasticizers (sorbitol, water, etc.) \cite{carvalho2008starch}, proven suitable option for food packaging and paper additive materials \cite{khan2017thermoplastic, stepto2003processing, de2022thermoplastic,kaboorani2021tailoring, correa2022biodegradability}. However, its poor mechanical properties (low Young's modulus and tensile strength, limited elongation at break) \cite{Cyras2008,chocyk2015influence,domene2019influence} and low moisture resistance \cite{salaberria2015role} necessitate the addition of components with complementary properties, such as addition of nanofillers \cite{Rezaei2015, zhou2015starch, Suter2015}, blending with other polymers like PE \cite{altayan2022toward,baumberger1998use}. The optimal formulation, consisting of PE and TPS, has demonstrated good film-forming properties with the additional advantage of biodegradability \cite{jimenez2012edible,attaran2017materials}. However, different polarities of TPS and PE lead to phase separation \cite{Wang2015, Mao2012}, which yields a material with poor mechanical properties \cite{altayan2022toward}. The compatibilization of polymer blends can be achieved by using nanoparticles \cite{Mao2012}, block copolymers \cite{Eastwood2002}, and Nano clay \cite{Huang2004, Rezaei2015}. The high surface-to-volume ratio, low cost, and easily achievable surface tuning in phyllosilicate clays have made it an attractive option for compatibilization purposes in packaging applications \cite{Rezaei2015, zhou2015starch, Suter2015}. Apart from the compatibilization, polymer nanocomposite also shows better mechanical, thermal, biodegradation, and barrier properties \cite{Krishnamoorti1996, Cyras2008, Huang2004, Bher2019, Dutta2017, Rezaei2015}. For example, the addition of \SI{5}{\percent} Cloisite-15A clay (a surface-modified clay) in the TPS-PE with PE-grafted maleic anhydride blend decreased the diameter TPS domains from \SI{500}{\nano\meter} to \SI{200}{\nano\meter}) and this uniformity in morphology has improved the tensile strength by \SI{35}{\percent} \cite{Sabetzadeh2016a,sharif2011study}.  %Several studies have shown improvements in the mechanical, thermal, and barrier properties of TPS films when organically modified montmorillonite (prepared by substituting surface ions of montmorillonite (MMT) with long organic chains) is added \cite{park2002preparation,zhou2015starch, schlemmer2010morphological,Cyras2008, majdzadeh2010optimization,rivadeneira2021green,Huang2004}. 
However, selecting the appropriate phyllosilicate for a specific formulation could be a time-consuming and cost-consuming exercise, and require optimization over a large parameter space, such as chemical composition, formulation of the base component, chemical structure of interacting polymer, the type of surface, etc. The compatibilization over multi-component systems with high chemical heterogeneity (branching in amylopectin and the presence of small molecules like sorbitol or water) further increases challenges in material design.\\
The MD simulations provide the structure-property relationship of multicomponent systems, which complement the material design by providing screening over a large parameter space \cite{huang2022molecular, guo2023molecular, lin2023multiscale, gartner2019modeling}. MD simulation at all-atom (AA) scale has shown their application in accurate prediction of Young's modulus and glass transition temperature of TPS \cite{patidar2024martini, Ozeren2020}, the radius of gyration and density of PE \cite{panizon2015martini}, and mechanical properties of tetramethylammonium-montmorillonite (TMA--MMT) clay \cite{Khan2019}. However, the small time-step (\SI{1}{\femto\second}) of the AA simulation, the polymer's large molecule weight with high relaxation time, and the large diameter of clay ($\sim$\SI{200}{\nano\meter}) make it computationally expensive or inaccessible in majority of the systems. For example, AA simulation of TPS-TMA-MMT consisting of smaller chains ( $R_\mathrm{g}$$\sim$\SI{2}{\nano\meter}) and MMT sheet ($\SI{10.32}{\nano\meter} \times \SI{10.71}{\nano\meter} \times \SI{1.2}{\nano\meter}$) took $10^9$ steps to extract some meaningful properties of polymer near the surface \cite{patidar2024martini}. Phenomena such as phase separation, occurring at time scales ranging from $10^1$\SI{}{\second}-$10^3$\SI{}{\second}, play a crucial role in material design but are currently inaccessible through AA simulation.\\
The coarse-grain (CG) approaches represent groups of atoms as a single bead, significantly reducing the computational cost while capturing the essential properties relevant to the phenomenon under study. Some mesoscale methods like dissipative particle dynamics (DPD) simulations \cite{Groot1997,Singh2018,Fu2013,Scocchi2007,long2006nonlinear,sliozberg2020dissipative,khani2015polymer,wang2021dissipative,ju2013miscibility},   
 field-based theories \cite{schweizer1992reference, schweizer1997polymer, zirkel2002small, wu2006density,oxtoby2002density,helfand1975theory,helfand1976block,lowden1973solution} and integral-equation theories \cite{sung2005integral, hall2011impact,martin2016using, hsu1979rism,martin2018pyprism} have been used extensively to study morphological, structural and thermodynamics properties of extensive large systems ($10^3$\SI{}{\nano\meter}). These methods used soft-core potential between larger chain units ($\sim$Kuhn length) and their values obtained from experiments or AA simulations. However, a small chemical modification at the monomer level can completely alter the material morphology \cite{zhou2015starch}, and these changes can not be directly captured in these methods. Also, the applicability of their parameters in certain temperature and pressure ranges, and increased difficulty in their estimation for complex formulation make them inefficient models for the present system.\\
 Chemical-specific CG models model (bead size $\sim$\SI{}{\nano\meter}) like iterative Boltzmann inversion (IBI) \cite{reith2001mapping,reith2003deriving}, force matching \cite{izvekov2005multiscale} and MARTINI CG force field \cite{Marrink2007} combine only 4-10 atoms in one bead and therefore provide better chemical representation with at least 100 times speed up as compared to AA simulation. However, IBI and force matching methods do not provide chemical transferability and even temperature transferability obtained by optimizing the parameters using data from multiple state points \cite{moore2014derivation}. The MARTINI-FF, based on partitioning energy between polar and apolar solvents, provided good transferability across diverse state-points \cite{Marrink2007}, and proven application in estimating correct interfacial properties of solvents \cite{ndao2015coarse}, making it a suitable choice for the present system. The MARTINI-2 force field parameters for PE \cite{panizon2015martini}, TPS \cite{Lopez2009,patidar2024martini}, and phyllosilicate nanocomposites \cite{Khan2019} are well-established. Their combined implementation in the PE-TMA-MMT system successfully reproduced all-atom chain properties and PE structural density profiles near TMA–MMT clay across temperatures, confirming strong parameter transferability across chemical environments and state points \cite{Khan2019}. %For example, the MARTINI CG parameters for polypropylene, based on partition energy, closely matched the all-atom (AA) radius of gyration ($R_\mathrm{g}$) across various polarity solvent \cite{panizon2015martini} and successfully replicated AA chain properties and PE density profiles around TMA–MMT clay at different temperatures \cite{Khan2019}. 
 However, some rescaling in cross-interaction was required to avoid freezing near the surface \cite{wu2012coarse,gobbo2013martini,piskorz2019nucleation, patidar2024martini}. The MARTINI-2 FF parameters of other TPS components like sorbitol \cite{sukenik2015osmolyte} and polysaccharides \cite{Schmalhorst2017,shivgan2020extending} also overestimate the solute-solvent interactions, making their results doubtful for highly complex polymer nanocomposite systems. The recently introduced MARTINI-3 FF parameters eliminated these limitations, making it an ideal forcefield to study highly heterogeneous systems in different operating conditions \cite{souza2021martini}. Additionally, several carbohydrate models including linear 1--4 $\alpha$-D glucose and branched 1--6 $\alpha$-D glucose are available in the literature \cite{lutsyk2022extending, grunewald2022polyply}. However, to the best of our knowledge, the MARTINI-3 model of pyrophyllite or any surface-modified MMT is not available in the literature.\\   
Given the wide interest in phyllosilicate-based composites, we have developed MARTINI-3 CG FF parameters for pyrophyllite and TMA-MMT clay using a combination of experimental and MD simulation data. The hydration energies obtained from experimental surface tension data were used for initial surface bead assignments of pyrophyllite, which were fine-tuned based on optimal reproduction of AA structural (radius of gyration), thermodynamic (two-body excess entropy, density, conformational entropy), and dynamic properties (diffusion coefficient) of TPS near pyrophyllite clay. The same set of AA properties in TPS--[tetramethylammonium (TMA)]-MMT composite was used to determine the optimal bead assignments for the TMA-MMT surface. Additionally, this study reported some key observations, including the dependency of polymer properties on the nature of the polymer-clay interaction.\\  
Further, the developed CG model was used to investigate the effect of Cloisite-15A compatibilizers on the morphological and interfacial behavior of a large TPS-PE system. The physical insights obtained by these CG simulations provided a quantitative agreement with some experimental studies \cite{Sabetzadeh2016,sharif2011study}, proving the robustness of models in material design.

\section{Methods}
\subsection{System Details}
