\subsubsection{AA System and Simulation Details}
AA structures and force field parameters of amylose (linear chain with eighteen $\alpha$-D glucose monomers), amylopectin (a primary chain comprising twenty-seven $\alpha$-D glucose monomers and a secondary branch composed of nine monomers), and sorbitol were obtained from CHARMM-GUI \cite{jo2008charmm, guvench7ii, guvench2009charmm}. The INTERFACE force field was employed to model pyrophyllite and TMA-MMT \cite{heinz2005force}, alongside the TIP3P water model. Packmol \cite{martinez2009packmol} was used to obtain a low-density TPS system ($\rho = \SI{117.62}{\kilo\gram\per\cubic\meter}$), which underwent multiple box rescaling and temperature-pressure ($T$-$P$) annealing cycles to obtain a high-density (\SI{1227.62}{\kilo\gram\per\cubic\meter}) equilibrated system (more details are provided in section \ref{annealing}). The TPS composite systems consist of pyrophyllite (TPS-PY) and TMA-MMT (TPS--TMA-MMT) sheets were obtained by sandwiching respective periodic sheets ($\SI{10.32}{\nano\meter} \times \SI{10.71}{\nano\meter} \times \SI{1.2}{\nano\meter}$ connected across $x$-$y$ periodic boundaries) between two well equilibrated TPS melt boxes (an equilibrated morphology TPS--TMA-MMT system shown in Figure \ref{fig:AACGcomposite} (a)).\\
AA MD simulations were performed using GROMACS 2024.2 \cite{abraham2015gromacs}, with a time step of \SI{1}{\femto\second}, V-rescale thermostat ($\tau$=\SI{1}{\pico\second}), and either the Berendsen barostat ($\tau$=\SI{2}{\pico\second}, first \SI{100}{\nano\second}) or the Parrinello-Rahman barostat ($\tau$=\SI{5}{\pico\second}).
% to ensure consistency in simulation accuracy and compatibility.
%CG parameters
\subsubsection{CG System and Simulation Details}
The MARTINI CG parameters for amylose and amylopectin were taken from the polysaccharide CG model based on AA simulations of mono- and disaccharides in polar and apolar solvents \cite{lutsyk2022extending}. Accordingly, we used the following bead assignments: (tiny) TP3 bead for hydroxymethyl, (small) SN4 for the ring oxygen, anomeric carbon, and the reduced-end anomeric hydroxyl, P3 for diol, and SN4 for the hydroxyethyl groups (see Figure \ref{fig:mapping} for mapping scheme). 
      \begin{figure}[!htb]
     \centering
     \includegraphics[width=6.35cm,height=8.20cm]{mappingMARTINI3.png}
     \caption{ \textbf{CG MARTINI mapping scheme for TPS and TMA-MMT clay.} \textbf{(a)} trimeric segment of $\alpha$-D-glucose units connected with 1--4 linkage (representing a small unit of linear amylose chain). \textbf{(b)} 1--4 linked trimeric segment of $\alpha$-D-glucose with an additional 1--6 connectivity (represent branching in amylopectin). \textbf{(c)} sorbitol. \textbf{(d)} A TMA-MMT unit cell illustrating one octahedral $Al$-for-$Mg$ substitution, resulting in $-1 \, e$ charge balanced by $+1 \, e$ TMA ion. Pyrophyllite clay contains no substitutions or counter ions. Red, white, cyan, yellow, pink, and green represent oxygen, hydrogen, carbon, silicon, aluminum, and magnesium atoms, respectively. Atoms within shaded circles are represented by a single CG bead, with the bead number (black) and type (red) labeled.}
     \label{fig:mapping}
 \end{figure}
The sorbitol was modeled using three P3-type beads, each representing a diol group (Figure \ref{fig:mapping} (c)). The center of geometry (COG) positions of these diol groups were used to estimate bond and angle parameters of sorbitol (see Figure \ref{fig:bondsorb} for distribution). The CG structure and parameters of PE were generated from Polyply software \cite{grunewald2022polyply}. The CG bonded parameters for the clays were taken from Khan \cite{Khan2019}, who showed a good agreement between AA and CG simulation results for Young's modulus and bending stiffness for these clays. The octahedral $\ce{AlOH_2}$ groups in both pyrophyllite and TMA-MMT clays were represented using SN4a-type CG beads due to their structural similarities with acetal groups. However, the isomorphic substitution of $Al^{3+}$ with $Mg^{2+}$ in the octahedral layer of TMA-MMT generates a net negative charge, which is compensated by $+1 \,e$ charged TMA counterions. These $\ce{MgOH_2}$ and TMA ions were represented by the charged SQ5 and Q2 beads, respectively. Clay materials exhibit distinct cleavage patterns, forming edges such as 110, 010, 101, and 130. We modeled the clay edges using the most stable 010 and 100 configurations, which feature surface $\mathrm{SiOH}$ groups and inner $Al$ sites with one or two hydroxyl groups, respectively. The $Al$ sites on the 010 edge were assigned bead type P2 (hemiacetal-like), and those on the 100 edge were assigned P4 (hydroxyl-like), while surface $\mathrm{SiOH}$ groups on both edges were represented using P2 bead type. The $\mathrm{MgOH_2}$ groups were consistently mapped to SQ5, regardless of their position (edge or inner). These assignments maintained an accurate representation of functional group chemistry according to the MARTINI-3 framework, while preserving relative polarity differences between edge types \cite{souza2021martini}. For instance, 010 edge has relatively lower sorption energy (-\SI{38.9}{\kilo\joule\per\mol}) compared to 100 edge (-\SI{57.7}{\kilo\joule\per\mol}) \cite{churakov2006ab}, and therefore represented by relatively lower-polarity bead combinations (P2 and P4). The CG bead-type for surface $\mathrm{SIO_2}$ of pyrophyllite was selected based on hydration energies, which were calculated from experimental contact angle values of polar and apolar liquids on the pyrophyllite surface \cite{giese1991surface}. This MARTINI model of pyrophyllite was tested by comparing the structural arrangement and dynamic behavior of hexadecane, propanol, ethanol, isopropanol, and sorbitol near the surface to AA simulation results. For relative comparison, CG simulations using some other surface bead assignments (SC4, SN2, SN4, SN6, and SP2) were also performed for each solvent-sheet system. Further, TPS-pyrophyllite (TPS-PY) nanocomposite simulations were performed to fine-tune these surface bead assignments using AA structural, thermodynamic, and dynamic properties. Similarly, we used AA properties from TPS--TMA-MMT composite simulations to obtain an optimal bead assignment of surface $\mathrm{SIO_2}$ for TMA-MMT. We observed that the substitution of octahedral $Al$ with $Mg$ in TMA-MMT increased the surface polarity as compared to pyrophyllite, which necessitates the use of an additional surface bead-type to account for this local polarity. Therefore, one-third of surface $\mathrm{SIO_2}$ coordinating with octahedral $Mg$ through bridging oxygen are represented using high-polarity CG bead (see Figure \ref{fig:mapping}). More details on bead assignment are provided in section \ref{TMA-MMT non-bonded parameters}.\\
%system preparation
The CG systems of TPS melt, TPS-PY, and TPS-TMA-MMT were directly prepared by placing CG beads at the center of mass positions (using Equation \ref{eq:mapping}) of constituent atoms (mapping scheme in Figure \ref{fig:mapping}) from well-equilibrated AA structures. A 50-50 wt\SI{}{\percent} phase-separated TPS-PE melt CG system was prepared in Packmol using long polymer chains exceeding entanglement lengths: amylose (300-mer), amylopectin (1,300-mer), polyethylene (3,000-mer), and sorbitol (see Table \ref{tab:system details} for details). This system contains a highly branched amylopectin structure where linear chains of sixteen and thirty-six $\alpha$-D-glucose monomers are grafted to the main chain at ten- and twenty-monomer intervals, respectively. The TPS composition in this system mirrored the TPS melt system (see Table \ref{tab:system details}), except that the water fraction was replaced with sorbitol. A TPS-PE-Cl (5 phr) composite system was prepared by adding Cloisite-15A clay tactoids ($x \times y$=\SI{10.32}{\nano\meter}$\times$\SI{10.71}{\nano\meter}) to this TPS-PE system, with two tactoids positioned at the PE-TPS interface and two within the PE bulk to ensure clay dispersion across both phases. The Cloisite-15A clay was prepared by grafting C16 alkyl chains (represented by three C1 and one Q2 type CG bead) onto the MMT surface, with its equilibrated structure then used to form a two-clay tactoid assembly (Figure \ref{fig:grafing}). These systems represent only a small fraction of the TPS-PE interface formed between TPS droplets and the surrounding PE matrix, as observed in experimental studies \cite{sharif2011study, sabetzadeh2016effect}. The TPS-PE melt and TPS-PE-CL composite systems obtained from Packmol underwent rescaling and temperature-pressure (T-P) annealing cycles to obtain well-packed systems of $\SI{24.11}{\nano\meter} \times \SI{24.59}{\nano\meter} \times \SI{53.12}{\nano\meter}$ and $\SI{24.82}{\nano\meter} \times \SI{24.58}{\nano\meter} \times \SI{57.15}{\nano\meter}$, respectively (equilibrated TPS-PE-CL composite system is shown in Figure \ref{fig:initialTPSPECL}). The system annealed between \SI{613}{\kelvin} and \SI{913}{\kelvin} with steps of \SI{50}{\kelvin}, at each step system was energy minimized followed by \SI{5}{\nano\second} NPT simulation for local equilibration, more details about the annealing protocols discussed in section \ref{annealing}.\\
In all CG simulations, the system was coupled to the V-rescale thermostat ($\tau$ = \SI{1}{\pico\second}) using a time step of \SI{5}{\femto\second}. The melt, TPS-TMA-MMT and TPS-PY-MMT composite system were coupled using Berendsen barostat ($\tau$ = \SI{8}{\pico\second}, first \SI{100}{\nano\second}) or the Parrinello-Rahman barostat ($\tau$ = \SI{40}{\pico\second}). However, for accurate estimation of interfacial properties, we used the semi-isotropic ($NP_zT$) pressure coupling using Berendsen barostat for TPS-PE melt and TPS-PE-CL composite systems. The CG potential is given as per Equation \ref{eq:LJ}.
\begin{multline}\label{eq:LJ}
U=4\epsilon_{ij}\left[\left(\frac{\sigma_{ij}}{r_{ij}}\right)^{12}-\left(\frac{\sigma_{ij}}{r_{ij}}\right)^6\right]+\frac{q_{i}q_{j}}{4{\pi}\epsilon_0\epsilon_{rel}{r_{ij}}}\\
+\frac{1}{2}K_l(l_{ij}-l_0)^2+\frac{1}{2}K_\theta(cos\theta_{ijk}-cos\theta_0)^2\\+K_\phi[1+(cos\phi_{ijkl}-cos\phi_0)]
\end{multline}
where $\sigma_{ij}$, $\epsilon_{ij}$, $q$, and $\epsilon_{rel}$ are bead diameter, interaction strength, bead charge, and relative dielectric constant, respectively. The force constant for bond ($l_{ij}$), angle ($\theta_{ijk}$), and torsion ($\phi_{ijkl}$) potentials are $K_l$, $K_\theta$, and $K_\phi$, respectively. The $\sigma_{ij}$ values can change from 0.34 to 0.62 depending on the size of interacting beads, which could be regular, small, or tiny. The choice of $\epsilon_{ij}$ is linked to the functional group(s) in the CG bead, with primary categorization defined as polar (P), intermediate/nonpolar (N), apolar (C), halo-compounds (X), monovalent ions (Q), divalent ions (D) or water (W). Except for W and D beads, all beads have subtypes characterized by an integer value ranging from 0 to 6, which indicates their interaction strength.

\subsection{Property Calculation}
In the TPS--TMA-MMT and TPS--PY-MMT composite systems, the near- and far-sheet regions were classified based on density profiles of polymer and sorbitol along the normal ($z$-axis) to the surface (marked by the dashed red vertical line in Figure \ref{fig:de}). The center of mass (COM) distance of the polymer's trimetric segment ($\sim$ persistence length (\SI{0.86}{\nano\meter}) of amylose) from the sheet was used to classify it as a near or a far segment, with a chain labeled as a near/far-region chain if $>\SI{70}{\percent}$ segments were in the near/far region. All properties of the melts and composites were evaluated at \SI{613}{\kelvin}, while the solvent properties were analyzed at \SI{300}{\kelvin}.
\subsubsection{Structural and Dynamic Properties}
We used in-built GROMACS utilities to calculate the density profiles ($\rho_n$), radius of gyration (${R_g}$), and radial distribution function (RDF) of melt and composite systems. The density profile integrals $Ar$ and $Ln$ were calculated using Equation \ref{eq:Areq} and \ref{eq:Lneq}, respectively.
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Areq}
Ar = \int_{0}^{z} \rho(z)dz
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Lneq}
Ln = \frac{1}{Ar}\int_{0}^{z} \rho(z)zdz
\end{equation}
COG of atoms making the CG beads were used for RDF calculation in the AA simulations to allow comparison with the CG simulations. For the composite system, a 2D RDF was calculated in \SI{0.37}{\nano\meter} slices parallel to the TMA-MMT sheet in the $x-y$ plane. The data is reported as average over four slices in the near region and five slices in the far region. The morphological evolution of TPS-PE melt and TPS-PE-Cl composite systems was evaluated using coordination number (CN) - time graphs. The CN of all possible pairs among TPS, PE, and Cl was calculated using Equation \ref{eqn:CN}, 
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{CN} = \rho_\beta \int_{0}^{r_{cut}} 2 \pi r^2 g_{\alpha\beta}(r) dr
    %\includegraphics{graphic}
  \label{eqn:CN}
\end{equation}
where, $g_{\alpha, \beta} (r)$ is RDF of these individual pairs, $r_{cut}$ in cut-off distance, and $\rho_\beta$ is number density of $\beta$ component. 
The $r_{cut}$ was chosen to be \SI{1.1}{\nano\meter}, a value that corresponds to the second minima distance observed in RDF (Figure \ref{fig:RDFTPSPECL}). The exclusions of up to five, three, and nine CG-bonded neighbors were used for the TPS, PE, and CL, respectively. We used small \SI{10}{\nano\second} trajectory (at \SI{613}{\kelvin}) portion from each annealing cycle, full \SI{25}{\nano\second} $NVT$ trajectory, and \SI{50}{\nano\second} blocks over full \SI{1500}{\nano\second} simulation for CN calculations. Diffusion coefficients of amylose and amylopectin trimetric segments were calculated from mean-square-displacement (${MSD} (t)$) profiles using Einstein’s Equation \ref{eq:MSD}), with normalized diffusion coefficients ($D_\mathrm{n}$) calculated as the lateral diffusion ratio (in the $x-y$ plane) relative to the melt ($D_\mathrm{n}=D_\mathrm{composite}/D_\mathrm{melt}$). 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:MSD}
\mathrm{MSD (t)} = \mathrm{6 \cdot D \cdot t}
\end{equation}
This normalization allowed the direct comparison of $D_\mathrm{n}$ values obtained from AA and CG simulations. 

\subsubsection{Thermodynamic Properties}
The pairwise ($S_{2,\alpha\beta}$) and total ($S_\mathrm{2}$) two-body excess entropies were calculated using the corresponding RDFs ($g_{\alpha,\beta}$) as per Equations \ref{eqn:tbe} and \ref{eq:overalltbe} (integral calculated up to \SI{2.5}{\nano\meter}), respectively \cite{sharma2008estimating},
\begin{multline}
   \mathrm{S_{2,\alpha\beta}}=-2\pi\rho\int_{0}^{\infty}g_{\alpha\beta}(r)\mathrm{ln}\,g_{\alpha\beta} (r)-\\
   [g_{\alpha\beta}(r)-1]r^2dr
    %\includegraphics{graphic}
  \label{eqn:tbe}
\end{multline}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:overalltbe}
\mathrm{S_{2}}=\sum_{\alpha,\beta}{x_\alpha x_\beta}\mathrm{S_{\alpha,\beta}},
\end{equation}
, where, $x_\alpha$ and $x_\beta$ are the mole fractions of the $\alpha$ and $\beta$ components, respectively. The $S_{2,\alpha\beta}$ of composite systems were estimated using Equation \ref{eqn:tbecomp}. The configurational entropies ($S_\mathrm{c}$) of amylose and amylopectin chains were calculated from the covariance matrix of each chain (after removing rigid-body translational and rotational motion) using Schlitter's equation \cite{schlitter1993estimation}, implemented with GROMACS utility anaeig.
\subsubsection{Interfacial Tension Calculations}
The Interfacial tensions of the TPS-PE melt and TPS-PE-CL composite were calculated using the method of Irving and Kirkwood (IK) (Equation \ref{eqn:IK}), \cite{kirkwood1949statistical},
\begin{equation}
       \mathrm{\gamma}=\int_{-L_(z/2)}^{L_(z/2)}(P_{N}(z)-P_{T}(z))dz=\frac{1}{2}\langle P_N - P_T \rangle L_z
    %\includegraphics{graphic}
  \label{eqn:IK}
\end{equation}
where $P_N(z)$=$P_{zz}(z)$ and $P_T(z)$=$\frac{1}{2}$($P_{xx}(z)$+$P_{yy}(z)$) are normal and tangential pressure tensor components of the simulation box (length=$L_z$), respectively. The $\gamma$ values were calculated in blocks of \SI{100}{\nano\second} over a trajectory length of \SI{1500}{\nano\second}, and errors were reported for converged trajectories. 
\section{Results and Discussion}
\subsection{Physical Insights from AA Simulation of Composites}\label{AA results pyrophyllite and MMT composite}
We observed that the water and sorbitol density peaks near the surface became sharper when the surface was switched from a lower polarity PY surface to a higher polarity TMA-MMT surface (Figure \ref{fig:de}), resulting in a more pronounced depletion of polymer near the surface. The increased polarity can be attributed to the presence of charged TMA ions, which facilitate the accumulation of polar TPS constituents such as sorbitol and water. This finding aligns with experiments indicating that higher water content in the TPS–Cloisite (a bentonite nanoclay) composites promotes TPS intercalation and clay swelling \cite{park2002preparation}. Although the polymer seems depleted near the TMA-MMT surface, it shows a sharp increase ($\sim\SI{50}{\percent}$ of the bulk value) in density within the first solvation layer. The presence of the hydroxyl group in amylose, amylopectin, and sorbitol might be the key reason for their mixing in the near-surface region. All component densities become identical to the bulk value at an average $z$-distance of \SI{2.5}{\nano\meter} from the sheet, indicating minimal clay's effect on the TPS beyond this distance. Therefore, this plane (shown by red line in Figure \ref{fig:de}) was used to differentiate near and far clay regions for subsequent calculation of all reported properties. The polymer-surface interactions are directly linked to polymer dynamics. For example, the AA simulation of polyethylene oxide with Na-MMT (favorable interaction) indicated a slowdown in polymer dynamics \cite{suter2009computer}, and the opposite was observed upon the addition of repulsive nanoparticles to an LJ polymer \cite{smith2002molecular, varnik2002reduction}. The $D_\mathrm{n}$ of amylose and amylopectin trimeric segments in the vicinity of pyrophyllite clay were 1.30 and 1.60, respectively (Table \ref{tab:pyrophylliteRgScDn}). However, these respective $D_\mathrm{n}$ were decreased to 0.96 and 1.16 in the near regions of TMA-MMT (Table \ref{tab:TMSMMTRgScDn}), indicating increased favorable interactions within the TMA-MMT system. These results strongly align with experimental findings in which favorable TPS-MMT interactions promote effective exfoliation and enhance key material properties, including Young’s modulus, glass transition temperature, and moisture resistance \cite{muller2012composites, Huang2004, schlemmer2010morphological}. Additionally, a high water contact angle (\SI{100}{\degree}) over the pyrophyllite surface indicated its nonpolar nature  \cite{giese1991surface}, making it a repulsive surface for polar formulation like TPS (as observed in this study).  
%In contrast to this observation, studies on polyethylene oxide-MMT \cite{suter2009computer} and polyethylene-graphite \cite{daoulas2005detailed} systems have shown that favorable interactions between the polymer and the surface can lead to decreased diffusion of polymer chains near the surface. This is further supported by AA simulations of polyethylene, polypropylene, and polystyrene near TMA-MMT surfaces, which showed reductions in diffusion coefficients by factors of 0.79, 0.73, and 0.34, respectively \cite{Khan2019}. A similar downtrend in binding energies of polymeric chains (polyacrylic acid,  polyethylene glycol, and polyacrylamide) on $\beta$-dicalcium silicate surface was reported ($\sim$\SI{65}{\percent}) upon the addition of water  \cite{tong2018molecular}.\\

Further, the near-to-melt $S_\mathrm{c}$ ratios for amylose (1.03) and amylopectin (1.04) in the TPS-PY system suggested neutral or repulsive polymer-sheet interactions, which decreased to 0.99 and 0.92 respectively in the TPS–TMA–MMT system, demonstrating enhanced interfacial adhesion in the latter composite. However, the reduction in $S_\mathrm{c}$ from the melt state to the TPS–TMA–MMT system remains modest, suggesting weakly attractive polymer-sheet interactions. A similar decrease ($\sim\SI{10}{\percent}$) in $S_\mathrm{c}$ was reported for polyethylene, polypropylene, and polystyrene near TMA-MMT, a case of weakly attractive interaction \cite{Khan2019}. We observed similar $R_\mathrm{g}$ for both polymers (amylose and amylopectin) in the near regions of the PY (1.34, 1.72) and TMA--MMT (1.32, 1.72), and both were slightly lower than melt values (1.38, 1.83). These findings contrast with previous reports, where attractive interactions in polyethylene oxide/Na-MMT systems led to a twofold increase in $R_\mathrm{g}$ \cite{suter2009computer}, while repulsive surfaces resulted in negligible changes in the $R_\mathrm{g}$ of LJ polymers \cite{smith2002molecular}. However, the strong solvent-surface affinity and interfacial polymer–plasticizer mixing (as evident in the density profiles) likely drive this deviation from conventional attractive/repulsive surface behavior.\\
The properties obtained from these AA simulations were used to complement the development of clay surface CG parameters obtained via Lifshitz theory over experimental contact angle data. The inclusion of multiple properties is expected to improve the model's accuracy in capturing the free energy of surface adsorption. For example, adding miscibility trend data along with partition-free energies improved balance between solute-solvent interaction in recently released MARTINI-3 FF \cite{souza2021martini,Marrink2007}.%, and state point transferability of IBI was improved by incorporating multiple state-point data \cite{moore2014derivation} or density correction \cite{hu2019developing}.

\subsection{Development of CG Parameters}
%We have used Lifshitz theory over experimental surface tension data to derive the MARTINI-3 CG parameter of PY clay surface beads, and these parameters were further optimized to reproduce AA structural, thermal, and dynamic properties of the TPS around periodic PY clay. The CG bead type for 010 and 100 edges were taken as per standard MARTINI-3 FF assignments and DFT-derived hydration energies were checked to ensure the correct relative edge polarity trend. The surface beads of MMT were also optimized to reproduce AA structural, thermal, and dynamic properties of TPS around periodic TMA-MMT clay. All these derived parameters were then used over the phase-separated systems of PE-TPS and PE-TPS-CL systems to investigate the time evolution of CN, $R_\mathrm{g}$, $S_\mathrm{c}$, and $\gamma$. 
\subsubsection{CG Parameters TPS Melt}\label{melt non-bonded paramters}
The accuracy of the MARTINI CG parameters for amylose, amylopectin, and sorbitol was initially validated within a TPS melt system, using reference all-atom (AA) simulation results. The RDF obtained using AA and CG simulation had an excellent agreement in polymer-polymer and sorbitol-sorbitol pairs, resulting in less than \SI{5}{\percent} error in two-body excess entropies. However, a small outward shift in sorbitol-polymer RDF resulted in higher $S_2$ CG simulation (see Figure \ref{fig:nearfarRDFpyrophyllite}), which indicates small room for improvement in cross-interaction parameters. It was also observed that CG simulation gave more expanded structures of the polymer chains, which resulted in a significant higher $R_\mathrm{g}$ ($\approx$ \SI{18}{\percent}) and slightly lower $S_\mathrm{c}$ (6-8\SI{}{\percent}) of amylose and amylopectin chains (see Table \ref{tab:pyrophylliteRgScDn}). These deviations in chain-level properties can be removed by deriving the parameters over a longer polysaccharide chain, as done in our previous work \cite{patidar2024martini}. %The $R_\mathrm{g}$ of amylose and amylopectin obtained in CG simulation were \SI{17.8}{\percent} and \SI{16.7}{\percent} higher, and the $S_\mathrm{c}$ of amylose and amylopectin were \SI{8.0}{\percent} and \SI{6.2}{\percent} lower than reference AA simulation. 
Additionally, the model overestimated the TPS density (\SI{1280.7}{\kg\per\cubic\meter}) compared to AA simulations (\SI{1214.7}{\kg\per\cubic\meter}) at \SI{613}{\kelvin}, but provided an improvement over MARTINI2 model (\SI{1141.1}{\kg\per\cubic\meter}) \cite{Lopez2009}. Overall, the CG MARTINI-3 model demonstrates small improvements in chain- and system-level properties of the TPS melt while achieving marked enhancements in bead-level properties compared to the MARTINI2 model.
%The CG MARTINI-3 model has improved results over the previous MARTINI2 carbohydrate model, but deviations in chain-level properties can be removed by deriving the parameters over a longer polysaccharide chain, as done in our previous work \cite{patidar2024martini}.
\subsubsection{CG Non-bonded Parameters for Pyrophyllite Surface}\label{pyrophyllite non-bonded parameters}
We used Lifshitz theory over experimental contact angle data to derive hydration energies of the pyrophyllite surface (Equation \ref{eq:hydration}), which was utilized to derive the MARTINI bead type for the surface. We obtained the hydration energy of \SI{2.31}{\kilo\joule\per\mol} using experimental contact angle values of non-polar ($\alpha$-bromonaphthalene and diiodomethane) and polar (water and ethylene glycol) liquids over pyrophyllite clay (more details are provided in section \ref{surface hydration energies}) \cite{giese1991surface}. It was reported that MARTINI-3 FF overestimates the hydration energy, but the relative trend for different polarity liquids is maintained throughout. Therefore, the obtained hydration energy value was extrapolated using the best-fit line of experimental and simulation hydration energies of thirty-six solvents (see Equation \ref{eq:extrapolationeq}) \cite{souza2021martini}. We found that the extrapolated hydration energy of \SI{-1.65}{\kilo\joule\per\mol} closely matches that of the SC6 bead (\SI{-2.00}{\kilo\joule\per\mol}), confirming it as an appropriate choice for the pyrophyllite surface. Additionally, the selected bead-type imparts surface hydrophobicity, consistent with experimental studies reporting dispersive nature of surface \cite{giese1991surface} and low water-sorption energies (\SI{1}{\kilo\joule\per\mol}) \cite{churakov2006ab}.\\
The density profiles along the $z$-direction of pyrophyllite sheet (Figure \ref{fig:densitysolvent}) and normalized diffusion coefficient ($D_\mathrm{n}$, see Table \ref{tab:solventDn}) values obtained from CG simulations were nearly identical across different surface bead types for each solvent (hexadecane, propanol, ethanol, isopropanol, and sorbitol) studied, highlights entropic interaction dominance in these systems. Additionally, lower $D_\mathrm{n}$ values in CG simulations, relative to AA simulations, suggest that the MARTINI-3 Force Field overestimates cross-interactions in these systems. We expect that this overestimation should increase with the molecular weight of the molecule as observed in glycans \cite{Schmalhorst2017}, making polymer nanocomposite systems particularly suitable for rigorous evaluation and optimization of CG parameters. Therefore, AA MD simulations of the TPS-PY composite were employed to systematically refine the CG interaction parameters, ensuring fidelity to atomic-scale interfacial behavior.     
The CG simulations of the TPS-PY composite were conducted using surface beads of varying polarity (SC4, SC6, SN2, SN4, and SP2) to systematically compare structural, thermodynamic, and dynamic properties with those obtained from AA simulations (results shown in Table \ref{tab:pyrophylliteRgScDn}).
