\subsection{Temperature-pressure annealing cycles}\label{annealing}
Preparing the Equilibrated TPS Structure
We prepared an equilibrated Thermoplastic Starch (TPS) structure, free from the memory effects of its initial configuration, through a multi-step simulation process. We began by generating an initial simulation box using the Packmol software. To achieve a dense, equilibrated system, we first incrementally rescaled the coordinates of the simulation box by \SI{29.17}{\cubic\nano\meter}, performing energy minimization with the steepest descent algorithm after each step. Following this density increase, we applied temperature-pressure (T-P) annealing cycles to the rescaled TPS system (referred to as "state A"). This was crucial for accelerating the slow dynamics inherent to starch chains, allowing the system to reach an equilibrated configuration more rapidly. During these cycles, we carefully controlled the temperature, ensuring simulation times at both lower and higher temperatures (\SI{613}{\kelvin}) to achieve local equilibration (evidenced by a potential energy plateau) and Brownian relaxation of the polymer chains (Figure \ref{fig:annealing} (A)). After each temperature cycle, we incrementally doubled the pressure up to \SI{1000}{\bar}, subsequently depressurizing back to \SI{1}{\bar} using the same stepwise approach (Figure \ref{fig:annealing} (C)). This entire T-P cycling process was repeated until the densities of the initial (state A) and final (state A') states converged, confirming the attainment of an equilibrated structure (Figure \ref{fig:annealing} (C)). 
