\subsection{Calculation of surface hydration energies}\label{surface hydration energies}
We used the Equation \ref{eq:surfacetension} over the contact angle of $\alpha$-bromonaphthalene (\SI{38.7}{\degree}) and diiodomethane (\SI{51.1}{\degree}) on pyrophyllite clay to estimate the dispersive lifshitz component ($\gamma_{s}^{LW}$) of pyrophyllite clay. The values of $\gamma_{l}^{LW}$ of $\alpha$-bromonaphthalene and diiodomethane were available in the literature and were \SI{44.4}{\milli\newton\per\meter} and \SI{50.8}{\milli\newton\per\meter}, respectively \cite{giese1991surface}. 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:surfacetension}
(1+cos\theta)\gamma_l = 2(\sqrt{ \gamma_{l}^{LW}\gamma_{s}^{LW}}) + \sqrt{ \gamma_{l}^{+}\gamma_{s}^{-}}+ \sqrt{ \gamma_{l}^{-}\gamma_{s}^{+}}
\end{equation}
The $\gamma_{s}^{-}$, $\gamma_{s}^{+}$, $\gamma_{l}^{-}$, and $\gamma_{l}^{+}$ are the donor and accepter contribution solid and liquid polar component ($\gamma_{l}^{AB}$ or $\gamma_{s}^{AB}$), respectively (see Equation \ref{eq:polar}). 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:polar}
\gamma_{l}^{AB} = 2\sqrt{ \gamma_{l}^{-}\gamma_{l}^{+}}
\end{equation}
The $\gamma_{l}^{-}$ and $\gamma_{l}^{+}$ is zero for these two liquids, making $\gamma_{l}^{LW}$ and $\gamma_{l}$ equal. We obtained $\gamma_{s}^{LW}$ of \SI{35.19}{\milli\newton\per\meter} and \SI{33.67}{\milli\newton\per\meter} from the data of these two liquids. Therefore, we have taken an average $\gamma_{s}^{LW}$ (\SI{34.4}{\milli\newton\per\meter}) of these two liquids for the other calculations. The values of the water and ethylene glycol contact angles over pyrophyllite surface (\SI{72.8}{\degree} and \SI{48}{\degree}), $\gamma_{l}^{-}$ (\SI{25.5} and \SI{1.92}{\milli\newton\per\meter}), and $\gamma_{l}^{+}$ (\SI{25.5} and \SI{47}{\milli\newton\per\meter}) were integrated in Equation \ref{eq:surfacetension} to calculate $\gamma_{s}^{+}$ (\SI{3.2}{\milli\newton\per\meter}) and $\gamma_{s}^{-}$ (\SI{1.7}{\milli\newton\per\meter}). Further, the values of polar and dispersive components of water and pyrophyllite were integrated into Equation \ref{eq:hydration} to calculate hydration energy ($\Delta F_{hydration}$) of the pyrophyllite surface.
\begin{equation}\label{eq:hydration}
\Delta F_{hydration} = (-2 \gamma_{l}^{LW}+4\sqrt{ \gamma_{l}^{LW}\gamma_{s}^{LW}}) -(2 \gamma_{l}^{AB}+4 \sqrt{ \gamma_{l}^{+}\gamma_{s}^{-}}+4\sqrt{ \gamma_{l}^{-}\gamma_{s}^{+}})
\end{equation}


%(α-bromonaphthalene and diiodomethane) and polar (water)
%The calculated $\Delta F_{hydration}$ = \SI{26.46}{\milli\joule\per\meter\squared} were converted to \SI{}{\kilo\joule\per\mol} by multiply with projected area of CG bead (\SI{87421}{\meter\squared\per\mol}).
The calculated $\Delta F_{hydration}$ (\SI{2.31}{\kilo\joule\per\mol}) cannot be directly compared to the hydration energies of the MARTINI-3 CG beads, as the force field captures only relative trends rather than absolute hydration energy values. Therefore, we used Equation \ref{eq:extrapolationeq} to extrapolate experimental $\Delta F_{hydration}$ to the simulation $\Delta F_{hydration}$. The equation was obtained by fitting the experimental and simulation hydration energies of thirty-six organic liquids of various polarities, and reported in the recent MARTINI-3 work \cite{souza2021martini}.
\begin{equation}\label{eq:extrapolationeq}
y=0.65x-4.52
\end{equation},
Where y and x represent the hydration energies of various polarity liquids using CG simulation and experiments, respectively.  The calculated hydration value (\SI{-1.65}{\kilo\joule\per\mol}) is in close agreement with SC6 beads (\SI{-2.00}{\kilo\joule\per\mol}) \cite{souza2021martini}, and the choice seems reasonable, as pyrophyllite clay has a nonpolar nature \cite{giese1991surface}.  
