To summarise, at each time step, agents observe the current population opinion and the external signal, compute payoffs and the corresponding switching probabilities, updating their states accordingly. This process is iterated over time and across multiple simulations. In the memory-based setting, agents evaluate actions based on the recent history of the external signal, not only on the instantaneous one. This introduces temporal inertia, reducing sensitivity to noise and fostering long-term adaptation to persistent environmental trends.

