\section{Introduction}

The optimal value function, often referred to as the marginal function, arises naturally in the study of parametric optimization problems. It quantifies the dependence of the optimal value on perturbations in the data and serves as a fundamental object in sensitivity analysis, variational analysis, and duality theory. Formally, let $X$ and $Y$ be locally convex Hausdorff topological vector spaces. Consider a proper extended-real-valued function $\phi \colon X \times Y \to \overline{\mathbb{R}} = \mathbb{R} \cup \{\infty, -\infty\}$, and let $F \colon X \rightrightarrows Y$ be a set-valued mapping. The \emph{optimal value function} (or \emph{marginal function}) associated with this pair is defined by  
\begin{equation}\label{optimalfunction}
\mu(x) = \inf\{\phi(x, y) \mid y \in F(x)\}, \; x \in X.
\end{equation}

The pioneering work of Auslender~\cite{auslender1979differentiable} on optimal value functions, with applications to sensitivity analysis in mathematical programming, has played a vital role in initiating this line of research.  
Since then, this class of functions has attracted considerable attention due to central significance in variational analysis, parametric optimization, control theory, a wide range of applications; see, e.g., the influential works~\cite{an2022subdifferentials,an2021differential,thi2024optimality,chuong2010subdifferentials,dien1991implicit,dinh2010subdifferentials,gauvin2009differential,gollan1984marginal,guo2014sensitivity,ha2005lagrange,le2025regular,huong2024subdifferentials,luc2018semi,lucet2001sensitivity,van2002extensions,rockafellar2009lagrange,qui2020subgradients,thibault1991subdifferentials} and the references therein.  
Comprehensive overviews of this research topic can be found in the seminal books~\cite{bauschke2017convex,borwein2006convex,borwein2005variational,hiriart2004fundamentals,mordukhovich2006variational,mordukhovich2009variational,Rockvariational,hoangtuy2016,zalinescu2002convex}.

Several qualitative properties of the optimal value function, such as lower and upper semicontinuity, were established in~\cite{aubin2007mathematical,bonnans2013perturbation}.  
Further developments on generalized differentiation, with a particular emphasis on the limiting/Mordukhovich subdifferential, were subsequently carried out by Mordukhovich and his collaborators, leading to a rich theory with numerous applications; see~\cite{mordukhovich2005variational,mordukhovich2009subgradients,mordukhovich2012variational}.  
Recent contributions by Yen and coauthors have provided new insights into generalized differentiation under convexity assumptions; see~\cite{an2015differential,an2024differential,an2020differential}.

As highlighted in the aforementioned works, general properties such as the semicontinuity of the optimal value function~\eqref{optimalfunction} often serve as sufficient conditions for strong duality. Moreover, a key feature of the function~\eqref{optimalfunction} is its inherent nondifferentiability, even in cases where the objective is smooth and the constraint set is convex. Consequently, sensitivity analysis for such functions requires tools from generalized differentiation.  
For example, Z\v{a}linescu~\cite{zalinescu2002convex} studied the $\epsilon$-subdifferential of the optimal value function in unconstrained perturbation settings.  
In a different direction, the central role of the Fenchel conjugate in convex optimization has motivated the development of generalized conjugates for set-valued mappings with convex graphs; see~\cite{nam2024notion}.  
This construction plays a pivotal role in the formulation of Fenchel-type dual problems and contributes to broader developments in duality theory.

Based on these observations, the goal of this paper is to further develop and unify the ideas presented in the aforementioned works. We begin by investigating both general and generalized differentiation properties of the optimal value function in a broad setting. In particular, we provide a detailed analysis of its structure, including the domain, epigraph, and strict epigraph. We also examine various regularity properties such as convexity, near convexity (via the strict epigraph), continuity, and both global and local Lipschitz continuity, in both convex and nonconvex settings. To the best of our knowledge, these properties have not been systematically studied for the optimal value function in this level of generality.


Through the lens of generalized convex duality from the pioneering work by Z\v{a}linescu and others (see ~\cite{borwein2006convex,zalinescu2002convex}), we consider the following constrained optimization problem:
\begin{equation}\label{cvx_opt}
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{minimize} & f(y), \vspace{1ex} \\
\text{subject to} & y \in \Omega,
\end{array}
\right.
\end{equation}
where $f \colon Y \to \overline{\mathbb{R}}=\R\cup\{\infty,-\infty\}$ is a function and $\Omega \subset Y$ is a nonempty set.

Suppose that $\phi(0, y) = f(y)$ and $F(0) = \Omega$.  
Then $\phi$ is called a \emph{perturbation function}, and problem~\eqref{cvx_opt} can be rewritten in the perturbed form:
\begin{equation}\label{primal problem}
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{minimize} & \phi(0,y), \vspace{1ex} \\
\text{subject to} & y \in F(0).
\end{array}
\right.
\end{equation}
Based on a formula for the Fenchel conjugate of the optimal value function \eqref{optimalfunction}, we introduce a dual problem for \eqref{primal problem}. This dual problem is defined using the Fenchel conjugate of the cost function $\phi$ and  the afore-mentioned notion of Fenchel conjugate for set-valued mappings introduced in \cite{nam2024notion}. 



The paper is structured as follows.  
In Section~\ref{sectionpre}, we present basic definitions and fundamental elements of variational and convex analysis that are used throughout the paper.  
Section~\ref{sectionproperties} focuses on several general properties of the optimal value function, including semicontinuity, near convexity, and Lipschitz continuity, among others. In Section~\ref{sectionduality}, we establish a representation formula for the Fenchel conjugate of the optimal value function using the notion of the conjugate for set-valued mappings and demonstrate applications to duality theory. Section~\ref{sectionsubdifferential} is devoted to developing major calculus rules for the $\epsilon$-subdifferential of the optimal value function and its Fenchel conjugate.  
Finally, several concluding remarks are given in Section~\ref{secconcluion}.

Throughout this paper (unless otherwise specified), the spaces $X$ and $Y$ under consideration are real, nontrivial, locally convex Hausdorff topological vector spaces.  
The topological dual of $X$ is denoted by $X^*$.  
The bilinear form $\langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle \colon X^* \times X \to \mathbb{R}$ is defined by $\langle x^*, x \rangle = x^*(x)$.



\section{Basic Definitions and Preliminaries} \label{sectionpre}
This section presents a brief overview of fundamental definitions and key properties of convex sets and convex functions that are used throughout the paper. For a more comprehensive treatment, the reader is referred to \cite{mordukhovich2022convex,  rockafellar1997convex, zalinescu2002convex}.

As usual, we adopt the following conventions: $\sup \emptyset =-\infty$, $\inf \emptyset =\infty$ and
	$$a + \infty = \infty, a + (-\infty) = a -\infty = -\infty\;\text{ for }a \in \R,$$
     where $\mathbb R$ stands for the set of real numbers. 
We denote
$$\mathbb R_+=[0,\infty)\text{ and }\overline{\mathbb R}=\mathbb R\cup \{\infty\}\cup \{-\infty\}.$$
 In the extended real line $\overline{\mathbb{R}}$, we follow the arithmetic conventions established by Z\v{a}linescu~\cite[p.~39]{zalinescu2002convex}.

For $\Om\subset X$, we say that $\Om$ is a \textit{convex set} if $\lambda x+(1-\lambda)y\in \Om$ for all $0\leq \lambda \leq 1$ and all $x,y\in \Om$. The topological interior and closure of $\Om$ are denoted by ${\rm int}\, \Om$ and $\overline{\Om}$, respectively. 
%The cone generated by $\Om$ is the set$$\cone\, \Om=\{\lambda x\;|\; \lambda\geq 0\text{ and } x\in \Om\}.$$


Given a function $f\colon X\to \overline{\mathbb R}$, the \textit{epigraph}, the {\em strict epigraph} and the \textit{effective domain} of $f$ are denoted and defined by
$$\epi\,f=\{(x,\lambda)\in X\times \mathbb R\;|\; f(x)\leq \lambda\},\text{ }\;   \epi_s\, f=\{(x, \lambda)\in X\times \R\; |\; f(x)<\lambda\}$$
and 
\begin{equation*}
\dom\, f=\{x\in X\;|\; f(x)<\infty\}, 
\end{equation*}
respectively. The function $f$ is said to be \textit{proper} if $\dom\, f\neq \emptyset$ and $f(x)>-\infty$ for all $x\in X$. 

We also denote and define the \textit{finiteness domain} of $f$ by
\begin{equation}\label{intrinsicdomain}
    \mbox{\rm dom}_{\mathbb R}\,f=\{x\in X\mid f(x)\in \R\}.
\end{equation}


Recall that $f$ is said to be a \textit{convex function} if $\epi\, f$ is a convex set in $X\times \mathbb R$. We can easily prove that $f$ is a convex function if and only if $\epi_s\,f$ is a convex set in $X\times \R$.



Furthermore, $f$ is called \textit{lower semicontinuous} at $\bar x\in X$ if for each $\alpha\in \R$ with $\alpha<f(\bar x)$, there exists a neighborhood $V$ of $\bar x$ such that
$$f(x)>\alpha\;\text{ for all }x\in V.$$ 
We also say that $f$ is upper semicontinuous if $-f$ is lower semicontinuous.  

The closed hull of $f$ is denoted by $\Bar f$ and defined as the function such that $\epi\, \Bar f=\Bar{\epi\, f}.$ An equivalent formulation is given by
\begin{equation}\label{closedf}
    \Bar f(\bar x)=\liminf_{x\to \bar x}f(x)=\sup_{V\in \mathcal{N}(\bar x)}\inf_{x\in V}f(x)\; \text{ for }\bar x\in X,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{N}(\bar x)$ denotes teh collection of all neighborhoods of $\bar x$.

The function $f$ is called {\em bounded from below} on a set $\Omega\subset X$ if there exists $\gamma\in \R$ such that
\begin{equation*}
    \gamma\leq f(x)\; \mbox{\rm for all }x\in \Omega.
\end{equation*}

The next notion plays a crucial role in duality theory. The \textit{Fenchel conjugate} of $f$ is the function $f^*\colon X^*\to \overline{\mathbb R}$ given by
$$f^*(x^*)=\sup\{\la x^*,x\ra-f(x)\;|\; x\in X\}.$$
The \textit{Fenchel biconjugate} of $f$ is a function $f^{**}: X\to\overline{\mathbb R}$ given by
$$f^{**}(x)=\sup\{\langle x^*,x\ra -f^*(x^*)\;|\; x^* \in X^*\}.$$


\begin{definition}
Let $f\colon X\to \overline{\mathbb R}$ be a function (not necessarily convex), let $\bar x\in\mbox{\rm dom}_{\mathbb R}\,f$, and let $\epsilon\in\mathbb R_+$. The \textit{$\epsilon$-subdifferential} of $f$ at $\bar x$ is the set
$$\partial_\epsilon f(\bar x)=\{x^*\in X^*\;|\: \la x^*, x-\bar x\ra \leq f(x)-f(\bar x)+\epsilon\text{ for all } x\in X\}.$$
We set $\partial_\epsilon f(\bar x)=\emptyset$ if  $\bar x\notin \mbox{\rm dom}_{\mathbb R}\,f$.
\end{definition}

The $\epsilon$-subdifferential of a  function $h\colon X^*\to\overline{\mathbb R}$ at $\bar x^*\in \mbox{\rm dom}_{\mathbb R}\,h$ is defined similarly.

As noted in \cite[pp. 82]{zalinescu2002convex}, we have $\dom\, (\partial_\epsilon f)\subset \dom\,f$. If $\partial_\epsilon f(\bar x)\neq \emptyset$ for some $\epsilon\in \mathbb R_+$, then $f$ is proper. Moreover, if $0\leq \epsilon_1\leq \epsilon_2<\infty$ then
$$\partial_{0} f(\bar x)\subset \partial_{\epsilon_1} f(\bar x)\subset \partial_{\epsilon_2} f(\bar x)$$
and 
$$\partial_{\epsilon} f(\bar x)=\bigcap_{\eta >\epsilon} \partial_{\eta} f(\bar x)\text{ for all } \epsilon\in \mathbb R_+.$$
Let $\Om$ be a subset of  $X$ and $\epsilon\in\mathbb R_+$. The \textit{indicator function} $\delta(\cdot; \Om)\colon X\to \overline{\mathbb R}$ is given by
$$\delta(x;\Om)=\begin{cases}
0     & \text{ if } x\in \Om, \\
\infty     & \text{ otherwise.}
\end{cases}$$
The \textit{support function} $\sigma_\Om: X^*\to \R$ of $\Om$ is defined by
\begin{equation}\label{supportfunction}
    \sigma_\Om(x^*)=\sup\{\la x^*,x\ra\mid x\in \Om\} \text{ for } x^*\in X^*.
\end{equation}
When $\Om$ is a nonempty set, the $\epsilon$-normal cone of $\Om$ at $\bar x\in \Om$ is defined by 
\begin{equation}\label{normalcone}
    N_\epsilon(\bar x;\Om)=\partial_\epsilon \delta(\bar x;\Om)=\{x^*\in X^*\;|\; \la x^*, x-\bar x\ra\leq \epsilon \text{ for all } x\in \Om\}.
\end{equation}
Given two proper function $f,h \colon X\to \overline{\mathbb R}$, define their \textit{infimal convolution} by
$$(f\square h)(x)=\inf \{ f(x_1)+h(x_2)\;|\; x_1+x_2=x\}\; \text{for }x\in X.$$
For a \textit{set-valued mapping} $F\colon X\rightrightarrows Y$, the \textit{graph}, the \textit{effective domain} and the \textit{range} of $F$ are denoted and given by
$$\gph\, F=\{(x,y)\in X\times Y\;|\; y\in F(x)\},$$
$$\dom\, F=\{x\in X\;|\; F(x)\neq \emptyset \}$$
and
$$\reg\; F=\{y\in Y\;|\; \exists x\in X\text{ such that } y\in F(x)\},$$
respectively. The set-valued mapping $F$ is said to a \textit{convex set-valued mapping} (respectively, a \textit{closed set-valued mapping}) if $\gph\, F$ is a convex set (respectively, a closed set) in $X\times Y$. If dom $F\neq \emptyset$, then $F$ is called is \textit{proper}. 

\begin{definition}
Let $F\colon X\rightrightarrows Y$ be a convex set-valued mapping and let $\epsilon\in \mathbb R_+$. The $\epsilon$-\textit{corderivative} of $F$ at $(\bar x,\bar y)\in\gph\, F$ is a set-valued mapping $D^*_\epsilon F(\bar x,\bar y)\colon Y^* \rightrightarrows X^*$ defined by
\begin{equation}\label{corderivative}
D^*_\epsilon F(\bar x,\bar y) (y^*)=\{x^*\in X^*\;|\; (x^*,-y^*)\in N_\epsilon((\bar x,\bar y);\gph\, F)\}\;\text{for } y^*\in Y^*.    
\end{equation}    
\end{definition}

For simplicity, we denote the $0$-subdifferential of $f$ at $\bar x\in \dom \, f$, the $0$-normal cone of~$\Om$ at $\bar x\in \Om$ and the $0$-corderivative by of $F$ at $(\bar x,\bar y)\in\gph\, F$ by $\partial f(\bar x)$, $N(\bar x;\Om)$, and $D^*F(\bar x,\bar y)$, 
respectively.

\section{Some General Properties of Optimal Value Functions} \label{sectionproperties}
In this section, several qualitative properties of the optimal value function are investigated, including semicontinuity, near convexity, and Lipschitz continuity, as they are fundamental in variational analysis and duality theory.

Define the {\em projection mappings} $\mathcal{P}_1\colon X\times Y\to X$ and $\mathcal{P}_{1, 3}\colon X\times Y\times \R\to X\times \R$ by
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{P}_1(x, y)=x\;\text{ for }(x,y)\in X\times Y
\end{equation*}

and
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{P}_{1, 3}(x, y, \lambda)=(x, \lambda)\; \text{ for } (x, y, \lambda)\in X\times Y\times \R,
\end{equation*}
respectively.

We begin this section by characterizing the domain of the optimal value function~\eqref{optimalfunction}.

\begin{proposition} Consider the optimal value function \eqref{optimalfunction}. Then, we always have
\begin{equation*}
    \dom\, \mu=\mathcal{P}_1(\dom\, \phi\cap \gph\,F).
\end{equation*}
Consequently, $\mu$ is proper if and only if $\phi(x, \cdot)$ is bounded from below on $F(x)$ for every $x\in X$, and $\dom\, \phi\cap \gph\,F\neq\emptyset$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof} Take any $x\in \dom\, \mu$. Since $\mu(x)=\inf\{\phi(x, y)\; |\; y\in F(x)\}<~\infty$, there exists $y\in F(x)$ such that $\phi(x, y)<\infty$. Thus, $(x, y)\in \dom\,\phi\cap \gph\, F$, which implies that $x\in \mathcal{P}_1(\dom\, \phi\cap \gph\, F)$. This justifies the inclusion $\dom\, \mu\subset \mathcal{P}_1(\dom\, \phi\cap \gph\,F).$ The proofs of the reverse inclusion and the subsequent statement are straightforward.    
\end{proof}

We next provide representations of the strict epigraph and  epigraph of the optimal value function~$\mu$ in terms of those of the objective function~$\phi$ and the constraint mapping~$F$.  
These representations play a crucial role in establishing geometric properties such as convexity and near convexity.

\begin{proposition}\label{epirep} Consider the optimal value function $\mu$ given in \eqref{optimalfunction}.
Define the sets:
\begin{equation}\label{om12}
\Omega_s=\epi_s\, \phi,\ \Omega=\epi\, \phi, \; \mbox{\rm and }\; \Theta=(\gph\, F)\times \R.
\end{equation}
Then, we have the following relations:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\epi_s\,\mu=\mathcal{P}_{1,3}(\Omega_s\cap \Theta)$.
    \item $\epi_s\, \mu\subset\mathcal{P}_{1,3}(\Omega\cap \Theta)\subset \epi\,\mu.$
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof} (a) Taking any $(x, \lambda)\in \epi_s\,\mu$, we have 
\begin{equation*}
 (x, \lambda)\in  X\times \R\; \mbox{\rm and }   \mu(x)<\lambda.
\end{equation*}
By the definition of the optimal value function $\mu$, there exist $(x, y)\in \gph\, F$ such that
\begin{equation*}
    \phi(x, y)<\lambda, 
\end{equation*}
which implies that $(x, y, \lambda)\in \epi_s\, \phi$. Thus, we have $(x, \lambda)\in \mathcal{P}_{1, 3}(\Omega_s\cap \Theta)$, and hence
\begin{equation*}
    \epi_s\, \mu\subset \mathcal{P}_{1, 3}(\Omega_s\cap \Theta).
\end{equation*}
Let us now prove the reverse inclusion. Take any $(x, \lambda)\in \mathcal{P}_{1, 3}(\Omega_s\cap \Theta)$ and find $y\in Y$ such that $(x, y, \lambda)\in \epi_s\, \phi$ and $(x, y)\in \gph\, F$. Then $\phi(x, y)<\lambda$. Since $y\in F(x)$, we deduce that $\mu(x)\leq \phi(x, y)<\lambda$. Therefore, $(x, \lambda)\in \epi_s\, \mu$, which completes the proof of (a). 

(b) Since $\Om_s\subset \Om$, by part (a) we have $$\epi_s\,\mu=\mathcal{P}_{1,3}(\Omega_s\cap \Theta)\subset \mathcal{P}_{1,3}(\Omega\cap \Theta).$$
 The second inclusion can be proved similarly to the proof of the reverse inclusion in (a). 
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}\label{def34}
    A subset $\Omega$ of $X$ is said to be {\em nearly convex} if there exists a convex set $C\subset X$ with nonempty interior such that
\begin{equation*}
    C\subset \Omega\subset \overline{C}.
\end{equation*}
\end{definition}
\begin{remark}\label{remark1}
    It follows directly from Definition \ref{def34} that  $\overline{\Omega} = \overline{C}$.
\end{remark}
Given a function $f\colon X\to \oR$, we say that $f$ is a \textit{nearly convex function} if $\epi\, f$ is a nearly convex set in $X\times\R$.

The proposition below provides a characterization for near convexity.

\begin{proposition}\label{NCC} Let $\Omega$ be a subset of $X$. Then, $\Omega$ is nearly convex if and only if $\overline{\Omega}$ is convex and $\emptyset\neq \mbox{\rm int}\,\overline{\Omega}\subset \Omega$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    Suppose that $\Omega$ is nearly convex. Then there exists a convex set $C\subset X$ with nonempty interior such that
\begin{equation*}
    C\subset \Omega\subset \overline{C}.
\end{equation*}
Consequently, $\overline{\Om}=\overline{C}$, and hence $\overline{\Om}$ is convex.  Moreover, it follows that
\begin{equation*}
   {\rm int}\, C\subset {\rm int}\, \Omega\subset {\rm int}\,\overline{\Om}= {\rm int}\,\overline{C}.
\end{equation*}
While Theorem 2.13(ii) in \cite{mordukhovich2022convex} gives the equality 
$${\rm int}\, C={\rm int}\, \overline{C}.$$
Therefore, we conclude that $${\rm int}\, \overline{\Om}={\rm int}\, C\subset C\subset \Om.$$
Conversely, we set $C={\rm int}\,\overline{\Om}$. Then $C$ is an open convex set with nonempty interior and it satisfies $C\subset\Om$. Since $\overline{\Om}$ is convex, it follows from Theorem 2.13 (i) in \cite{mordukhovich2022convex} that
$$\overline{C}=\overline{{\rm int}\,\overline{\Om}}=\overline{\Om}\supset \Om.$$
Thus, $\Om$ is nearly convex. The proof is complete.
\end{proof}

The following lemmas are instrumental in studying the near convexity of the optimal value function $\mu$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem0}
  Let $f\colon X\to \oR$ be a function. Then, we have
    $$\overline{\epi_s\,f}=\overline{\epi\,f}$$
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The proof is essentially the same as that of Lemma 5.1 in \cite{huy2023nearly}, originally established for finite-dimensional spaces. 
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lemmaintersectionnearlyconvex}
Let $ \Omega_1, \Omega_2 \subset X $ be nearly convex sets. If ${\rm int} \,\Omega_1 \cap {\rm int} \,\Omega_2 \neq \emptyset$, then the intersection $\Omega_1 \cap \Omega_2$ is nearly convex.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Since $\Omega_1$ and $\Omega_2$ are nearly convex, there exist convex sets $C_1$ and $C_2$ with ${\rm int}\, C_1 \neq \emptyset$ and ${\rm int}\, C_2 \neq \emptyset$ such that
\[
C_1 \subset \Omega_1 \subset \overline{C_1} \; \text{and} \; C_2 \subset \Omega_2 \subset \overline{C_2}.
\]
Define the set
\[
C = {\rm int}\, C_1 \cap {\rm int}\, C_2.
\]
According to Theorem 2.13(ii) in \cite{mordukhovich2022convex}, we have
\[
{\rm int}\, C_1 = {\rm int}\, \overline{C_1} \; \text{and} \; {\rm int}\, C_2 = {\rm int}\, \overline{C_2}.
\]
It follows that
\[
{\rm int}\, C_1 = {\rm int}\, \Omega_1 = {\rm int}\, \overline{C_1} \; \text{and} \; {\rm int}\, C_2 = {\rm int}\, \Omega_2 = {\rm int}\, \overline{C_2},
\]
which implies \( C \subset \Omega_1 \cap \Omega_2 \).
By the hypothesis, ${\rm int}\, \Omega_1 \cap {\rm int}\, \Omega_2 \neq \emptyset$, so \( C \) is a nonempty open convex set.


Next, we observe that
$$
\overline{C} = \overline{{\rm int}\, C_1 \cap {\rm int}\, C_2} = \overline{{\rm int}\,C_1} \cap \overline{{\rm int}\,C_2}   = \overline{C_1} \cap \overline{C_2}\supset \Omega_1\cap \Omega_2,$$
where we use Theorem 2.14 and Theorem 2.13(i) in \cite{mordukhovich2022convex}.
Therefore,  \( \Omega_1 \cap \Omega_2 \) is nearly convex.
\end{proof}



\begin{lemma}\label{lemma1}
Let $ \Omega \subset X $ be a nearly convex set 
and let $T \colon X \to Y $ be a surjective  continuous linear operator.  Assume that both $ X $ and $ Y $ are Fr\'echet spaces. 
Then, the image $ T(\Omega) $ is nearly convex and
\begin{equation}\label{near13}
    \operatorname{int}\overline{ T(\Omega)} = {\rm int}\,T(\Omega)=T({\rm int}\,\Om).
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} Since $\Omega$ is nearly convex, there exists a convex set $C\subset X$ with ${\rm int}\,C\neq \emptyset$ such that
\begin{equation*}
    C\subset \Omega\subset \overline{C}.
\end{equation*}
These inclusions imply
 \begin{equation}\label{near2}
      {\rm int}\, C\subset {\rm int}\,\Om\subset {\rm int}\,\overline{C}
 \end{equation}
 and
\begin{equation}\label{near1}
    T(C)\subset T(\Om)\subset T(\overline{C})\subset \overline{T(C)},
\end{equation}
    where the inclusion $T(\overline{C}) \subset \overline{T(C)}$ follows from the continuity of $T$. It remains to show that ${\rm int}\,T(C)\neq \emptyset$.
    Since $T$ is a surjective  continuous linear operator, the open mapping theorem (see \cite[Theorem~1.3.14]{zalinescu2002convex}) ensures that $T({\rm int}\,C)$ is an open set in $Y$. Consequently, we have
    \begin{equation}\label{near3}
        \emptyset\neq T({\rm int}\,C)\subset{\rm int}\,T(C).
    \end{equation}
    Moreover, since both $T(C)$ and $\overline{T(C)}$ are convex, we conclude that  $T(\Om)$ is nearly convex.
Consequently, we have   
    $$\operatorname{int}\,\overline{ T(\Omega)} = {\rm int}\,T(\Omega)\neq\emptyset.$$
    For brevity, the proof of the second inclusion is left to the reader.
\end{proof}

We next present two important structural properties of the optimal value function $\mu$, depending on the convexity/near convexity assumptions of the objective function and the constraint mapping. 

\begin{theorem}\label{thmmuconvex}
Consider the optimal value function $\mu$ given by \eqref{optimalfunction}. Then, we have the following assertions: 
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $\phi$ is a convex function and $F$ is a convex set-valued mapping, then $\mu$ is a convex function.
\item If in addition that $X$ and $Y$ are Fr\'echet spaces, $\phi$ is a nearly convex function, and $F$ is a nearly convex set-valued mapping such that 
\begin{equation}\label{QC1}
    \mbox{\rm int}\,\Om\cap \mbox{\rm int}\,\Theta\neq\emptyset,\end{equation}
where $\Om$ and $\Theta$ are defined by \eqref{om12}, then $\mu$ is nearly convex.     
\end{enumerate} 
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
(a) Suppose that $\phi$ is a convex function and $F$ is a convex set-valued mapping. Then $\epi_s\,\phi$ and $\gph\, F$ are convex sets. Using Proposition \ref{epirep}(a), we have $\epi_s\,\mu=\mathcal{P}_{1,3}(\Omega_s\cap \Theta)$ is convex because $\Omega_s\cap \Theta$ is a convex set and $\mathcal{P}_{1,3}$ is a linear mapping. Therefore, $\mu$ is a convex function.

(b) By Proposition \ref{epirep}(b), we have
\begin{equation*}
    \overline{\epi_s\, \mu}\subset\overline{\mathcal{P}_{1,3}(\Omega\cap \Theta)}\subset \overline{\epi\,\mu}.
\end{equation*}
Moreover, from Lemma \ref{lem0}, we have $\overline{\epi_s\, \mu}=\overline{\epi\,\mu}$, and hence 
\begin{equation}\label{near18}
\overline{\epi\,\mu}=\overline{\mathcal{P}_{1,3}(\Omega\cap \Theta)}.
\end{equation}
By Remark \ref{remark1}, $\overline{\mathcal{P}_{1,3}(\Omega\cap \Theta)}$ is convex, and so is 
$\overline{\epi\,\mu}$.

Since both $\Om$ and $\Theta$ are nearly convex, by Lemma \ref{lemmaintersectionnearlyconvex} the set $\Om\cap \Theta$ is nearly convex under  \eqref{QC1}. Because $\mathcal{P}_{1,3}$ is a surjective continuous linear operator, Lemma \ref{lemma1} ensures that $\mathcal{P}_{1,3}(\Omega\cap \Theta)$ is nearly convex and 
\begin{equation}\label{near111}
    \mbox{\rm int}\,\overline{\mathcal{P}_{1,3}(\Omega\cap \Theta)}=\mbox{\rm int}\,\mathcal{P}_{1,3}(\Omega\cap \Theta)\neq \emptyset.
\end{equation}
Combining \eqref{near18} with \eqref{near111} and Proposition \ref{epirep}(b), we obtain
\begin{equation*}
    \emptyset\neq\mbox{\rm int}(\overline{\epi\,\mu})=\mbox{\rm int}\,\overline{\mathcal{P}_{1,3}(\Omega\cap \Theta)}=\mbox{\rm int}\,\mathcal{P}_{1,3}(\Omega\cap \Theta)\subset \mbox{\rm int}(\epi\,\mu)\subset \mbox{\rm epi}\,\mu.
\end{equation*}  
Therefore, by Proposition \ref{NCC} the function $\mu$ is nearly convex. 
\end{proof}


Our next goal to study the semicontinuity properties of the optimal value function $\mu$.  We begin by recalling the definitions of lower semicontinuity and upper semicontinuity of set-valued mappings. 
\begin{definition}
Let $F \colon X \rightrightarrows Y$ be a set-valued mapping and let $\bar x \in \dom\, F$.
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(a)] $F$ is said to be \emph{lower semicontinuous} at $\bar x$ if for any open set $V$ in $Y$ satisfying $F(\bar x)\cap V~\neq \emptyset$, there exists a neighborhood $U$ of $\bar x$ in $X$ such that $F(x)\cap V\neq \emptyset$ for all $x\in U.$

    \item[(b)] $F$ is said to be \emph{upper semicontinuous} at $\bar x$ for any open set $V\supset F(\bar x)$, there is a neighborhood $U$ of
$\bar x$ such that $F(U ) \subset V.$ 
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}
The following result provides sufficient conditions for the upper semicontinuity of the optimal value function.
\begin{proposition} Let $\bar x\in \dom_{\R}\,\mu$, where $\dom_{\R}\,\mu$ is denified by \eqref{intrinsicdomain}. Suppose that $F$ is lower semicontinuous at $\bar x$ and that $\phi$ is upper semicontinuous at every  point $(\bar x,y)$, where $y\in F(\bar x)$. Then, $\mu$ is upper semicontinuous at $\bar x$. \end{proposition}
\begin{proof} Take any $\alpha\in \R$ such that $\mu(\bar x)<\alpha$. Then there exists $\bar y\in F(\bar x)$ such that
 $\phi(\bar x, \bar y)<\alpha$. By the upper semicontinuity of $\phi$, there exist neighborhoods $U$ of $\bar x$ and $V$ of $\bar y$ such that $\phi(x, y)<\alpha$ whenever $(x, y)\in U\times V$. Since $F(\bar x)\cap V\neq\emptyset$, under the lower semicontinuity of $F$, there exists a neighborhood $U_1$ of $\bar x$ such that
 $$F(x)\cap V\neq\emptyset\; \mbox{\rm whenever }x\in U_1.$$
Set $W=U\cap U_1$. Taking any $x\in W$ and $y\in F(x)\cap V$, we obtain
\begin{equation*}
    \mu(x)\leq \phi(x, y)<\alpha.
\end{equation*}
This implies the upper semicontinuity of $\mu$. 
\end{proof}


%The next definition is standard in the literature; see, e.g., \cite[Definition 17.16]{arutyunov2016convex}.
%\begin{definition}   Let $F\colon X\tto Y$ be a set-valued mapping. We say that $F$ is locally compact around $\bar x \in X$ if there is a neighborhood $V$ of $\bar x$ and a compact set $C$ such that     \begin{equation}\label{locallycompact}F(x)\subset C\; \mbox{\rm whenever }x\in V.    \end{equation}\end{definition}

%In order to avoid trivialities, we assume that $F(x)\neq \emptyset$ for all $x\in V$.

Sufficient conditions guaranteeing the lower semicontinuity of the optimal value function are presented below.
\begin{proposition} \label{prolowerseimicontinuity} Let $\bar x\in \dom_{\R}\,\mu$.  Suppose that $F(\bar x)$ is compact. If $F$ is upper semicontinuous at $\bar x$ and $\phi$ is lower semicontinuous at $(\bar x, y)$ for all $y\in F(\bar x)$, then $\mu$ is lower semicontinuous at~$\bar x$. 
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Take any $\alpha \in \R$ with $\alpha<\mu(\bar x).$ We can find $\gamma\in \R$ such that $\alpha<\gamma<\mu(\bar x)$. Then we have
\begin{equation*}
    \gamma<\mu(\bar x)\leq\phi(\bar x,y)\; \text{ for all } y\in F(\bar x).
\end{equation*}
For any $y\in F(\bar x)$, by the lower semicontinuity of $\phi$ at $(\bar x, y)$, there exist open neighborhoods $U_y$ of $\bar x$ and $V_y$ of $y$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{ct3}
    \gamma<\phi(x, z)\; \mbox{\rm whenever }(x, z)\in U_y\times V_y.
\end{equation}
Since $F(\bar x)$ is compact, from the open cover $\{V_y\}_{y\in F(\bar x)}$ above, there a finite subcover
$$
F(\bar x)\subset \bigcup_{i=1}^n V_{y_i}.
$$
By the upper semicontinuity $F$ at $\bar x\in \dom\, F$, there exists a neighborhood $U_{\bar x}$ of $\bar x$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{ct4}
    F(x) \subset \bigcup_{i=1}^n V_{y_i}\; \text{ for all } x \in U_{\bar x}.
\end{equation}
Define the set
\begin{equation*}
U=\bigcap_{i=1}^n U_{y_i}\cap U_{\bar x}.    
\end{equation*}
Then, $U$ is a neighborhood of $\bar x.$ Fix any $x \in U$. Taking any $z \in F(x),$ it follows from \eqref{ct4} that $z\in V_{y_i}$ for some $i\in \{1, \ldots, n\}$. Since $(x, z)\in U_{y_i}\times V_{y_i}$, by \eqref{ct3} we have
$$
\gamma<\phi(x, z).$$ 
Consequently, 
$$
\alpha<\gamma\leq \mu(x)\; \text{ for all } x\in U,
$$
which implies the lower semicontinuity of $\mu$ .
\end{proof}

Let $(X, \|\cdot\|_X)$ and $(Y, \|\cdot\|_Y)$ be normed spaces. In this paper, we equip the product space $X \times Y$ with the \emph{sum norm} defined by
$\|(x, y)\| = \|x\|_X + \|y\|_Y$ for all $(x, y) \in X \times Y$. From now on, we use the simplified notation $\|\cdot\|$ for the norms on $X$, $Y$, and $X \times Y$ whenever no confusion arises.



Next, we discuss the Lipschitz continuity of the optimal value function. We begin by recalling the following notions; see, e.g., \cite[p. 66]{zalinescu2002convex}.



\begin{definition}
Let $f \colon X \to \overline{\mathbb{R}}$ be a function defined on a normed vector space~$X$.
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(a)] We say that $f$ is \emph{Lipschitz continuous} on a subset $\Omega\subset \dom_\R\, f$ if there exists a constant $\ell \geq 0$ such that
    $$
    |f(x) - f(u)| \leq \ell \|x - u\| \; \text{for all } x, u \in \Omega.
    $$
    \item[(b)] We say that $f$ is \emph{locally Lipschitz continuous} around $\bar{x} \in  \dom_\R\, f$ if there exist a neighborhood $U$ of $\bar{x}$ and a constant $\ell \geq 0$ such that
    $$
    |f(x) - f(u)| \leq \ell \|x - u\| \; \text{for all } x, u \in U.
    $$
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}


\begin{definition} Let $F \colon X \rightrightarrows Y$ be a set-valued mapping between normed vector spaces.  
We say that $F$ is \emph{Lipschitz continuous} if the exists a constant $\ell \geq 0$ such that
$$
F(u) \subset F(x) + \ell \|x-u\| \mathbb{B}_Y \; \text{for all } x, u \in X,
$$
where $\mathbb{B}_Y$ denotes the closed unit ball in $Y$.
\end{definition}


We now establish sufficient conditions for the Lipschitz continuity of the optimal value function.

\begin{theorem} Let $X$ and $Y$ be normed vector spaces. Consider the optimal value function $\mu$ defined by \eqref{optimalfunction}. Suppose that $\phi$ is finite and Lipschitz continuous  on $X\times Y$ and $F$ is Lipschitz continuous. Then $\mu$ is  Lipschitz continuous on the set $\dom_{\R}\,\mu$. 
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Taking any $x, u\in \dom_{\R}\,\mu$, we have $x, u\in \dom\, F$. By the Lipschitz continuity of $\phi$, there exists $\ell\geq0$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{lip1}
    \mu(x)\leq \phi(x, y)\leq \phi(u, y)+\ell\|x-u\|\;\ \mbox{\rm whenever }y\in F(x).
\end{equation}
Since $F$ is Lipschitz continuous, there exists $\ell_1\geq0$ such that   
\begin{equation*}
    F(u)\subset F(x)+\ell_1\|x-u\|\B_Y.
\end{equation*}
Thus,  any $z\in F(u)$ can be written as
\begin{equation*}
    z=y+\ell_1\|x-u\|e,
\end{equation*}
for some $y\in F(x)$ and $e\in \B_Y$. It follows from \eqref{lip1} that
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
    \mu(x)&\leq \phi(u, y)+\ell\|x-u\|\\
    &=\phi(u, z-\ell_1\|x-u\|e)+\ell\|x-u\|\\
    &\leq \phi(u, z)+\ell_1\ell \|x-u\|+\ell\|x-u\|,
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
where the last inequality uses the Lipschitz continuity of $\phi$.
Since $z\in F(u)$ is arbitrary, taking the infimum over all such $z$ yields
\begin{equation*}
    \mu(x)\leq \mu(u)+L\|x-u\|,
\end{equation*}
where $L=\ell_1\ell+\ell$. This clearly shows that $\mu$ is globally Lipschitz continuous on $\dom_{\R}\,\mu$. \end{proof}


To proceed, we recall the following notions; see, e.g., \cite[Definitions~1.40(ii) and 1.63(ii)]{mordukhovich2009variational}.


\begin{definition}
 Let $F \colon X \rightrightarrows Y$ be a set-valued mapping between normed vector spaces and let $(\bar x, \bar y)\in \gph F$. We say that $F$ has the  \emph{Aubin property} around $(\bar{x}, \bar{y})$ if there exist a constant $\ell \geq 0$ and neighborhoods $U$ of $\bar{x}$ and $V$ of $\bar{y}$ such that
$$
F(u) \cap V \subset F(x) + \ell \|u - x\| \mathbb{B}_Y \; \text{ for all } x, u \in U,
$$
where $\mathbb{B}_Y$ denotes the closed unit ball in $Y$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
    Let $G\colon X\tto Y$ be a set-valued mapping between normed vector spaces. We say that $G$ is {\em inner semicompact} at $\bar x\in \dom\, G$ if  for any sequence $\{x_k\}$ in $X$ that converges to $\bar x$, there exists a sequence $y_k\in G(x_k)$ such that $\{y_k\}$  has a convergent subsequence.
\end{definition}

The solution mapping associated with the perturbed problem is defined by
$$S(x)=\{y\in F(x)\mid \phi(x,y)=\mu(x)\}.$$

We conclude this section by establishing sufficient conditions for the local Lipschitz continuity of the optimal value function $\mu$.



\begin{theorem}\label{MLL}
Let $X$ and $Y$ be normed vector spaces. Consider the optimal value function $\mu$ defined by \eqref{optimalfunction} and let $\bar{x} \in \mbox{\rm dom}_{\mathbb R}\,{\mu}$. Suppose that the following conditions are satisfied:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $S$ is inner semicompact at $\bar x$.
\item For any $\bar y\in F(\bar x)$, the function $\phi$ is locally Lipschitz continuous around $(\bar{x}, \bar{y})$ and the set-valued mapping $F$ has a locally closed graph and satisfies the Aubin property around $(\bar{x}, \bar{y})$.
\end{enumerate}
Then $\mu$ is locally Lipschitz continuous around $\bar{x}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof} Suppose on the contrary that $\mu$ is not locally Lipschitz continuous around $\bar x$. This means that for any neighborhood $U$ of $\bar x$ and any $L\geq 0$, there exist  $x, u \in U$ such that the inequality
$$L\|x-u\|<|\mu(x)-\mu(u)|$$
holds. Consequently, we can construct sequences $\{x_k\}$ and $\{u_k\}$ converging to $\bar{x}$ such that
\begin{equation*}
    k\|x_k-u_k\|<|\mu(x_k)-\mu(u_k)|\; \ \mbox{\rm for all }k\in \N.
\end{equation*}
Since $S$ is inner semicompact at $\bar x$, we can assume that  there exist sequences $y_k\in S(x_k)$ and $z_k\in S(u_k)$ such that $y_k\to \bar y$ and $z_k\to \bar z$. By the local closedness of $\gph\, F$ we have $(\bar x, \bar y)\in \gph\, F$ and $(\bar x, \bar z)\in \gph \, F$.  Under the given assumptions, we may assume that there exists a constant $\ell\geq 0$ and neighborhoods $U$ of $\bar x$, $V_1$ of $\bar y$ and $V_2$ of $\bar z$  such that
\begin{equation}\label{LLI}
    |\phi(x_1, x_2)-\phi(y_1, y_2)|\leq \ell (\|x_1-y_1\|+\|x_2-y_2\|)
\end{equation}
whenever $(x_1, x_2), (y_1, y_2)\in U\times V_1$ or $(x_1, x_2), (y_1, y_2)\in U\times V_2$. Furthermore,
\begin{equation}\label{API}
    F(u)\cap V_i\subset F(x)+\ell\|x-u\|\, \B_Y
\end{equation}
whenever $x, u\in U$ and $i=1, 2$.


For sufficiently large $k$, we now
consider two possible cases: either $\mu(x_k)>\mu(u_k)$ or $\mu(x_k)<\mu(u_k)$. 

In the first case where $\mu(x_k) > \mu(u_k)$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{locallip1}
    k\|x_k-u_k\|<\mu(x_k)-\mu(u_k)=\mu(x_k)-\phi(u_k, z_k).
\end{equation}
Since $z_k \in F(u_k)$, $z_k\to \bar z$, by \eqref{API} there exists $\widetilde{y}_k \in F(x_k)$ such that
$$\|z_k-\widetilde{y}_k\|\leq \ell\|x_k-u_k\|.$$
Then, by \eqref{LLI} we obtain
\begin{equation*} \label{local1}
\begin{array}{ll}
       k\|x_k-u_k\|&<\mu(x_k)-\phi(u_k, z_k)\\
       &\leq \phi(x_k, \widetilde{y}_k)-\phi(u_k, z_k)\\
       &\leq \ell(\|x_k-u_k\|+\|\widetilde{y}_k-z_k\|)\\
       &\leq (\ell+\ell^2)\|x_k-u_k\|.
\end{array}
\end{equation*}
In the second case where $\mu(x_k) < \mu(u_k)$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{locallip2}
    k\|x_k-u_k\|<\mu(u_k)-\mu(x_k)=\mu(u_k)-\phi(x_k, y_k).
\end{equation}
By applying a similar argument as above, we conclude there exists $\widetilde{z}_k \in F(u_k)$ such that
$$\|y_k-\widetilde{z}_k\|\leq \ell\|x_k-u_k\|.$$
Then, we have the following estimates
\begin{equation*} 
\begin{array}{ll}
       k\|x_k-u_k\|&<\mu(u_k)-\phi(x_k, y_k)\\
       &\leq \phi(u_k, \widetilde{z}_k)-\phi(x_k, y_k)\\
       &\leq \ell(\|u_k-x_k\|+\|\widetilde{z}_k-y_k\|)\\
       &\leq (\ell+\ell^2)\|x_k-u_k\|. 
\end{array}
\end{equation*}
Therefore, we have arrived at a  contradiction, 
which shows that $\mu$ is locally Lipschitz continuous around $\bar{x}$.
\end{proof}


\begin{remark}
    Let $G\colon X\tto Y$ be a set-valued mapping between normed vector spaces. Recall that $G$ is {\em inner semicontinuous} at $(\bar x, \bar y)\in \gph\,G$ if  for any sequence $\{x_k\}$ in $X$ that converges to $\bar x$, there exists a sequence $y_k\in G(x_k)$ such that $\{y_k\}$  converges to $\bar y$. In the setting of Theorem \ref{MLL}, suppose that $S$ is inner semicontinuous at $(\bar{x}, \bar{y}) \in \operatorname{gph} S$, the function $\phi$ is locally Lipschitz continuous around $(\bar{x}, \bar{y})$, and the set-valued mapping $F$ satisfies the Aubin property around this point. Then we can also show that $\mu$ is locally Lipschitz continuous around $\bar{x}$.
\end{remark}

\section{Fenchel Conjugate and 
Duality for Constrained Optimization via Optimal Value Functions}

\label{sectionduality}

In this section, we derive a representation formula for the Fenchel conjugate of the optimal value function~\eqref{optimalfunction} and apply it to the development of duality theory, within the framework of convex or nearly convex data.

The concept below, first introduced and studied in \cite{nam2024notion}, plays a crucial role in what follows.

\begin{definition}
Given a set-valued mapping $F\colon X\tto Y$, the \textit{Fenchel conjugate} of $F$ is the extended-real-valued function $F^*\colon X^*\times Y^* \to \oR$ defined by
\begin{equation}
    F^*(x^*,y^*)=\sup\{\la x^*,x\ra+\la y^*,y\ra\mid (x,y)\in \gph\, F\}\; \text{ for } (x^*,y^*)\in X^*\times Y^*.
\end{equation}
\end{definition}
It is easy to see from the definition of the support function~\eqref{supportfunction} that the Fenchel conjugate of $F$ is the support function of $\gph\, F$, i.e,
$$F^*(x^*,y^*)=\sigma_{\gph\, F}(x^*, y^*)\text{ for } (x^*,y^*)\in X^*\times Y^*.$$
The following lemma serves as a technical tool for representing the Fenchel conjugate of the optimal value function. 
\begin{lemma}\label{lemsigma}
    Let $\Om_1$ and $\Om_2$ be nearly convex subsets of $X$. Assume that ${\rm int}\, \Om_1\cap {\rm int}\, \Om_2\neq \emptyset.$ Then for every $x^*\in \dom\,\sigma_{\Om_1\cap \Om_2}$, there exist $x^*_1,x^*_2\in X^*$ such that $x_1^*+x_2^*=x^*$ and
    $$\sigma_{\Om_1\cap\Om_2}(x^*)=\sigma_{\Om_1}(x_1^*)+\sigma_{\Om_2}(x_2^*).$$
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Since $\Omega_1$ and $\Omega_2$ are nearly convex, there exist convex sets $C_1$ and $C_2$ with ${\rm int}\, C_1 \neq \emptyset$ and ${\rm int}\, C_2 \neq \emptyset$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{est0}
    C_1 \subset \Omega_1 \subset \overline{C_1} \; \text{and} \; C_2 \subset \Omega_2 \subset \overline{C_2}.
\end{equation}
For $i=1, 2,$ it is well known that $\sigma_{C_i}(x^*)=\sigma_{\overline{C}_i}(x^*)$ for all $x^*\in X^*$. Therefore, we obtain 
\begin{equation}\label{estimation0}
    \sigma_{C_i}(x^*)=\sigma_{\Om_i}(x^*).
\end{equation}
Moreover, as shown in the proof of Lemma \ref{lemmaintersectionnearlyconvex}, we have
$$
{\rm int}\, C_1 = {\rm int}\, \Omega_1\; \text{and} \; {\rm int}\, C_2 = {\rm int}\, \Omega_2,$$
Then, by Theorem 4.23 (i) in \cite{mordukhovich2022convex}, there exist $x^*_1$ and $x^*_2$ in $X^*$ such that $x_1^*+x_2^*=~x^*$ and $$\sigma_{C_1\cap C_2}(x^*) = \sigma_{C_1}(x_1^*)+\sigma_{C_2}(x_2^*).$$
This together with \eqref{est0} and \eqref{estimation0} yields to the following estimations:
\begin{equation}\label{etimation1}
    \begin{array}{ll}
    \sigma_{\Om_1\cap\Om_2}(x^*)&\geq \sigma_{C_1\cap C_2}(x^*) \\
    &= \sigma_{C_1}(x_1^*)+\sigma_{C_2}(x_2^*) \\
     &= \sigma_{\Om_1}(x_1^*)+\sigma_{\Om_2}(x_2^*).
\end{array}
\end{equation}
On the other hand, we always have
$$\sigma_{\Om_1\cap\Om_2}(x^*) \leq \sigma_{\Om_1}(x_1^*)+\sigma_{\Om_2}(x_2^*).$$
Combining this with \eqref{etimation1} gives
    $$\sigma_{\Om_1\cap\Om_2}(x^*)=\sigma_{\Om_1}(x_1^*)+\sigma_{\Om_2}(x_2^*).$$
    The proof is complete.
\end{proof}
We now provide a representation of the Fenchel conjugate of the optimal value function, which will serve as a key component in the duality framework.


\begin{theorem} \label{thm42} Consider the optimal value function $\mu$ defined by \eqref{optimalfunction}. Suppose that the function $\phi$ is a nearly convex and the set-valued mapping $F$ is nearly convex. Suppose further that condition \eqref{QC1} holds.
Then for every $x^*\in X^*$, we have the following representation: 
\begin{equation*}
    \mu^*(x^*)=(\phi^*\square F^*)(x^*,0).
\end{equation*}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Fix any $x^*\in X^*$.  Taking any $x^*_1, x^*_2\in X^*$ such that $x^*_1+x^*_2=x^*$ and any $y^*\in Y^*$, we have
    $$\begin{array}{ll}
        \phi^*(x_1^*,y^*)+ F^*(x_2^*,-y^*)&=\sup\{\la x_1^*,x\ra +\la y^*,y\ra -\phi(x,y)\mid (x,y)\in X\times Y\}\\
        &\text{  }+\sup\{\la x_2^*,x\ra -\la y^*,y\ra \mid (x,y)\in \gph\; F\}\\
        &\geq \la x_1^*,x\ra +\la y^*,y\ra -\phi(x,y) + \la x_2^*,x\ra -\la y^*,y\ra \\
        &= \la x^*,x\ra -\phi(x,y) \text{ whenever }x\in X \text{ and } y\in F(x).
    \end{array}$$
Taking the infimum over $y\in F(x)$, we obtain that for every $x\in X$,
$$\phi^*(x_1^*,y^*)+ F^*(x_2^*,-y^*)\geq \la x^*,x\ra - \mu(x).$$
Therefore, 
$$\phi^*(x_1^*,y^*)+ F^*(x_2^*,-y^*)\geq \sup\{\la x^*,x\ra - \mu(x)\mid x\in X\}=\mu^*(x^*).$$
Since $x^*_1,x^*_2$ and $y^*$ are arbitrary with $x^*_1+x^*_2=x^*$, we have 
$$(\phi^*\square F^*)(x^*,0)\geq \mu^*(x^*).$$
It remains to prove that the following converse inequality holds
\begin{equation}\label{conin}
    (\phi^*\square F^*)(x^*,0)\leq \mu^*(x^*).
\end{equation}
We now consider two possible cases: either $\mu^*(x^*)=\infty$ or $\mu^*(x^*)<\infty$. Clearly, if $\mu^*(x^*)=\infty$ then the inequality~\eqref{conin} holds trivially. Otherwise, we first derive from \eqref{QC1} that $\mu^*(x^*)>~-\infty$, and hence $\mu^*(x^*)\in \R$. In this case, by the definitions involved, we have
$$\begin{array}{ll}
     \mu^*(x^*)&=\sup\limits_{x\in X}\{\la x^*,x\ra -\mu (x)\}  \\
     & =\sup\limits_{x\in X}\Big\{\la x^*,x\ra -\inf\limits_{y\in F(x)} \phi(x,y)\Big\}\\
     &=\sup\limits_{x\in X,\; y\in F(x)} \{\la x^*,x\ra -\phi(x,y)\}\\
     &= \sup\limits_{(x,y,\lambda)\in \Om\cap \Theta}\la (x^*,0,-1),(x,y,\lambda)\ra\\
       &= \sigma_{\Om\cap \Theta}(x^*,0,-1), 
\end{array}
$$
where $\Om$ and $\Theta$ are defined by \eqref{om12}. Since condition \eqref{QC1} holds and $\sigma_{\Om\cap \Theta}(x^*,0,-1)\in\R$,
we can apply Lemma \ref{lemsigma} to obtain $x^*_1,x^*_2\in X^*$, $y^*\in Y^*$ and $\lambda_1,\lambda_2\in\R$ such that $x^*=x^*_1+x_2^*$, $\lambda_1+\lambda_2=-1$ and
\begin{equation}\label{thm423}
\mu^*(x^*)=\sigma_{\Om\cap \Theta}(x^*,0,-1)=\sigma_{\Om}(x_1^*,y^*,\lambda_1)+\sigma_{\Theta}(x_2^*,-y^*,\lambda_2).
\end{equation}
If $\lambda_2\neq 0$, then it follows from the structure of $\Theta=(\gph\,F)\times \R$ that $\sigma_{\Theta}(x_2^*,-y^*,\lambda_2)= \infty$, which leads to a contradiction. Therefore, we must have $\lambda_2=0$, which yields $\lambda_1=-1$.  Combining this with \eqref{thm423}, we obtain  
$$\begin{array}{ll}
     \mu^*(x^*)&=\sigma_{\Om}(x_1^*,y^*,-1)+\sigma_{\gph\; F}(x_2^*,-y^*)  \\
     & =\phi^*(x_1^*,y^*)+ F^*(x_2^*, -y^*)\\
     &\geq (\phi^*\square F^*)(x^*,0), 
\end{array}$$
i.e., the converse inequality \eqref{conin} holds, and thus completes the proof of the theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}\label{remark43}
    If $\phi$ is a convex function and $F$ is a convex set-valued mapping, then condition \eqref{QC1} can be replaced by the following weaker condition:
    	\begin{equation} \label{QCS}
			 \text{either }\text{\rm int} (\epi\, \phi)\cap (\gph\, F\times \R)\neq \emptyset \text{ or } \epi\,\phi\cap \text{\rm int} (\gph\; F\times \R)\neq \emptyset. 
		\end{equation}
       Under this condition, the conclusion of Theorem~\ref{thm42} still holds.
\end{remark}
Next, we present a corollary corresponding to the situation in which the set-valued mapping~$F$ plays no role in the constraint, namely when $F(x)=Y$ for all $x\in X.$   

\begin{corollary} \label{cor1}
    For the optimal value function \eqref{optimalfunction}, suppose that $\phi$ is nearly convex and $F(x)=Y$ for all $x\in X$. Then the Fenchel conjugate of $\mu$ satisfies the identity
    \begin{equation*}\label{cor1}
        \mu^*(x^*)=\phi^*(x^*,0)\;\text{ for all } x^*\in X^*.
    \end{equation*}
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof} 
    Since $F(x)=Y$ for all $x\in X$, we have $\gph\; F=X\times Y$. Moreover, since $\phi$ is nearly convex, ${\rm int}(\epi\, \phi)\neq \emptyset$. Therefore, condition \eqref{QC1} is satisfied. By Theorem \ref{thm42}, for every $x^*\in X^*$ we have
    \begin{equation}\label{ctthm4.2}
        \mu^*(x^*)=(\phi^*\square F)^*(x^*,0)=\inf\{\phi^*(x_1^*, -y^*)+F^*(x_2^*,y^*)\mid x^*_1+x^*_2=x^*,\; y^*\in Y^*\}.
    \end{equation}
    Observe that
    $$F^*(x^*,y^*)=\sup\{\la x^*, x\ra+\la y^*,y\ra\mid (x,y)\in X\times Y\}=\begin{cases}
        0& \mbox{ if } (x^*,y^*)=(0,0),\\
        \infty &\mbox{ otherwise.}
    \end{cases}$$
    Consequently, the infimum in \eqref{ctthm4.2} is only concerned with the case where $x^*_2=0$ and $y^*=0$. Then we have $x^*_1=x^*$ and $-y^*=0$. Substituting into \eqref{ctthm4.2} gives $\mu^*(x^*)=\phi^*(x^*,0)$ as desired.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}In Corollary~\ref{cor1}, if the near convexity assumption of $\phi$ is replaced by convexity, then the conclusion follows directly from Remark~\ref{remark43}, which corresponds to Theorem~2.6.1(i) in \cite{zalinescu2002convex}.  
\end{remark}

In the remainder of this section, we study  dual problems associated with the primal problem~\eqref{primal problem}:
\begin{equation*}
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{minimize} & f(y)=\phi(0,y), \vspace{1ex} \\
\text{subject to} & y \in \Omega=F(0).
\end{array}
\right.
\end{equation*}
Denote by $\mathcal{V}_p$  the optimal value of the primal problem, i.e.,
\begin{equation}\label{Vp}
    \mathcal{V}_p=\inf\{\phi(0, y)\; |\; y\in F(0)\}.
\end{equation}
We first consider the following dual problem:

\begin{equation}\label{dual problem2}
\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
    \mbox{\rm maximize }&-\mu^*(x^*),\vspace{1ex}\\
\mbox{\rm subject to} &  x^*\in X^*.
\end{array}\right.
\end{equation}
Based on Theorem~\ref{thm42}, which characterizes the conjugate of the optimal value function, we also consider another form of the dual problem:
\begin{equation}\label{dual problem}
\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
    \mbox{\rm maximize }&-(\phi^*\square F^*)(x^*,0),\vspace{1ex}\\
\mbox{\rm subject to} &  x^*\in X^*.
\end{array}\right.
\end{equation}
The optimal values of the dual problems \eqref{dual problem2}  and \eqref{dual problem} are denoted and given respectively by:
\begin{equation*}\label{Vd}
    \mathcal{V}^1_d=\sup\{-\mu(x^*)\; |\; x^*\in X^*\}\; \mbox{\rm and } \mathcal{V}^2_d=\sup\{-(\phi^*\square F^*)(x^*,0)\; |\; x^*\in X^*\}.
\end{equation*}


We now present several results linking the primal and dual problems to the optimal value function~$\mu$. The first proposition establishes weak duality and provides conditions for the equality of the two dual optimal values.


\begin{proposition}\label{proweakduality} We always have the relations:
\begin{equation*}
 \mathcal{V}_p=\mu(0),\;\mathcal{V}^1_d=\mu^{**}(0) \; \mbox{\rm and }   \mathcal{V}^2_d\leq \mathcal{V}^1_d\leq \mathcal{V}_p.
\end{equation*}
Furthermore, assume that $\phi$ is a nearly convex function, $F$ is a nearly convex set-valued mapping and that condition \eqref{QC1} holds. Then we have
\begin{equation}\label{wdl}
    \mathcal{V}^1_d=\mathcal{V}^2_d.
    \end{equation}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
By definitions, we obtain the first relation:
\begin{equation*}
    \mu(0)=\inf\{\phi(0,y)\; |\; y\in F(0)\}=\mathcal{V}_p.
\end{equation*}
From the definition of the Fenchel biconjugate $\mu^{**}$, we have the second relation:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{array}{ll}
     \mu^{**}(0)&=\sup\{\la 0, x^*\ra-\mu^*(x^*)\; |\; x^*\in X^*\} =\mathcal{V}^1_d.
\end{array}
\end{equation*}
Since $\mu^{**}(0)\leq \mu(0)$, we conclude that  $\mathcal{V}_d^1\leq \mathcal{V}_p.$ Moreover, it follows from the proof of Theorem \ref{thm42} that
\begin{equation*}
    (\phi^*\square F^*)(x^*, 0)\geq \mu^*(x^*)\; \mbox{\rm for all }x^*\in X^*,
\end{equation*}
which implies the last relation
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{V}^2_d=\sup\{-(\phi^*\square F^*)(x^*,0)\; |\; x^*\in X^*\}\leq \sup\{-\mu(x^*)\; |\; x^*\in X^*\}=\mathcal{V}^1_d\leq \mathcal{V}_p.
\end{equation*}
Under the additional assumptions made, the identity \eqref{wdl} follows directly from Theorem~\ref{thm42}. The proof is complete.
\end{proof}



The following theorem characterizes strong duality within the framework of near convexity.

\begin{theorem} \label{resultdualrelation}
 Let $\bar x^*\in X^*$. 
 Suppose that $\phi$ is a nearly convex function and that $F$ is a nearly convex set-valued mapping. Suppose further that $\mu$ is proper and condition \eqref{QC1} holds.
 Then $\bar x^*\in \partial \mu(0)$ if and only if $\bar x^*$ is a solution to problem \eqref{dual problem2}, $(\bar x^*,0)$ is a solution to problem \eqref{dual problem} and the optimal values coincide, that is, $$\mathcal{V}_p = \mathcal{V}^1_d=\mathcal{V}^2_d.$$ 
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof} Observe first that, by Theorem~\ref{thmmuconvex}(ii), the function $\mu$ is nearly convex.

If $\bar x^*\in \partial \mu(0)$ then
$$\la \bar x^*,x-0\ra \leq \mu(x)-\mu(0)\,\text{ for all } x\in X,$$
which implies that
$$\mu(0)\leq -\mu^*(\bar x^*).$$
Combing this with Theorem \ref{thm42} and Proposition \ref{proweakduality}, we obtain the following relations:

$$\mathcal{V}_p=\mu(0)\leq -\mu^*(\bar x^*)=-(\phi^*\square F^*)(\bar x^*,0)=\mathcal{V}^2_d=\mathcal{V}^1_d=\mu^{**}(0)\leq \mu(0).$$

Therefore, $\bar x^*$ is a solution to problems \eqref{dual problem2} and \eqref{dual problem} and $$\mathcal{V}_p=\mathcal{V}^1_d=\mathcal{V}^2_d.$$

Conversely, we have by the hypotheses that
$$-\mu^*(\bar x^*)= \mathcal{V}^1_d=\mathcal{V}^2_d=\mathcal{V}_p.$$
Then it follows from the definition and Proposition \ref{proweakduality} that
$$
  \sup\{\la \bar x^*,x\ra-\mu(x)\mid x\in X\} =-\mu(0),$$
  which immediately yields $
\bar{x}^* \in \partial \mu(0).$ The proof is complete.
\end{proof}

Using results from Section~\ref{sectionproperties}, we now provide sufficient conditions under which strong duality holds in the convex setting.

\begin{theorem}
    Suppose that $0\in\dom_\R\,\mu$ and $F(0)$ is compact. Suppose further that $F$ is convex and upper semicontinuous at $0$ and $\phi$ is convex lower semicontinuous at $(0, y)$ for all $y\in F(0)$. Then we have the strong duality:
    $$\mathcal{V}_d^1=\mathcal{V}_p.$$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Under the given assumptions, Theorem~\ref{thmmuconvex}(i) ensures the convexity of $\mu$, while Proposition~\ref{prolowerseimicontinuity} guarantees its lower semicontinuity at $0$.
Moreover, since $0\in\dom_\R\mu$, we can apply Theorem 2.3.4(ii) in \cite{zalinescu2002convex} to obtain $\mu(0)=\mu^{**}(0).$ Then, by Proposition~\ref{proweakduality}, we conclude the strong duality:
    $$\mathcal{V}_d^1=\mathcal{V}_p.$$
    This completes the proof.
\end{proof}


Next, we consider a minimization problem under inequality constraints of the following form:
\begin{equation}\label{Q4}
\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
\mbox{\rm minimize } &f(y), \vspace{1ex}\\
\mbox{\rm subject to} &  g_i(y) \leq 0\; \mbox{\rm for }i=1, \ldots,m, 
\end{array}\right.
\end{equation}
where $f\colon Y\to \R$  and $g_i\colon Y\to \R$ for $i=1, \ldots, m$. 

Set $$X=\R^m\text{ and }g=(g_1, \ldots, g_m).$$ 

Define the Lagrange function by
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{L}(\lambda,y)=f(y)+\sum_{i=1}^m \lambda_i g_i(y)\;\text{ for } (\lambda,y)\in X^*\times Y, 
\end{equation*}
where $\lambda=(\lambda_1\ldots,\lambda_m)\in X^*=\R^m.$
Then the associated Lagrange dual function is defined as:
\begin{equation*}
    \Hat{\mathcal{L}}(\lambda)=\inf_{y\in Y}\mathcal{L}(\lambda,y)\; \text{ for }\lambda\in X^*.
\end{equation*}

Now, let the function $\phi\colon X\times Y\to \R$ be given by
\begin{equation}\label{key29}
\phi(x, y)=f(y)\; \text{ for all }(x,y)\in X\times Y    
\end{equation}
 and let $F\colon X\tto Y$ be the set-valued mapping defined by
\begin{equation}\label{Lagrange Constraint}
    F(x)=\{y\in Y\; |\; g_i(y)\leq x_i\; \mbox{\rm for all }i=1, \ldots, m\}\; \text{ for } x=(x_1, \ldots, x_m)\in X.
\end{equation}
The following proposition expresses the Fenchel conjugate of the function $\phi$ and the set-valued mapping $F$.
\begin{proposition} Consider the function defined in \eqref{key29} and the set-valued mapping defined in \eqref{Lagrange Constraint}. For any $y^*\in Y^*$ and $\lambda=(\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_m)\in X^*=\R^m$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{key31}
    \phi^*(\lambda,y^*)=\begin{cases}
     \sup\Big\{\la y^*,y\ra-f(y)\mid y\in Y\Big\}=f^*(y^*)&\text{\rm if } \lambda=0,\\
     \infty &\text{\rm otherwise,}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation} \label{key32}
    F^*(\lambda,y^*)=\begin{cases}
\sup\Big\{\sum\limits_{i=1}^m\lambda_ig_i(y)+ \la y^*, y\ra\; |\; y\in Y\Big \} &\mbox{\rm if }\lambda\leq 0,\\
\infty &\mbox{\rm otherwise}.      
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof} By the definition and \eqref{key29}, we have
$$\begin{array}{ll}
    \phi^*(\lambda,y^*)&=
     \sup\Big\{\sum\limits_{i=1}^m\lambda_i x_i +\la y^*,y\ra-\phi(x,y)\mid (x,y)\in X\times Y\Big\}\\
     &=\sup\Big\{\sum\limits_{i=1}^m\lambda_i x_i +\la y^*,y\ra-f(y)\mid (x,y)\in X\times Y\Big\},
\end{array}
$$
where $x=(x_1\ldots,x_m)\in X=\R^m$. Clearly, if $(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_m)\neq (0,\ldots,0)$, then $$\phi^*(\lambda,y^*)=\infty.$$ Otherwise, we obtain
$$\phi^*(\lambda,y^*)=
     \sup\{\la y^*,y\ra-f(y)\mid y\in Y\},
$$
and hence \eqref{key31} is satisfied.
Next, we also have by definition that
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
F^*(\lambda,y^*)=\sup\Big\{\sum\limits_{i=1}^m\lambda_i x_i+ \la y^*, y\ra\; |\; g(y)\leq x,\; x\in X,\; y\in Y\Big\}.    
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Consider the case where $\lambda_i>0$ for some $i=1, \ldots, m$. In this case, we can see that $F^*(\lambda,y^*)=\infty$. In the case where $\lambda\leq 0$ (i.e., $\lambda_i\leq 0$ for all $i=1, \ldots, m$), we can verify that
$$
\begin{array}{ll}
     F^*(\lambda,y^*)&=\sup\{\la \lambda,x\ra+\la y^*, y\ra\; |\; g(y)\leq x,\; x\in X, \, y\in Y\} \\[0.1in]
     &=\sup\{\la \lambda,x\ra+\la y^*, y\ra\; |\; g(y)+\alpha= x,\; \alpha\geq 0,\; x\in X, \, y\in Y\} \\[0.1in]
    &=\sup\{\la \lambda,g(y)\ra+\la \lambda,\alpha\ra+\la y^*, y\ra\; |\; \alpha\geq 0,\; y\in Y\} \\[0.1in]
    &=\sup\{\la \lambda,g(y)\ra+\la y^*, y\ra\; |\; y\in Y\} \\[0.1in]
     &= \sup\Big\{\sum\limits_{i=1}^m \lambda_ig_i(y)+\la y^*, y\ra\; |\; y\in Y\Big\}.
\end{array}$$
This clearly implies that \eqref{key32} is true. 
\end{proof}


To conclude this section, we demonstrate that the dual problem \eqref{dual problem} can be reformulated as the classical Lagrangian dual problem.

\begin{proposition} Consider the function $\phi$ and the set-valued mapping $F$ given by \eqref{key29} and \eqref{Lagrange Constraint}, respectively. Then for every $\lambda=(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_m)\in X^*$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{duallagrane1}
      \mu^*(-\lambda)=\begin{cases}
    -\Hat{\mathcal L}(\lambda) & \text{  if } \lambda\geq 0\\
    \infty & \text{ otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof} It follows from \eqref{key29} and \eqref{Lagrange Constraint} that the optimal value function \eqref{optimalfunction} reduces to
\begin{equation}\label{optc}
    \mu(x)=\inf\{f(y)\; |\; g(y)\leq x,\; y\in Y\} \; \text{ for } x\in X.
\end{equation}
Hence, we have      
$$\begin{array}{ll}
     \mu^*(-\lambda)&=\sup\limits_{x\in X}\{-\la \lambda, x\ra-\mu(x)\} \\[0.1in]
     &=\sup\limits_{x\in X}\Big\{-\la \lambda, x\ra-\inf\limits_{y\in Y,\; g(y)\leq x}f(y)\Big\} \\[0.1in]
     &=\sup\limits_{x\in X}\sup\limits_{y\in Y,\; g(y)\leq x}\{-\la \lambda, x\ra-f(y)\}\\[0.1in]
     &= \sup\limits_{y\in Y}\sup\limits_{x\in X,\; g(y)\leq x}\{-\la \lambda, x\ra-f(y)\}.
\end{array}$$
Since $$\sup\limits_{x\in X,\;g(y)\leq x}\{-\la \lambda, x\ra-f(y)\}=\begin{cases}
     -\la \lambda,g(y)\ra -f(y) &\text{ if } \lambda \geq 0  \\
     \infty&\text{ otherwise}, 
\end{cases}$$ 
we obtain
$$
\mu^*(-\lambda)=\sup\limits_{y\in Y}\sup\limits_{x\in X,\; g(y)\leq x}\{-\la \lambda, x\ra-f(y)\}=\begin{cases}
     -\Hat{\mathcal L}(\lambda) &\text{ if } \lambda \geq 0  \\
     \infty&\text{ otherwise}. 
\end{cases}
$$
The proof is complete. 
\end{proof}



\section{Subgradients of the Optimal Value Function and Its Fenchel Conjugate} \label{sectionsubdifferential}
In this section, we present new results on the $\epsilon$-subdifferentials of the optimal value function and its Fenchel conjugate. For simplicity, the analysis is carried out under convexity assumptions, although similar results can be extended to the setting of near convexity.


Consider the optimal value function~\eqref{optimalfunction}.  Given any $\eta > 0$ and $x \in \dom_\R\, \mu$, the approximate solution mapping is defined by

\begin{equation*}
    S_\eta(x)=\{y\in F(x)\; |\; \phi(x, y)<\mu(x)+\eta\} \;\text{ for } x\in X.
\end{equation*}
By the definition of the optimal value function $\mu$, we have 
\begin{equation} \label{emptyS}
S_\eta(x)\neq\emptyset\text{ for all }\eta>0.
\end{equation}
 The following result provides a formula for computing the $\epsilon$-subdifferential of the optimal value function $\mu$ in terms of the $\epsilon$-coderivative of the set-valued mapping $F$ and the $\epsilon$-subdifferential of the extended-real-valued function $\phi$. 


\begin{theorem}\label{thm1}Consider the optimal value function given by \eqref{optimalfunction}. Assume that $\phi$ is a convex function and that $F$ is a convex set-valued mapping.  Assume further that there exists $(\bar x, \bar y)\in \dom\, \phi\cap \gph\;F$ such that  $\phi$ is continuous at $(\bar x, \bar y).$
Then for any $\epsilon\in\mathbb R_+$ and any $\bar x\in \dom_\R\,\mu$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{Esubmu}
\partial_\epsilon\mu(\bar x)=\bigcap_{\eta>0}\;\bigcap_{\bar y\in S_\eta(\bar x)}\;\bigcup_{\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2=\epsilon+\eta,\;\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2\geq 0}\big\{x_1^*+D_{\epsilon_2}^*F(\bar x, \bar y)(y_1^*)\; \big|\; (x_1^*, y_1^*)\in \partial_{\epsilon_1} \phi(\bar x, \bar y)\big\}.
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
To verify the inclusion ``$\subset$'', let us take any $x^*\in \partial_\epsilon \mu(\bar x)$. By the definition of $\epsilon$-subdifferential, we have
\begin{equation} \label{key1}
    \la x^*, x-\bar x\ra\leq \mu(x)-\mu(\bar x)+\epsilon\;\text{ for all } x\in X.
\end{equation}
Take any $\eta>0$ and   any  $\bar y\in S_\eta(\bar x)$. Then, we have $\bar y\in F(\bar x)$ and $$\phi(\bar x, \bar y)<\mu(\bar x)+\eta.$$ This and~\eqref{key1} imply that
\begin{equation*}
    \la x^*, x-\bar x\ra\leq \phi(x, y)-\phi(\bar x, \bar y)+\epsilon+\eta\;\  \mbox{\rm whenever }(x, y)\in \gph\;F.
\end{equation*}
Defining the convex function 
$$\varphi(x, y)=\phi(x, y)+\delta((x, y); \gph\;F)\; \text{ for } (x,y)\in X\times Y,$$ 
we can easily verify that
\begin{equation}\label{key2}
    (x^*,0)\in \partial_{\epsilon+\eta}\varphi(\bar x, \bar y).
\end{equation}
Indeed, by considering two cases $(x, y)\in \gph\;F$ and $(x, y)\notin \gph\;F$, we obtain
\begin{equation*}
   \la x^*, x-\bar x\ra+\la 0, y-\bar y\ra\leq \varphi(x, y)-\varphi(\bar x, \bar y)+\epsilon+\eta\; \mbox{\rm whenever }(x, y)\in X\times Y.
\end{equation*}
Thus, the inclusion \eqref{key2} is valid.  
Applying to Theorem 2.8.3(iii) in \cite{zalinescu2002convex} to the function $\varphi$, we obtain
$$\partial_{\epsilon+\eta}\varphi(\bar x,\bar y)=\bigcup\big\{\partial_{\epsilon_1} \phi(\bar x, \bar y)+\partial_{\epsilon_2}\delta((\bar x,\bar y);\gph F)\;\big|\; \epsilon_1,\epsilon_2\geq 0,\; \epsilon_1+\epsilon_2=\epsilon+\eta\big\}.$$
This and \eqref{key2} imply that 
there exist  $\epsilon_1, \epsilon_2\geq 0$ with $\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2=\epsilon+\eta$ and
 $(x^*_1, y^*_1)\in \partial_{\epsilon_1} \phi(\bar x, \bar y)$, $(x^*_2,y^*_2)\in \partial_{\epsilon_2}\delta((\bar x,\bar y);\gph F)$ such that
\begin{equation*}
    (x^*,0)=(x^*_1,y^*_1)+(x^*_2,y^*_2).
\end{equation*}
Then it follows from \eqref{normalcone} that $(x^*_2,-y^*_1)\in N_{\epsilon_2}((\bar x, \bar y);\gph F)$. By \eqref{corderivative}, we have
\begin{equation*}
x^*=x^*_1+x^*_2\in  x^*_1+D^*_{\epsilon_2}F(\bar x, \bar y)(y_1^*),
\end{equation*}
which completes the proof of the inclusion ``$\subset$''.

To verify reverse inclusion, fix any $x^*$ in the set on the right-hand side of \eqref{Esubmu}. Then, for any $\eta >0$ and $\bar y\in S_\eta(\bar x)$, there exist $\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2\geq 0$ with $\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2=\epsilon+\eta$ such that
\begin{equation*}
    x^*\in x_1^*+D_{\epsilon_2}^*F(\bar x, \bar y)(y_1^*)
\end{equation*}
for some $(x_1^*, y_1^*)\in \partial_{\epsilon_1}\phi(\bar x, \bar y)$. By definition of $\epsilon$-subdifferential, we have
\begin{equation} \label{key3}
    \la x_1^*, x-\bar x\ra+\la y_1^*, y-\bar y\ra\leq \phi(x, y)-\phi(\bar x, \bar y)+\epsilon_1\; \mbox{\rm for all }(x, y)\in X\times Y.
\end{equation}
Fix any $x\in X$. Since $(x^*-x_1^*, -y_1^*)\in N_{\epsilon_2}((\bar x, \bar y); \gph F)$, if $y\in F(x)$ then we have
\begin{equation*}
    \la x^*-x_1^*, x-\bar x\ra + \la -y_1^*, y-\bar y\ra\leq \epsilon_2.
\end{equation*}
This together with \eqref{key3} implies that
\begin{equation*}
    \la x^*, x-\bar x\ra \leq \la x_1^*, x-\bar x\ra+\la y_1^*, y-\bar y\ra+\epsilon_2\leq \phi(x, y)-\phi(\bar x, \bar y)+\epsilon+\eta
\end{equation*}
whenever $y\in F(x)$.  Taking the infimum of $\phi (x,y)$ as 
$y$ ranges over $ F(x)$ gives us
\begin{equation*}
    \la x^*, x-\bar x\ra\leq \mu(x)-\phi(\bar x, \bar y)+\epsilon+\eta.
\end{equation*}
Since $\bar y\in F(\bar x)$, we obtain  $\mu(\bar x)\leq \phi(\bar x, \bar y)$, and hence
\begin{equation*}
    \la x^*, x-\bar x\ra\leq \mu(x)-\mu(\bar x)+\epsilon+\eta.
\end{equation*}
Letting $\eta\to 0^+$, we have
\begin{equation*}
    \la x^*, x-\bar x\ra\leq \mu(x)-\mu(\bar x)+\epsilon,
\end{equation*}
which implies that $x^*\in \partial_\epsilon\mu(\bar x)$. This completes the proof of the theorem.  
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}{\rm Observe that if $F(x)=Y$ for all $x\in X$, then Theorem \ref{thm1} becomes Theorem 2.6.2(i) in \cite{zalinescu2002convex}.
}

\end{remark}
%\end{proof}
The next simple lemma is useful in the sequel.
\begin{lemma}\label{lem1}
Let $\varphi \colon X\times Y\to \oR$ be defined by 
$$
        \varphi(x, y)=\phi(x, y)+\delta((x, y); \gph\;F) \; \text{ for } (x,y)\in X\times Y.
$$
Then 
\begin{equation}\label{ctthm21}
    \mu(x)=\inf\{\varphi(x, y)\; |\; y\in F(x)\}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, for every $\bar x^*\in X^*$ we have
\begin{equation}\label{muvarphi}
\mu^*(\bar x^*)=\sup\limits_{(x,y)\in X\times Y}\Big\{\la (\bar x^*,0),(x,y)\ra-\varphi(x, y)\Big\}=\varphi^*(\bar x^*,0).
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
It suffices to prove \eqref{muvarphi} because \eqref{ctthm21} holds obviously. Let $\bar x^*$ be an arbitrary element in $ X^*$. Fix any $x\in X$. If $y\notin F(x)$, then $\varphi(x,y)=\infty,$ which implies that
$$\la \bar x^*,x\ra- \inf\limits_{y\notin F(x)}\varphi(x,y)=-\infty.$$
Since $x$ is arbitrary, we deduce that
\begin{equation}\label{varphi}
\sup\limits_{x\in X}\Big\{\la \bar x^*,x\ra-\inf\limits_{y\notin F(x)}\varphi(x, y)\Big\}=-\infty.
\end{equation}
Moreover, by the definitions involved, we have
$$\mu^*(\bar x^*)=\sup\limits_{x\in X}\Big\{\la \bar x^*,x\ra-\inf\limits_{y\in F(x)}\varphi(x, y)\Big\},$$
which, combined with \eqref{varphi}, yields
$$
\mu^*(\bar x^*)=\sup\limits_{(x,y)\in X\times Y}\Big\{\la (\bar x^*,0),(x,y)\ra-\varphi(x, y)\Big\}=\varphi^*(\bar x^*,0).$$
This completes the proof.
\end{proof}

We conclude this section by presenting an explicit representation of the $\epsilon$-subdifferential of the Fenchel conjugate of the optimal value function $\mu$. We follow the proof of \cite[Theorem~2.6.2 ]{zalinescu2002convex}.


\begin{theorem} \label{thm2} Under the setting of Theorem \ref{thm1}, suppose further that $\mu^*$ is proper. Then for any $\epsilon\in\mathbb R_+$ and $\bar x^* \in \dom_\R\, \mu^*$, we have
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
    \partial_\epsilon \mu^*(\bar x^*)=\bigcap_{\eta>0}\mbox{\rm cl}\big\{ x\in X\; \big|\;&\exists y\in F(x)\; \mbox{\rm such that } \\
    &x^*\in \bigcup_{\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2=\epsilon+\eta, \;\epsilon_i\geq 0}\{x_1^*+D_{\epsilon_2}^*F(x, y)(y_1^*)\; |\; (x_1^*, y_1^*)\in \partial_{\epsilon_1}\phi(x^*, y^*)\big\}.
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof} Define the convex function
\begin{equation}\label{varphi1}
        \varphi(x, y)=\phi(x, y)+\delta((x, y); \gph\;F) \; \text{ for } (x,y)\in X\times Y.
\end{equation} 
To verify the inclusion ``$\subset$'', let $\eta>0$ be arbitrary. Taking any $\bar x\in \partial_\epsilon \mu^*(\bar x^*)$, we have
$$\begin{array}{ll}
  \Bar{\mu}(\bar x)+ \mu^*(\bar x^*)&=\mu^{**}(\bar x)+\mu^*(\bar x^*) \\
  &=\sup\{\la x^*, \bar x\ra -\mu^* (x^*)\mid x^*\in X^*\}+\mu^*(\bar x^*) \\
      &\leq \la \bar x^*,\bar x\ra+\epsilon,\\ 
\end{array}$$
and hence $$\Bar{\mu}(\bar x)+ \mu^*(\bar x^*)<\la \bar x^*, \bar x\ra +\epsilon+\frac{\eta}{2}.$$
 It follows from \eqref{closedf} that for every neighborhood $V$ of $\bar x$, there exists $x_V\in V$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{ctthm22}
\mu(x_V)+\mu^*(\bar x^*) < \la \bar x^*, x_V\ra +\epsilon+\frac{\eta}{2}.    
\end{equation}

Since $x_V\in \dom\, \mu$, it follows from \eqref{emptyS} that there exists $y_V\in S_{\eta/2}(x_V)$. Then we have 
$$y_V\in F(x_V)\text{ and }\phi(x_V,y_V)<\mu (x_V)+\frac{\eta}{2}.$$
Combining this with \eqref{muvarphi}, \eqref{varphi1} and \eqref{ctthm22}, we obtain
\begin{equation}\label{bdt1}
\begin{array}{ll}
     \phi(x_V,y_V)+\mu^*(\bar x^*)&=\varphi(x_V,y_V)+\mu^*(\bar x^*)
     \\&=  \varphi(x_V,y_V)+\sup\limits_{(x,y)\in X\times Y}\Big\{\la (\bar x^*,0), (x,y)\ra -\varphi (x,y)\Big\} \\
     &< \la \bar x^*, x_V\ra +\epsilon+\eta,
\end{array}
\end{equation}
which implies that $(\bar x^*,0)\in \partial_{\epsilon+\eta} \varphi(x_V,y_V)$.
Using the same argument as in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm1} gives us that$(x^*,0)\in \partial_{\epsilon+\eta}\varphi(x_V, y_V)$ if and only if there exist $\epsilon_i\geq 0$ with $i=1, 2$ such that $\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2=\epsilon+\eta$ such that 
\begin{equation*}
    \bar x^*\in x_1^*+D^*_{\epsilon_2}(x_V,y_V)(y^*_1)
\end{equation*}
for some $(x_1^*, y_1^*)\in \partial_{\epsilon_1}\phi(x_V, y_V)$. Since $\eta>0$ is arbitrary, we have
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
    \bar x\in \bigcap_{\eta>0}\mbox{\rm cl}\big\{x\in X\; |\;&\exists y\in F(x)\; \mbox{\rm such that } \\
    &x^*\in \bigcup_{\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2=\epsilon+\eta, \;\epsilon_i\geq 0}\{x_1^*+D_{\epsilon_2}^*F(x, y)(y_1^*)\mid (x_1^*, y_1^*)\in \partial_{\epsilon_1}\phi(x, y)\},
    \end{aligned}
    \end{equation*}
    which justifies the inclusion ``$\subset$''.

To verify reverse inclusion, let $\bar x$ be an arbitrary element in the set on the right-hand side and let $\eta >0$ be given. Since $\bar x^*$ is continuous, there exists a neighborhood $V_0$ of $\bar x$ such that
\begin{equation} \label{keyeta}
    \la \bar x^*, x\ra < \la \bar x^*, \bar x\ra+\frac{\eta}{2}\text{ for all } x\in V_0.
\end{equation}
On the other hand, for every neighborhood $V$ of $\bar x$, by using the argument as in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm1} again, there exist $(x_V,y_V)\in  X\times Y$ such that $x_V\in V_0\cap V$, $y_V\in F(x_V)$ and \begin{equation*}
 (\bar x^*,0)\in \partial_{\epsilon+\eta/2} \varphi(x_V,y_V).
\end{equation*}
Combining this with \eqref{ctthm21}, \eqref{muvarphi} and \eqref{keyeta}, we obtain 
$$\begin{array}{ll}
    \mu(x_V)+ \mu^*(\bar x^*) &\leq \varphi(x_V,y_V)+\sup\limits_{(x,y)\in X\times Y}\Big(\la (\bar x^*,0), (x,y)\ra -\varphi (x,y)\Big) \\
     & \leq \la \bar x^*, x_V\ra +\epsilon+\frac{\eta}{2}\\
     &< \la \bar x^*, \bar x\ra +\epsilon+\eta,
\end{array}$$
which implies that
$$\inf_{x\in V}\mu(x)+ \mu^*(\bar x^*) <\la \bar x^*, \bar x\ra +\epsilon+\eta.$$
Combining this with \eqref{closedf}, we obtain $$\Bar{\mu}(\bar x)+ \mu^*(\bar x^*) \leq \la \bar x^*, \bar x\ra +\epsilon+\eta.$$
Letting $\eta \to 0$ gives us the relation
$$\mu^{**}(\bar x) + \mu^*(\bar x^*)=\Bar{\mu}(\bar x)+ \mu^*(\bar x^*) \leq \la \bar x^*, \bar x\ra +\epsilon.$$
This shows that $\bar x\in \partial_{\epsilon}\mu^{*}(\bar x^*),$ which verifies the inclusion ``$\supset$'', and thus completes the proof of the theorem.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
Note that Theorem~\ref{thm2} is a generalization of Theorem 2.6.2(ii), extending the result from unconstrained problems to those involving inclusion constraints.    
\end{remark}

\section{Conclusion}\label{secconcluion}
This paper has systematically examined several key properties of the optimal value function in perturbed optimization problems, including convexity, near convexity, continuity, and Lipschitz behavior in both convex and nonconvex settings. We also developed a duality framework for constrained problems defined by set-valued mappings, based on the concept of the Fenchel conjugate for such mappings.

These results enhance the theoretical foundations of variational and nearly convex analysis and provide a basis for future research on duality theory and generalized subdifferential calculus in broader settings.

