\section{The Proposed Method}
\label{section4}
To address these challenges, we first introduce an embedding paradigm, termed \textsc{core}, based on which we introduce CoreMark, a novel watermarking method. As illustrated in Figure \ref{flowchart}, CoreMark operates in two distinct stages: watermark embedding and watermark extraction. 

\subsection{Watermark Embedding}
\label{sec:embed}
The embedding stage involves three components: (1) extraction of \textsc{core}s from individual characters, (2) data embedding through modification of \textsc{core} width, and (3) robustness enhancement for small font sizes via our proposed independent embedding strength (ES) modulator. 

\vspace{0.3em}
\noindent \textbf{Component 1: \textsc{core} and its Extraction Model.}
A \textsc{core} comprises a series of consecutive black pixels, denoted as $ S_{\text{core}} = \left\{S_i\mid i=1, \dots, n\right\}$, where $n$ represents the total number of black pixel sequences. Each \textsc{core} is determined by three fundamental parameters: direction $\mathcal{D}$, starting point $\mathcal{P} = \left\{\left(x_i, y_i\right)\mid i = 1, \dots, n\right\}$, and length $\mathcal{L} = \left\{ l_i\mid i = 1, \dots, n\right\}$.
Specifically, when direction $\mathcal{D}$ is horizontal, each $S_i$ is extracted from an image row; when $\mathcal{D}$ is vertical, each $S_i$ is extracted from a column. The starting point $\mathcal{P}$ indicates the initial pixel in each $S_i$, corresponding to the leftmost pixel when $\mathcal{D}$ is horizontal and the topmost pixel when $\mathcal{D}$ is vertical. The length $\mathcal{L}$ specifies the number of black pixels in each sequence.
In the following, we provide a detailed explanation of the calculation methods for these three parameters, separately.

\noindent \textbf{(1) Determine the direction $ \mathcal{D}$.}
We determine $ \mathcal{D}$ by comparing the average length of consecutive black pixels across different directions. Taking the horizontal direction as an example,
let the dimensions of the character image be (M $\times$ N). 
For each row $ R_m , m=1,...,M$, we apply Run Length Coding (RLC) \cite{thyagarajan2011still} to represent $ R_m$ as a series of data pairs $\zeta_m(\mu_{j}^m, \rho_{j}^m)$, where each $\zeta_m(\mu_{j}^m, \rho_{j}^m)$ signifies a sequence of consecutive pixels with identical values. Here, $ \mu_{j}^m \in (0, \rm N]$ is the repetition count and $ \rho_{j}^m \in \left\{0, 1\right\}$ denotes the pixel value being repeated (where "0" represents black pixels and "1" represents white pixels).
For instance, given the following pixel sequence for $ R_i $:
\begin{equation}
	1, 1, 1, 1, \underbrace{0, \cdots, 0}_{36\enspace  pixels}, 1, 1, 1
\end{equation}
the resulting data pairs are $\zeta_m(4, 1)$, $\zeta_m(36, 0)$, $\zeta_m(3, 1)$.
For each $ R_m$, we identify the maximum value of $ \mu_{j}^m $ across all pairs, denote as $ \mu_{\text{max}}^m $, with the corresponding $ \rho_{j}^m $ denoted as $ \rho_{\text{max}}^m $. After determining these values for all rows, we compute the average length of consecutive black pixels in the horizontal direction by dividing $ U_h$ by $ K_h$, where $ U_h$ and $ K_h$ are calculated as follows.
\begin{equation}
	U_h = \sum_{r=1}^{M}{\mu_{\text{max}}^m \times {\rm sign_v}\left(\rho_{\text{max}}^m\right)}.
	\label{3}
\end{equation}
$\rm sign_v( \cdot ) $ is the sign function defined by 
\begin{equation}
	{\rm sign_v}\left(x\right) = \begin{cases}
		1, &x\ =\ 0\\
		0, &x\ =\ 1\\
	\end{cases}.
\end{equation}
$K_h$ represents the frequency of occurrences where $ \rho_{\text{max}}^m =0$ across all rows. We apply identical procedures to derive $ U_v $ and $ K_v $ for the vertical direction.
If $\frac{U_h}{K_h}$ exceeds $\frac{U_v}{K_v}$, the direction is horizontal, and $S_{\text{core}}$ is calculated using row pixels; otherwise, $S_{\text{core}}$ is calculated using column pixels. Figure~\ref{uh_illustrate} shows the $ U_{h} $ calculation process in the horizontal direction.
\begin{figure}[t]\color{black}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{fig2}
\caption{Illustration of calculating $U_{h}$ in the horizontal direction.}
\label{uh_illustrate}
\vspace{-0.5em}
\end{figure}

\noindent \textbf{(2) Determine the starting coordinates $\mathcal{P}$ and lengths $\mathcal{L}$.}
In general, multiple consecutive black pixel sequences exist along $\mathcal{D}$; however, not all consecutive black pixels along $\mathcal{D}$ qualify as components of $ S_{\text{core}} $. 
Below, we describe the selection process for suitable consecutive black pixel segments in the horizontal direction to construct $ S_{\text{core}} $. We assign the pixel sequences  $\zeta_m(\mu_{\text{max}}^m, \rho_{\text{max}}^m)$ in $\mathcal{D}$, where $ \rho_{\text{max}}^m=0$, as `candidate vectors', denoted as $V_{\text{cand}}$. The corresponding $m$ values of all `candidate vectors' are in the array $ \varPhi$. The starting coordinates and lengths of $V_{\text{cand}}$ are represented as $C_{\text{start}} =\left\{\left(x_t, y_t\right)|\ t \in \varPhi\right\}$ and $C_{\text{len}} =\left\{\mu_t^{\text{max}}|\ t \in \varPhi\right\}$.
The coordinates $(x_t, y_t)$ can be directly derived from $\zeta_m(\mu_{\text{max}}^m, \rho_{\text{max}}^m)$, where $x_t = m$ represents the row index of the sequence $\mu_{\text{max}}^m$. $y_t$  can be computed as follows:
\begin{equation}
    j_\text{max} = \mathop{\arg\max}\limits_{j}\mu_{\text{max}}^{m}; 
    \quad y_t = \sum\nolimits_{t=1}^{j_\text{max}-1}{\mu_t^m} + 1.
    \label{yt}
\end{equation}


Next, we cluster all `candidate vectors' based on their $C_{\text{start}}$ and $C_{\text{len}}$, subsequently selecting one cluster as the final $S_{\text{core}}$.
\begin{algorithm}[t]
	\caption{Method of assigning “candidate vectors”.}\label{alg:alg1}
	\textsl{}
	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
		\renewcommand{\algorithmicrequire}{\textbf{Input:}}
		\renewcommand{\algorithmicensure}{\textbf{Output:}}
		\REQUIRE{$C_{\text{start}}$, $ C_{\text{len}} $}
		\ENSURE{$\mathcal{I}$}

		\STATE Initiate $ C_{1,...,k}^{\text{num}} $, $ C_{1,...,k}^{\text{ave}} $
		\STATE $ // $ \textit{$ C_{1,...,k}^{\text{num}} $ is the number of “candidate vectors” in each cluster; $ C_{1,...,k}^{\text{ave}} $ is the average length of each cluster.}
		\STATE boolean $b_1$, $b_2$, $b_3$
		\STATE $\hat{y} \gets y_1$, $\hat{x} \gets x_1$, $\mu_{\text{max}}, \mu_{\text{joint}} \gets \mu_{x_1}^{\text{max}}$, $n \gets 1$, $k \gets 0$
		\FOR{each $i \in [2, P_{r}]$}
		\STATE $ // $\textit{ Verify compliance with the two rules.}
		\STATE $b_1 \gets x_i-\hat{x}==1$, $b_2 \gets {\rm abs}(y_i-\hat{y})\le T_c$
		\STATE $b_3 \gets {\rm abs}(y_i + \mu_{x_i}^{\text{max}} - \hat{y} - \mu_{\text{max}})\le T_c $
		\IF {$ b_1 \& b_2 \& b_3$}
		\STATE $ // $ \textit{If it matches, join the current cluster.}
		\STATE  $\hat{x} \gets x_i$ , $\hat{y} \gets y_i$, $\mu_{\text{max}} \gets \mu_{x_i}^{\text{max}}$, $\mu_{\text{joint}} \gets \mu_{\text{joint}} + \mu_{x_i}^{\text{{max}}}$, $n \gets n + 1$ 
		\ELSE
		\STATE $ // $ \textit{If it doesn’t match, start the next cluster.}
		\STATE $ C_k^{\text{num}} \gets n$, $ C_k^{\text{ave}} \gets \frac{\mu_{\text{joint}}}{n} $, $ k \gets k + 1 $, $ n \gets 1$, $\mu_{\text{max}},\mu_{\text{joint}} \gets \mu_{x_i}^{\text{max}}$, $\hat{x} \gets x_i$ , $\hat{y} \gets y_i$ 
		\ENDIF
		\ENDFOR
		\STATE $ k \gets k + 1 $, $ C_k^{\text{num}} \gets n$, $ C_k^{\text{ave}} \gets \frac{\mu_{\text{joint}}}{n} $, $ k_{\text{max}} \gets \mathop{\arg\max}_k (C_k^{\text{ave}})$
        \STATE$ \beta^{\text{start}} \gets \sum_{t=1}^{k_{\text{max}}}{C_t^{\text{num}}}$, $ \beta^{\text{end}} \gets \sum_{t=1}^{k_{\text{max}}}{C_t^{\text{num}}} + C_{k_{\text{max}}}^{\text{num}} $
		\STATE Put $ y_{\beta^{\text{start}}},...,y_{\beta^{\text{end}}} $ in list $\mathcal{I}$
		\RETURN $\mathcal{I}$
	\end{algorithmic}
	\label{alg1}
\end{algorithm}

The clustering rules for `candidate vectors' are as follows: (1) rows of $V_{\text{cand}}$s within a cluster must be adjacent; (2) the distance between $ y_t $ values of each $V_{\text{cand}}$ in a cluster must be less than $ T_c $; (3) the distance between $ y_t + \mu_{x_t}^{\text{max}} $ values must also be less than $ T_c $.
After applying these rules and generating multiple clusters, we select the cluster with the highest $ C_{\text{max}}^{\text{ave}} $ as the final $ S_{\text{core}} $, where $ C_{\text{max}}^{\text{ave}} $ represents the average value of $ \mu_{x_t}^{\text{max}} $ within each cluster. We use $\mathcal{I}$ to store the indices of $ R_i $s where the 'candidate vectors' of the final selected cluster are located. With $\mathcal{I}$, we can readily extract the starting coordinates $\mathcal{P}$ and lengths $\mathcal{L}$ of $S_{\text{core}}$ from $C_{\text{start}}$ and $C_{\text{len}}$, thereby obtaining the three essential parameters for locating  $S_{\text{core}}$, namely direction, starting coordinates, and length. 
The same methodology applies when the direction is vertical, with the corresponding $S_{\text{core}}$ determined through identical steps. The complete procedure is depicted in Algorithm~\ref{alg1}.

We now explain the robustness of $S_{\text{core}}$, which arises from two key factors. \textbf{1)} We exclusively focus on $S_{\text{core}}$, ensuring that unconnected noisy pixels exert no influence on its extraction. \textbf{2)} We employ RLC to represent each $R_i$, ensuring that only sequences of consecutive pixels with identical values are considered valid candidates $V_{\text{cand}}$s. Since noisy pixels typically appear as isolated and randomly distributed points, they are inherently excluded from $V_{\text{cand}}$. 

\vspace{0.3em}
\noindent \textbf{Component 2: Character Selection.}
As is known, characters from different languages, fonts, and punctuation systems across the world possess unique pixel distributions. In particular, the distributions of their \textsc{core}s also differ significantly. As shown in Figure \ref{fig_6}, the blue points represent the unsorted average lengths of $ S_{\text{core}} $s, while the red points show these average lengths after sorting, revealing a distribution ranging from long to short.
Longer lengths correlate with greater robustness. Consequently, certain characters prove unsuitable for information embedding due to insufficient robustness.  For example, the $ S_{\text{core}} $ extracted from character `,' is too short to withstand distortions during transmission.
To address this limitation, we propose a character selection strategy. 

Let $ C = \left\{c_i, i=1,..., n_c\right\}$ be the set of all characters in the text image, and $L_{\text{core}} = \left\{l_{\text{core}}^i, i=1,...,n_c\right\} $ be the set of lengths of all $ S_{\text{core}} $s in $ C $, where $n_c$ is the number of all characters. Each $ l_i $ can be calculated using Eq.~\ref{ls}:
\begin{equation}
	l_{\text{core}}^i = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^{\text{len($\mathcal{I}$)}}\mu_j^{\text{max}}}{\text{len($\mathcal{I}$)}}.
	\label{ls}
\end{equation}
We establish a threshold $ T_{\lambda} $ to determine character eligibility. Notably, $ T_{\lambda} $ is not fixed but calculated based on the lengths of $ S_{\text{core}} $s in the document. 
To calculate $ T_{\lambda} $, we first sort all lengths of $ S_{\text{core}} $s from longest to shortest according to $ L_{\text{core}} $,  with the sorted values represented by $\tilde{L}_{\text{core}} = \left\{\tilde{l}_{\text{core}}^i, i=1,...,n_c\right\}$. 
Assuming $ \lambda $ is a value less than 1, we calculate $ T_{\lambda} $ as follows:
\begin{equation}
T_{\lambda} = \tilde{l}_{\text{core}}^{\mathop{\min}\left\{ i \mid \tilde{l}_i > \tilde{l}_{i\times \lambda}\right\}}. 
\label{tlamda}
\end{equation}

Let W = $ w_i, i = 1,...,L_w$ be a watermark sequence with length $ L_w $ to be embedded. We first assess whether the length of $ S_{\text{core}} $ for the current character $ c_i $ exceeds $ T_{\lambda} $; If it does, we embed one bit of information in $ c_i $; if not, we apply the same evaluation to the next character. This process continues until all information has been embedded. 

