\vspace{0.3em}
\noindent \textbf{Component 3: Embedding Mechanism.}
\label{watermark embed}
In our proposed scheme, the thickness of $S_{\text{core}}$, defined by the number of `candidate vectors' it contains, is used as the embedding feature for watermarking. To embed a bit “0”, we ensure the thickness of $ S_{\text{core}} $ exceeds a predetermined threshold $ T_{\Delta} $ by $t$. Conversely, to embed a bit "1", we constrain the thickness of $ S_{\text{core}} $ to be less than $ T_{\Delta} $ by $t$. Here, $t$ represents the watermark embedding strength. 
Let $\alpha_i$ and $\tilde{\alpha}_i$ denote the thickness of $ S_{\text{core}} $ before and after embedding, respectively, and let $w_i$ represent the watermark to be embedded. The watermark embedding process for each character can then be expressed mathematically as:
\begin{equation}
	{\tilde{\alpha_i}} = \begin{cases}
		T_{\Delta} - t, &\text{if $\Delta \le t$ and $ w_i = 1$}\\
		\alpha_i, &\text{if $\Delta > t$ and $ w_i = 1$}\\
		T_{\Delta} + t, &\text{if $\Delta \ge t$ and $ w_i = 0$}\\
		\alpha_i, &\text{if $\Delta < t$ and $ w_i = 0$}\\
	\end{cases}.
	\label{eq8}
\end{equation}
Specifically, we select a specific text line as a benchmark for calculating $ T_{\Delta} $. Characters in this benchmark line are excluded from watermark embedding. If $ N_q $ represents the number of characters in the benchmark line, $ T_{\Delta} $ can be formulated as:
\begin{equation}
	T_{\Delta} = \frac{1}{N_q}\sum_{i=1}^{N_q}{\alpha_i}.
	\label{tdelta}
\end{equation}
In addition, $\Delta$ is computed as $\Delta = T_{\Delta} - \alpha_i$.

We satisfy the condition specified in Eq.~\ref{eq8} by either thinning or thickening $ S_{\text{core}} $. In particular, human perceptual factors are considered by analyzing neighboring pixels of $S_{\text{core}}$ to ensure that modifications do not introduce visually perceptible differences. The methods for thickening and thinning are discussed separately below.

\subsubsection{Thinning}
To ensure that $ C_{\text{max}}^{\text{ave}} $ remains the largest value among $ C_{1,...,k}^{\text{ave}} $ after thinning, we select the shorter `candidate vectors' on both sides of $ S_{\text{core}} $ and convert their black pixels to white. Using the horizontal direction as an example, the thinning process follows these steps:
\begin{enumerate}[\hspace{2.95em}]
	\item[Step 1:] Compare $ l_1 $ and $ l_n $ to identify the shorter side.
	\item[Step 2:] {\color{black}If $l_1$ is shorter, use row $\mathcal{I}_1 \!-\! 1$ as a reference. A black pixel in row $\mathcal{I}_1$ is deemed `flippable' if the adjacent pixel in row $\mathcal{I}_1\!-\!1$ is white. Conversely, if  $l_n$ is shorter, use row $\mathcal{I}_{\text{len($\mathcal{I}$)}} + 1$ as the reference row.}
	\item[Step 3:] Repeat the operation in Step 2 for $ \text{abs}(\Delta) + 1$ iterations to complete the thinning process.
\end{enumerate}


\subsubsection{Thickening} Contrast to thinning, we flip the pixels that do not belong to $ S_{\text{core}} $ to thicken a $ S_{\text{core}} $ so that the thickness of $ S_{\text{core}} $ is larger than $ T_{\Delta} $ after thickening. The process of thickening is listed as follows:
\begin{enumerate}[\hspace{2.95em}]
	\item[Step 1:] Determine the longer side by comparing $ l_1 $ and $l_n$.
	\item[Step 2:] {\color{black}If $l_1$ is greater, flip all white pixels to black within the region bounded by abscissas from $ \mu_{\mathcal{I}_1}^{\text{max}} - \text{abs}(\Delta) - 1$ to $ \mu_{\mathcal{I}_1}^{\text{max}} - 1 $ and ordinating from $y_{\mu_{\mathcal{I}_1}^{\text{max}}}$ to $y_{\mu_{\mathcal{I}_1}^{\text{max}}} + \mu_{\mathcal{I}_1}^{\text{max}} $.
	Alternatively, if $l_n$ is greater, flip all white pixels to black within the region bounded by abscissas from $ \mu_{\mathcal{I}_\text{len($\mathcal{I}$)}}^{\text{max}} + 1$ to $ \mu_{\mathcal{I}_\text{len($\mathcal{I}$)}}^{\text{max}} + \text{abs}(\Delta) + 1 $ and ordinating from $y_{\mu_{\mathcal{I}_\text{len($\mathcal{I}$)}}^{\text{max}}}$ to $y_{\mu_{\mathcal{I}_\text{len($\mathcal{I}$)}}^{\text{max}}} + \mu_{\mathcal{I}_\text{len($\mathcal{I}$)}}^{\text{max}}$.}
\end{enumerate}


\vspace{0.3em}
\noindent \textbf{Component 4: Independent Embedding Strength Modulator.}
\label{ES modulator}
\begin{figure}[t]
\includegraphics[width=0.49\textwidth]{fig5}
\centering
\caption{The model of character segmentation. “Complete characters” in blue: A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I, J, K, M, N, O, P, R, S, T, U, W, X, Y, Z. “Incomplete characters” in red: L, Q, V. }
\label{fig_7}
\vspace{-0.5em}
\end{figure}
To enhance robustness when working with small font sizes, we propose a plug-and-play embedding strength modulator (ES modulator). 
First, we extract all text lines from the text image and segment each line into $ N_s $ sub-lines of equal length. Figure \ref{fig_7} illustrates our independent embedding strength modulator, where $N_s = 5$. Characters that remain intact during segmentation are termed "complete characters" (shown in blue), while those that are divided are termed "incomplete characters" (shown in red). Let sub-lines be denoted as $ B_i, i=1,...,N_s $, we embed the same bit $ w_i $ in all “complete characters” within each $ B_i $.  This approach allows the ES modulator to adaptively increase the number of "complete characters" to enhance robustness as font size decreases. Furthermore, we argue that ES modulator is highly modular and method-independent and it can be applied to various methods to improve robustness, which will be demonstrated in Section~\ref{experiment}. 

\subsection{Watermark Extraction}
To extract the hidden message from a text image, we first segment all characters and extract $ S_{\text{core}} $s of each one. Next, robust characters are selected as described in Section \ref{watermark embed}. Let $ \alpha_i^{'}$ represent the thickness of $ S_{\text{core}} $ in the extraction stage. After computing $ T_\Delta^{'} $ by Eq~\ref{tdelta}, we can extract the watermark by comparing $ \alpha_i^{'} $ with $ T_\Delta^{'} $. The watermark detection can be formulated as follows.
\begin{equation}
w_i = \begin{cases}
		1, & \text{if $ T_\Delta^{'} - \alpha_i \ge 0$}\\
		0, & \text{if $ T_\Delta^{'} - \alpha_i < 0$}\\
	\end{cases}.
\end{equation}


