\section{Complex Banach spaces}\label{app:complex}
We recall here a standard procedure to complexify a Banach space $(\cB,\|\cdot \|)$. See \cite[Section I]{Sin} for the details. We consider a real vector space $\cB^2$ and for $(x,y)\in \cB^2$ we define the multiplication by $a+ib \in \bC$ as $(a+ib)\star (x,y)=(ax-by, ay+bx)$. We can thus define the norms
\[
\begin{split}
&\|(x,y)\|_{\cB, \bR}=\sqrt{\|x\|^2+\|y\|^2}\\
&\|(x,y)\|_{\cB, \bC}=\sup_{\theta \in [0,2\pi]}\|e^{i\theta} \star (x,y)\|_{\cB,\bR}.
\end{split}
\] 
The space $(\cB^2, \|\cdot\|_{\cB,\bR})$ is a real Banach space and we can define the complex Banach space $(\bB, \|\cdot\|_{\cB,\bC})$ whose elements are $x+iy$, $x,y \in \cB$. The following easy inequalities hold:
\begin{equation}\label{eq: complex}
\begin{split}
&\|f\|_{\cB,\bC}\le 2\sqrt 2 \|f\|_{\cB,\bR}\\
&\|x\|+\|y\| \le \sqrt 2 \|(x,y)\|_{\cB,\bR}\le \sqrt 2 \|x+iy\|_{\cB,\bC}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}




