\section{Introduction}

The aim of this work is to determine both the $c$-fair profit sharing ratios and the induced expected payoffs at maturity for  the partners of  the islamic profit and loss (PL) sharing - {\em mudharabah} and {\em musharakah} contracts, possibly combined with a {\em wakalah} contract (an agency contract). A {\em PL-mudharabah} contract is a partnership contract  where one of the partner brings the capital and the other, his expertise or labour to manage the funds.  A {\em PL-musharakah }  contract is a contractual partnership where all the partners participate to the capital and  in which profits  and losses are shared between the partners. 

  In fact, the notion of {\em fair} profit sharing ratios  may be find  in \cite{Lessy} for a {\em PL-mudharabah} contract. It  consists of finding the profit sharing ratios that equalise the expected payoffs of the partners  of the contract at maturity. The {\em $c$-fair}  profit sharing ratios principal extends the {\em fair} concept to more general contracts that may be a combination of a {\em PL-mudharabah} contract, a  {\em PL-musharakah }  contract, and  a  {\em Wakalah} contract (an agency contract) and that may involve several partners.  It  is a rating allocation  system  that affects a rating coefficient $c_{\ell}$ to each partners $\ell$ according to his  contribution to the  global  success of the project and  aims to determine the profit sharing ratios that equalise the associated rated contributing payoffs at  maturity: means the $c_{\ell}\, \mbox{Pay}_{\ell}$, for $\ell=1, \ldots, d$, where $\mbox{Pay}_{\ell}$ is the payoff of the partner $\ell$. We then show several new results that elucidate the relation between the profit ratios and several important economic factors as  the investment risk, the labor and the capital,  connecting accordingly the  choice of the profit sharing ratios with the  real economy.    
%We consider  islamic (in fact, the idea is more based on a societal and economic - as well on a financial -  argument than a religious argument so that it may also be used outside the islamic context) Profit and Loss (PL) sharing contract: more explicitly,  {\em PL-mudharabah} and   {\em PL-musharakah} contracts,  and introduce the notion of {\em $c$-fair}  profit sharing ratios as an extension of a {\em fair} profit sharing ratios introduces in \cite{Lessy}.  A {\em PL-mudharabah} contract, means, a partnership contract where one of the partner brings the capital and the other, his expertise or labour,  is said to be fair  if the payoffs of the two partners match at the maturity of the contract.   In this work, we extend the fair-contract idea in different ways by considering first a  different modelling framework that leads to some payoffs - and then, to fair profit sharing ratios - different  from that obtained in  \cite{Lessy}.  Secondly, the  $c$-fair-contract ($c = (c_1, \ldots,c_d) \in (\mathbb R^{\star})^d$, where $d$ is the number of partners) idea  is used to find the $c$-fair-profit sharing ratios in a {\em PL-mudharabah} contract  and  in a general {\em PL-musharakah }  contract (a contractual partnership where all the partners participate to the capital and  in which profits and losses are shared) that may involve several partners with a study of  the particular case where one of the partners (the manager) is bounded with the others by a {\em PL-mudharabah} contract or by a {\em Wakalah} contract (an agency contract).  
 To be more explicit about our contribution we next consider an example that helps understand the context of this work. 
\medskip

  Let us consider a corn farmer who sowed a quantity $L$ of corn seed for a harvesting time horizon $T$. The picked quantity $R_T$ (we suppose that $R_T$ is not identically equal to $L$: $R_T \not \equiv L$) may be considered as a random variable defined on a probability space $(\Omega, {\cal F}, \mathbb P)$. The payoff of the farmer at time $T$ will be $(R_T - L)^{+}$ (for any real number $x$,  we denote \ $x^{+} = \max(x, 0)$) in case of success of the harvest, and, $(L - R_T)^{+}$ when the pick failed. A (commonly acceptable)  rational behaviour from the farmer is to invest in the labor when he expects a success of its operation more than its failure, that is, when
\begin{equation}  \label{Eq-Viable-Intro}
\mathbb E (R_T - L)^{+} \, \ge \,  \mathbb E (L- R_T )^{+} \quad \mbox{or, equivalently } \quad \Delta(R_T,L):= \mathbb E(R_T) - L \  \ge \ 0. 
\end{equation}
Any  $T$ horizon and  $R$-asset based investment with initial capital (which could be physical, like in the previous example) $L$ satisfying the Equation \eqref{Eq-Viable-Intro} at time $T$ will be called a {\em viable investment}.

\smallskip

Now, introduce an investor who brings the quantity $L$ of seed  that the farmer (who cannot get hold of it) needs  in order to receive back a corn.
%
%Money is a medium of exchange and has no intrinsic utility.   An investor who gives a money to a entrepreneur (who may be a farmer, for example) in order to get back a money at time horizon $T$  is like an investor that gives a corn (as a seed) to a corn farmer in order to receive back a corn.
%
%Let  $R_T$ be a random variable,  that models  the harvested corn quantity at time $T$ and let  $L$ be the quantity of seed. 
Here's what we may line up on the behaviour of the investor.
 \begin{enumerate} [leftmargin=*,itemsep=0cm]
\item If the investor suspected strongly that the farmer operation will fail, means, $R_T< L$,  he would likely not take part in it. This naturally imposes the viability constrain \eqref{Eq-Viable-Intro} that  is the framework we consider in this paper. 
\item Now, if he expects a success of the operation (as does the farmer, by the way, if we suppose that they share the same informations), means, $\mathbb E(R_T) \ge L$,  and takes part in the (physical) capital of the corn farmer, two situations may happen at time $T$:
\begin{enumerate} [label= $\diamond$, leftmargin=0.4cm,itemsep=0cm]
 \item {\em the operation succeeds}  $R_T\ge  L$:  the farmer will get the reward from his labor he must  share  with the investor, in proportion to his contribution to the (physical) capital, 
\item  {\em the operation failed} $R_T < L$:  the farmer loses the reward of his labor and is reasonably not  entitled to retain the harvest $R_T$ that should belong to the investor.   Moreover, except if the failure  of the operation results from  a mismanagement of the farmer, in which case the investor may claim a refund of the damage $L-R_T$, it is not justifiable that the investor claims  more than (the wholeness of) the crop  $R_T$. In fact, any additional advantage, in cash or in any other  kind, in  favour of the investor in this context is a {\em rib\^a} (which precept includes the interest-bearing loans principle, but, not only),  prohibited by the {\em Qur'an}.
\end{enumerate}
 \end{enumerate}
%\medskip

\noindent  The islamic financing rests on the application of the {\em Shariah} (the Islamic law)  and  operates in the framework of  {\em rib\^a}-free business transactions. Its major basic principles  include (see e.g. \cite{Askari, Ayub, Beng, Caus, Guer, Iqbal, Kunh-Ming, Lessy}):  the  {\em gharar}-free (and the {\em maysir}-free) financing principle, seeking to avoid excessive contractual uncertainty ({\em gharar}) and speculative,  or gambling transactions ({\em maysir}), the asset-backed financial principal (the principal that any financial transaction must be linked to  tangible assets, promoting  real economic activities and discouraging speculative practices), the risk sharing principle in sale transactions (avoiding for example the sale of an item that is not actually hold) and the sharing of profit and loss principle in partnership contracts. The last two principles may  clearly be seen as some  tools that allow building alternative trading activities to the   first two (the {\em rib\^a}-free and the  {\em gharar} (or {\em maysir})-free principles) restrictions.   The paper deals with the $c$-fair profit sharing ratios in such profit and loss sharing contracts: namely,  the  {\em PL sharing-mudharabah} and the {\em PL sharing-musharakah} contracts.  

\smallskip

To describe  more explicit our contribution, consider  a {\em PL sharing-musharakah} contract involving $d$ partners. In this contract, the profits (if any) are  shared according to some ratios,  we denote $\gamma_{\ell}$, $\ell=1$, $\ldots$, $d$,   that must be determined (but may be amended at any time  before the maturity of the contract) by a mutual  agreement between the partners.   However,  losses, if any,  are absorbed by all the partners, proportionally to their contribution to the capital. For the time being, a {\em PL sharing-mudharabah}  contract between two partners may (in theory) be seen as a {\em PL sharing-musharakah} contract between  two partners where the investor  brings  the whole capital and the other partner, the manager, brings his expertise or his labour to manage the money.  

\smallskip

  Suppose that the partners of the {\em PL sharing-musharakah} contract contributed to the amounts   $L_1$, $\ldots$,  and $L_d$, of the capital $L= L_1$ $+ \ldots $ $+ L_d$.  Let  $\kappa_{\ell} = L_{\ell}/L$ be the equity stake of partners $\ell$, for every $\ell \in \{1, \ldots, d\}$ and let  $\gamma_1, \ldots,  \gamma_d$, with $\gamma_1$ $+$ $\cdots$ $+$ $\gamma_d=1$, be the respective profit sharing ratios of the partners.   As previously,  $R_T$ denotes the net income process of the investment  at the maturity $T$ of the contract. Then, the expected  payoff of each partner $\ell$ at the maturity of the contract is given by  
 \begin{equation}  \label{Eq-Payoff-ell-intro}
\mbox{Pay}_{\ell} (\gamma_{\ell},\kappa_{\ell}) =  \gamma_{\ell} \,  \mathbb E(R_T-L)^{+}  - \kappa_{\ell} \, \mathbb E(L-R_T)^{+}.
 \end{equation}
The  fair profit sharing ratios $\gamma_{\ell}$, $\ell=1, \ldots, d$ is determined by solving the system of equations 
\[
\mbox{Pay}_{1} (\gamma_{1},\kappa_{1}) = \ldots = \mbox{Pay}_{\ell} (\gamma_{\ell},\kappa_{\ell}) = \ldots = \mbox{Pay}_{d} (\gamma_{d},\kappa_{d}).
\]
We will see that this make sense and leads to the solution $\gamma_{\ell} = \kappa_{\ell}$, $\ell=1, \ldots, d$, if all the partners contributed equally to the management of the project.  We know that this is not usually the case and it is of capital interest to know how to take into consideration the additional management (or labour) push of some partners (who, as a partner,  may  compensated their extra-contribution only through their profit sharing ratios).  To see how its works, let us just consider  a  {\em PL sharing-musharakah} contract of two partners who share contributions to  the capital are  $\kappa_1$ and $\kappa_2$.  If the partners manage  together the project with an equal contribution to the management, then, the difference on their global contributions on the project  lies in their share contributions $\kappa_1$ and $\kappa_2$  to the capital. It is reasonable to consider the  fair  profit sharing ratios to be  $\gamma_1 = \kappa_1$ and $\gamma_2 = \kappa_2$. In fact,   in this case, we have
    \begin{eqnarray*}
&&\kappa_2\, \mbox{Pay}_1 (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)   =   \kappa_1\, \mbox{Pay}_2(\gamma_2,\kappa_2)\quad  \\
 &\mbox{or, equivalently, } &  \mbox{Pay}_1  (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)    =    c  \, \mbox{Pay}_2 (\gamma_2,\kappa_2), \ \mbox{with } c = \kappa_1/ \kappa_2.
   \end{eqnarray*}
Suppose now that the two partners (partner $1$ and partner $2$) participate to the management on different degrees and they agree to rate their respective participation to the management (including the labor) by the coefficients $c_1$ and $c_2$ in order to find the profit sharing ratios that  weight accordingly their payoffs. We may think  a situation where $c_{\ell}$ is the number  (supposed strictly greater than $0$)  of unjustified absences of partner $\ell$ to the work, in which case  choosing  $c_1=2$ and $c_2=1$, for example, means that partner $1$ contributed two times less to the management and  partner $1$ contributed $1$ time less to the management.      We aim in this case to  find $\gamma_1$ and $\gamma_2$ so that $\mbox{Pay}_{2} (\gamma_{2},\kappa_{2}) = 2 \, \mbox{Pay}_{1} (\gamma_{1},\kappa_{1}) $ or, equivalently,  $c_1\, \mbox{Pay}_{1} (\gamma_{1},\kappa_{1}) = c_2\, \mbox{Pay}_{2} (\gamma_{2},\kappa_{2})$.   We will show that for any $c_1,  c_2>0$ (see Proposition \ref{Prop-Musharakah-d-Partners2Parts}),
\begin{eqnarray}
\gamma_{1} &=&   \frac{c_{2}}{c_1+c_2}  \big( 1- \rho(R_T,L) \big) +  \kappa_{1} \,  \rho(R_T,L), \label{EqPart1-1-intro}\\
\gamma_{2} &=&   \frac{c_{1}}{c_1+c_2}  \big( 1- \rho(R_T,L) \big) +  \kappa_{2} \,  \rho(R_T,L), \label{EqPart1-2-intro}
\end{eqnarray}
where $ \rho(R_T,L) =  \mathbb E(L-R_T)^{+}  \, / \, \mathbb E(R_T-L)^{+}$ (observe that $\mathbb E(R_T-L)^{+}>0$ if $R_T \not \equiv L$) is seen  as the {\em investment risk} whereas $1-\rho(R_T,L)$ is seen as the {\em investment opportunity}. It is very important to remark that the {\em viability} assumption  \eqref{Eq-Viable-Intro} guarantees $\rho(R_T,L) \in [0,1]$ and then, $\gamma_{\ell} \in [0,1]$, for $\ell \in \{1, 2\}$.  Also note that $\gamma_1 + \gamma_2 =1$. 
 \smallskip

 Furthermore, setting $\bar{\ell} = \{1, 2\} \backslash \ \{\ell \}$ and $c_{\ell, \bar{\ell}} = c_{\bar{\ell}}\, / \, (c_1 + c_2)$, $\ell=1, 2$,  we show that  the expected payoff of each partner $\ell \in \{1, 2\}$ is given by
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Pay}_{\ell} (\gamma_{\ell},\kappa_{\ell})  = c_{\ell, \bar {\ell}}\, \Delta(R_T,L),  \qquad \ell =1, 2,
\end{equation}
meaning that the $c$-fair profit sharing ratios leads the two partners to  share the  {\em expected investment profit }   $\Delta(R_T,L)$ according the respective weights  $c_{\ell, \bar {\ell}}$, \ $\ell =1, 2$. 

   We may also remark that a {\em PL sharing - mudharabah} contract appears   to be a particular case of the  {\em PL sharing - musharakah} contract  where the share contribution of one of the manager (say, partner $2$) is null. In fact, putting $\kappa_1 =1 $ and $\kappa_2=0$ in Equation \eqref{EqPart1-1-intro}-\eqref{EqPart1-2-intro} leads to  the $c$-fair profit sharing ratios obtained for a {\em PL sharing - mudharabah} contract:
   \begin{eqnarray}
\gamma_{1} &=&   \frac{c_{2}}{c_1+c_2}  \big( 1- \rho(R_T,L) \big) +    \rho(R_T,L), \label{EqPart1-1-intro-Mudhara}\\
\gamma_{2} &=&   \frac{c_{1}}{c_1+c_2}  \big( 1- \rho(R_T,L) \big). \label{EqPart1-2-intro-Mudhara}
\end{eqnarray}
 A comparison of the previous result with that  of  \cite{Lessy} may be done by  putting $c_1 =  c_2$ (see Remark \ref{Rem-Comparison-Lessy} for further details). 
