 \smallskip

 Besides the particular application   of  Equation \eqref{EqPart1-1-intro}-\eqref{EqPart1-2-intro} to the  {\em PL sharing - mudharabah} contract, an additional important remark concerns its understanding. In fact,  we may decompose it, for every $\ell \in \{1, 2\}$, as
\begin{equation}   \label{Eq-Reward-PL-intro}
  \gamma_{\ell} =   \underbrace{ c_{\ell, \bar {\ell}}  \, \big(1-\rho(R_T,L)  \big)}_{\footnotesize \mbox{labor reward}} + \underbrace{\kappa_{\ell}   \, \rho(R_T,L)}_{\footnotesize \mbox{funding reward}}.
   \end{equation}
This means  that  the $(c_1,c_2)$-fair profit sharing ratio $\gamma_{\ell}$ of each partner $\ell$ of both  {\em PL sharing - mudharabah}  and   {\em PL sharing - musharakah} contracts appears to be  a reward  $\kappa_{\ell}   \, \rho(R_T,L)$ coming from his contribution  to  the capital (with is proportional to the {\em investment risk}) and a reward $c_{\ell, \bar {\ell}}  \,(1-\rho(R_T,L) )$  due to his contribution to the global success  of the project (which is proportional to the {\em investment opportunity}).  We  still  deduce from Equation \eqref{Eq-Reward-PL-intro}  that   a moderated risky investment (means, the situation where the investment opportunity is greater than the investment risk, or, equivalently,  $\rho(R_T,L) \le 1/2$) rewards more a  contribution to the labour of the project than a participation to the capital, whereas a high risky investment (means,   $\rho(R_T,L) \ge 1/2$) rewards more a  contribution to the capital.  In other words, if a firm, for example, is looking for raising a capital to  finance its business, it will be  in its best interest to find a highly profitable (or a low-risk) activity that will increases its share of the profit.
\smallskip

We also consider the case where the two partners of a  {\em PL sharing - musharakah} contract  appoint a manager (partner 3) outside of them and are bounded with him even by a {\em PL sharing - mudharabah} contract  or by a {\em Wakalah} contract.  In the former case where the management is made via a {\em PL sharing - mudharabah} contract,  the $c$-fair profit sharing ratios are similar to  the one we obtained  for a  {\em PL sharing - musharakah} contract  with three partners by putting to zero the share contribution  of the manager to the capital.  When the management is made via   a {\em Wakalah} contract which arranges a fixed remuneration (a amount $p$ that is paid out  $k$ times up to the maturity $T$) and if they agree (partner 1, partner 2 and the manager: partner 3) to rate their respective payoff by the coefficients $c_1$, $c_2$ and $c_3$, then, the $(c_1,c_2,c_3)$-fair profit sharing  ratios and periodic remuneration $p$ satisfy
  \begin{eqnarray}  
  \varpi(r,T,k) \, p &=&    c_{3,\bar {3}}    \, \Delta(R_T,L)\\
 \gamma_{\ell}  &=& \Big( \frac{c_{3, \bar 3}}{2} + c_{\ell,\bar{\ell}}\Big) \,  \big(1-\rho(R_T,L) \big)   + \kappa_{\ell} \, \rho(R_T,L), \qquad  \ell \in \{1, 2\}  \label{EqPart1-Wakala-d-Prop1-intro}  
\end{eqnarray}
where  the coefficient $\varpi(r,T,k)$ comes from the updating value  of the expected payoff of the manager by considering that their is a ($T$-period) discounted rate $r$ of return that could be earned from  the investment  on some good (in fact, when their is no actuatization, $\varpi(r,T,k) = k$) and is given by $\varpi(r,T,k) =  ((1+r )^{ \mbox{\tiny $\frac{T}{k}$} } -1 ) \, / \, ( (1+r )^{ \mbox{\tiny $T$} } - 1)$,
%\[
%\varpi(r,T,k) =  \frac{(1+r )^{ \mbox{\tiny $\frac{T}{k}$} } -1}{(1+r )^{ \mbox{\tiny $T$} } - 1}, 
%\]
 the weights  $(c_{\ell, \bar {\ell}})_{\ell \in \{1, 2, 3 \}}$ are given by
\begin{equation}  \label{Eq-weights}
 \big(c_{1, \bar {1}},\, c_{2, \bar {2}},\, c_{3, \bar {3}} \big) = \frac{1}{ c_1 c_2 + c_1 c_3 + c_2 c_3} \, \big(c_{2} c_3, \,c_1 c_3, \,c_1 c_2 \big).
\end{equation}
Observe that $\gamma_1 + \gamma_2 =1$.  Furthermore, the expected payoff of each partner $\ell$ (even the partner $3$ who has a fixed $k$-periodic remuneration up to maturity) is given by
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Pay}_{\ell} (\gamma_{\ell},\kappa_{\ell})  = c_{\ell, \bar {\ell}}\, \Delta(R_T,L),  \qquad \ell =1, 2,3.
\end{equation}
 Hence,  the $c$-fair profit sharing ratios leads the three partners to  share the {\em expected investment profit }   $\Delta(R_T,L)$ with the respective weights  $c_{\ell, \bar {\ell}}$,  \ $\ell \in \{1, 2, 3\}$.

A typical application of this result is  the situation  where we consider three partners: an investor (partner $1$)  that  brings all the capital  (so that $\kappa_1=1$),  a bank (partner $2$) that  acts as an  agent  for the investor via a {\em wakalah} contract and arranges a fixed remuneration  $p$ that is paid out  $k$ times up to the maturity $T$ and,  a company (partner $3$), that needs a funding and that is bounded with the bank by a {\em PL-mudharabah} contract  (so that $\kappa_3=0$). This case is studied in Example \ref{Exam-mudha-wakalah}. 
\smallskip

We also generalise the previous results to contracts with several partners. However,  it is  capital to remark that  the computation of the profit sharing ratios and related quantities in all the previous results only needs the computation of the quantities $\rho(R_T,L)$  and $\Delta(R_T,L)$. These quantities may be explicitly computed in some models, like when the  stochastic process $(R_t)$ is supposed to evolve following the famous Black-Scholes model that assumes in particular  that the distribution of $R_T$ is log-normal (see Remark \ref{Rem-Perspectives}). However,  even if the values obtained from the Black-Scholes model  may somewhere be considered as some benchmarks values (especially when considering  a lower volatility framework in which case these benchmark values will be closed to what we may expect from the use of  some more general models), it is  well known that most of the financial data set distribution are not log-normal in general. Then, one of the challenging tasks is to use all the range of econometrics  models (like the  $\mbox{ARIMA}$ or the $\mbox{(G)ARCH}$ model) or more general stochastic diffusion  models (like  local volatility or stochastic volatility models) to find the appropriate model  for $R$,  given an asset data set.  This challenging task will be addressed in another work in order  to alleviate and reduce the size of the present paper. 

\medskip
The paper is organised as follow. We introduce and discuss first the viability assumption and its involvements.  We then study the $c$-fair profit sharing ratios, first  for a  a {\em PL sharing - mudharabah} contract and then, for a {\em PL sharing - musharakah} contract. For the later case,  the matter  of two and / or three partners  has been  considered first   to bring the reading better understood the quantities at stake before generalising it. 


