Now, we may think of the situation where we want  to determine the profit  sharing ratios $\gamma_1$ and $\gamma_2$ such that the expected payoff at maturity for the owner of the capital is rather  $c$ times  (with $c>0$)  the payoff of the worker.  Denoting by
\begin{eqnarray*}
&& \mbox{Pay}_{1} (\gamma_1) :=  \gamma_1\, \mathbb E[ (R_T-L)^{+}] -  \mathbb E[(L-R_T)^{+} \\
&\mbox{ and }&  \mbox{Pay}_{2} (\gamma_2) := \gamma_2\, \mathbb E[ (R_T-L)^{+}], 
\end{eqnarray*}
 the respective expected payoffs of the capital owner and the labourer (or the manager), 
this means 
\[
\mbox{Pay}_{1} (\gamma_1)   =   c \,  \mbox{Pay}_{2} (\gamma_2).
\]
Now,  setting $c = c_1/c_2$, this boils down to 
\[
c_1\, \mbox{Pay}_{1} (\gamma_1)   =   c_2 \,  \mbox{Pay}_{2} (\gamma_2).
\]

This motivates the introduction of an $(c_1, c_2)$-fair {\em PL sharing - mudharabah} contract, $c_i \in \mathbb R^{\star}$, for $i \in \{1,2\}$.  In the notation of the $(c_1, c_2)$-fair contract, the rating constant  $c_{\ell}$ is associated to the payoff $ \mbox{Pay}_{\ell}$.  This rating is introduced to take account of the contribution of each partner to the global success of the project. As it will be seen in Equation \eqref{Eq-Sharing-Weights-mudharabah}, these rating coefficients induces the weight to assign to each partner in the sharing of the common expected investment profit $\Delta(R_T,L)$. Remark that the smaller is the rating coefficient, the bigger will be the  sharing weight of the expected investment profit.


\begin{defn} \label{Def-mudharabah-cfair} 
 A $T$-term viable  {\em PL sharing - mudharabah} contract on a asset $R$  is said to be $(c_1,c_2)$-{\em fair} if \begin{equation}  \label{Eq-Def-mudharabah-c-fair}
  c_1\, \mbox{Pay}_{1} (\gamma_1)  =   c_2\,  \mbox{Pay}_{2} (\gamma_2).
\end{equation}
\end{defn}


\begin{prop}  \label{Prop-Ratios-Profit-Sharing} In a $(c_1,c_2)$-fair {\em PL sharing - mudharabah} contract, the funding owner and the worker profit  ratios are given by
\begin{eqnarray} 
 \gamma_1   &=&  \frac{1}{c_1+c_2}  \big(c_2 + \rho(R_T,L) \, c_1 \big) \nonumber  \\
 & = & \frac{c_2}{c_1+c_2}  \big(1 - \rho(R_T,L)  \big)  +  \rho(R_T,L) ,   \label{Eq-fair-ratio-c-fair1} \\
 \gamma_2  & = & \frac{c_1}{c_1+c_2}  \big(1 - \rho(R_T,L)  \big).   \label{Eq-fair-ratio-c-fair2}
 \end{eqnarray}
Moreover, their respective expected payoff are given by 
\begin{equation} \label{Eq-Sharing-Weights-mudharabah}
 \frac{c_2}{c_1+c_2}\, \Delta(R_T,L) \quad \mbox{ and  } \qquad   \frac{c_1}{c_1+c_2}\, \Delta(R_T,L),
\end{equation}
meaning that they will share the {\em expected investment profit}  $\Delta(R_T,L)$ with the respective weights $ c_2 \, / (c_1+c_2)$ and $ c_1 \, / (c_1+c_2)$.
\end{prop}

\begin{proof} Keeping in mind that $\gamma_1+\gamma_2=1$,  we  get \eqref{Eq-fair-ratio-c-fair1}-\eqref{Eq-fair-ratio-c-fair2}  by solving Equation \eqref{Eq-Def-mudharabah-c-fair}.  The expected payoff $\mbox{Pay}_{1} (\gamma_1)$ and $\mbox{Pay}_{2} (\gamma_2)$ of the capital owner  and the manager are obtained by replacing $\gamma_1$ and $\gamma_2$ by their values in \eqref{Eq-fair-ratio-c-fair1}-\eqref{Eq-fair-ratio-c-fair2}. 
\end{proof}


\begin{example} In this example we fix  a value of $\rho(R_T,L) \in \{ \frac{1}{4}, \frac{1}{2}\}$ and aim to find the $(c_1,c_2)$-fair profit sharing ratios in a {\em PL sharing - mudharabah} contract (between a bank and a firm, for example).  
\smallskip

\noindent 
$\diamond$\, Suppose we want to choose  the profit sharing ratios  such that the expected  payoff at maturity pays off the bank  $\frac{3}{2}$ times than it pays off the company, so that  we may set $c_1=2$ and $c_2=3$. In this case,  the $(c_1,c_2)$-fair profit sharing ratios are given as follows:
\begin{eqnarray*}
& & \mbox{ when }  \ \rho(R_T,L) = \frac{1}{4}, \ \mbox{ then}, \   \gamma_1  = 70\%   \quad  \mbox{ and } \quad \gamma_2  = 30\%,    \\
&\mbox{ and } &  \mbox{ when }  \ \rho(R_T,L) = \frac{1}{2}, \  \mbox{ then}, \  \gamma_1  = 80\%   \quad  \mbox{ and } \quad \gamma_2  = 20\%.  
\end{eqnarray*}
With the previous choice of the ratios $\gamma_1$ and $\gamma_2$, we expect a  sharing of the anticipated profit  $\Delta(R_T,L)$ with the respective weighted shares $(c_2 \, / (c_1+c_2), c_1 \, / (c_1+c_2)) = (3 /5, 2/5)$. 
\smallskip

\noindent 
$\diamond$\, Now,  for a fair (when $(c_1,c_2) = (1,1)$) profit sharing, we get 
\begin{eqnarray*}
& & \mbox{ when }  \ \rho(R_T,L) = \frac{1}{4}, \ \mbox{ then}, \   \gamma_1  = 62.5\%   \quad  \mbox{ and } \quad \gamma_2  = 37.5\%,    \\
&\mbox{ and } &  \mbox{ when }  \ \rho(R_T,L) = \frac{1}{2}, \  \mbox{ then}, \  \gamma_1  = 75\%   \quad  \mbox{ and } \quad \gamma_2  = 25\%.  
\end{eqnarray*}

This leads to a equal  sharing of the anticipated expected profit  $\Delta(R_T,L)$.
\smallskip

\noindent 
$\diamond$  \,Finally, when we want, because of several extra services developed by the company to ease the management and the tracking of the project for example,  to choose the profit sharing ratios  such that the bank may expect to be paid off at maturity $2/3$ times than  the company may be expecting (we may set in this case  $c_1=3$ and $c_2=2$) then
\begin{eqnarray*}
& & \mbox{ when }  \ \rho(R_T,L) = \frac{1}{4}, \ \mbox{ then}, \   \gamma_1  = 55\%   \quad  \mbox{ and } \quad \gamma_2  = 45\%,    \\
&\mbox{ and } &  \mbox{ when }  \ \rho(R_T,L) = \frac{1}{2}, \  \mbox{ then}, \  \gamma_1  = 70\%   \quad  \mbox{ and } \quad \gamma_2  = 30\%.  
\end{eqnarray*}
Choosing these  profit sharing ratios ($\gamma_1$ still be for the bank  and $\gamma_2$ for the company) leads to a  sharing of the anticipated expected profit  $\Delta(R_T,L)$ with the respective weighted shares $(c_2 \, / (c_1+c_2), c_1 \, / (c_1+c_2)) = (2 /5, 3/5)$. 
\end{example} 

As discussed previously, we see from this example that the profit sharing ratios depends on the exposure on the risk $ \rho(R_T,L)$. Recall that the investor is more exposed to the risk than the funding manager  due to the fact that he will have to support the losses, if any, even if in return the worker (or the manager) will also lose the fruits of his labor. The   exposure of the investor on the (investment) risk  is compensated  by its profit ratio share and, the risky is the investment, the high will be its profit ratio. 


