 \section{The PL sharing - musharakah contract}
 A {\em musharakah} contract is a partnership between two or several partners to combine their assets, their labour, or their liabilities in order to make profits. The share contribution of each partner to the  capital must be determined in advance. The profit  sharing ratios  are determined according to an agreement between the partners and may be amended at any time before the maturity. However, losses are absorbed by all the partners, proportionally to their contribution to the capital.  Our aim in this section is to determine the $c$-fair profit sharing ratios between the partners of a {\em PL sharing - musharakah} contract.


  \subsection{The $c$-Fair profit  sharing ratios}  

 Suppose that we have two partners that contributed to the amounts $L_1$ and $L_2$ of the capital $L$ so that their respective share contributions to the capital are $\kappa_1 = L_1/L$ and $\kappa_2 = L_2/L$.  Denote by $\mbox{Pay}_{1} (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)$ and by $\mbox{Pay}_2(\gamma_2,\kappa_2)$ the expected payoff of the partners. Then,  
  \begin{eqnarray*}
&&  \mbox{Pay}_1(\gamma_1,\kappa_1)  =  \gamma_{1} \,  \mathbb E (R_T-L)^{+}  - \kappa_{1}\, \mathbb E (L-R_T)^{+} \\
& \mbox{ and } &   \mbox{Pay}_2(\gamma_2,\kappa_2) = \gamma_{2} \,   \mathbb E (R_T-L)^{+}  - \kappa_{2}\,  \mathbb E (L-R_T)^{+},
 \end{eqnarray*}
 where  $\gamma_1$ and  $\gamma_2$ stand  respectively for the profit ratios of partner $1$ and partner $2$. Now, let us discuss on the choice of the fair profit sharing ratios $\gamma_1$ and $\gamma_2$ according to the type of management of  the {\em musharakah}  project.
 \begin{enumerate} [leftmargin=*,itemsep=0cm]
  \item If the partners manage  together the project with an equal contribution to the management, then, the difference on their global contributions on the project  lies in their share contributions $\kappa_1$ and $\kappa_2$  to the capital. It could be reasonable to choose the  fair  profit sharing ratios to be  $\gamma_1 = \kappa_1$ and $\gamma_2 = \kappa_2$ and in this case we have 
    \begin{eqnarray*}
&&\kappa_2\, \mbox{Pay}_1 (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)   =   \kappa_1\, \mbox{Pay}_2(\gamma_2,\kappa_2)\quad  \\
 &\mbox{or, equivalently, } &  \mbox{Pay}_1  (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)    =    c  \, \mbox{Pay}_2 (\gamma_2,\kappa_2), \ \mbox{with } c = \kappa_1/ \kappa_2.
   \end{eqnarray*}
   Now, one question of interest may be to determine the $(c_1, c_2)$-fair profit sharing ratios $\gamma_1$ and $\gamma_2$ heeding the contribution of partner $2$ to the management which is such that one may reasonably consider: 
     \begin{eqnarray*}
&&c_1\, \mbox{Pay}_1 (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)   =   c_2\, \mbox{Pay}_2(\gamma_2,\kappa_2)\quad  \\
 &\mbox{or, equivalently, } &  \mbox{Pay}_1  (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)    =    c  \, \mbox{Pay}_2 (\gamma_2,\kappa_2), \ \mbox{with } c = c_2/ c_1.
   \end{eqnarray*}
   We have to solve the system of equations
   \begin{equation}  \label{Eq-Example-Musharac1c2}
	   \left\{
	 \begin{array}{lcl}
	 	c_1\, \mbox{Pay}_1 (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)   =   c_2\, \mbox{Pay}_2(\gamma_2,\kappa_2) \\
		\gamma_1 + \gamma_2 =1, \ \kappa_1 + \kappa_2=1,
	\end{array}
	\right.  
\end{equation}
which solutions reads (setting  $\bar{\ell} = \{1,2\} \backslash \{ \ell \}$)
\begin{equation} \label{EqPart1-2}
\gamma_{\ell} = \frac{c_{\bar{\ell}}}{c_1+c_2} - \left( \frac{c_{\bar{\ell}}}{c_1 + c_2}  - \kappa_{\ell} \right)\rho(R_T,L), \qquad \ell =1, 2.
\end{equation}
%The {\em PL sharing - mudharabah} contract appears then to be a particular case of the  {\em PL sharing - musharakah} contract  where the share contribution of one of the partners (say, partner $2$, considered as the worker) is null. In fact, putting $\kappa_1 =1 $ and $\kappa_2=0$ in Equation \eqref{EqPart1-2} leads to equations \eqref{Eq-fair-ratio-c-fair1}-\eqref{Eq-fair-ratio-c-fair2}. 

 \item Suppose that  they agree to appoint one of them (say, Partner $2$) to manage the project. In this case, it is not allowed to specify a fixed remuneration  for the manager whom is at the same time one of the partners of the {\em musharakah} contract. It is however possible to reward his extra contribution to the management of the project by increasing his profit sharing ratio.  As previously,  we have to  determine the $(c_1, c_2)$-fair profit sharing ratios $\gamma_1$ and $\gamma_2$ by choosing the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ so that to  offset the extra task of partner $2$.  This amounts to solve a system of equations  like  \eqref{Eq-Example-Musharac1c2} which solution is given by \eqref{EqPart1-2}. 


 \item  If they appoint a manager outside of them, there is several way of compensated him.
  \begin{enumerate} [leftmargin=*,itemsep=0cm]
 \item[$\diamond$]   They may  be bounded to the manager by a {\em wakalah} contract, arranging to pay him a fixed remuneration (a amount $p$ that is paid out  $k$ times up to the maturity $T$) that will be included in their expenses.  In this case,  supposing that their is a ($T$-period) discounted rate $r$ of return that could be earned from  the investment  on some good,  the actualised expected payoff  $\mbox{Pay}_{1} (\gamma_1,\kappa_1,p)$ and $\mbox{Pay}_2(\gamma_2,\kappa_2,p)$ of the partners of the {\em PL sharing - musharakah} contract  satisfy respectively:
   \begin{eqnarray*}
&&  \mbox{Pay}_1 (\gamma_1,\kappa_1,p)  \, = \,  (1+r)^{\mbox{\tiny $-T$}}\, \big(\gamma_{1} \,  \mathbb E (R_T-L)^{+}  - \kappa_{1}\, \mathbb E (L-R_T)^{+} \big)  - \frac{\mbox{Pay}_3 (p,k)}{ 2} \\
 &&  \mbox{Pay}_2 (\gamma_2,\kappa_2,p)  \,  = \,  (1+r)^{\mbox{\tiny $-T$}}\, \big(  \gamma_{2} \,  \mathbb E (R_T-L)^{+}  - \kappa_{2}\, \mathbb E (L-R_T)^{+}\big)  - \frac{\mbox{Pay}_3 (p,k)}{ 2},
% &&  \qquad \ \  \   \mbox{Pay}_3 (p,k)  \,  = \,  k\,  p. 
   \end{eqnarray*}
 where  $\mbox{Pay}_3(p,k)$ is the (actualised expected) payoff of  partner 3 (which is supposed to be the manager). The quantity  $\mbox{Pay}_3(p,k)$ is  the present value of  the sum of the future cash flow $p$ up to the maturity $T$ of the contract.  So, we have
  \[
  \mbox{Pay}_3(p,k)  = \sum_{i = 1}^k p\, (1+r)^{-{\footnotesize \frac{T}{k}} i }  = p  \, \big(1+r \big)^{ \mbox{\tiny $-T$} }  \, \frac{(1+r )^{ \mbox{\tiny $T$} } - 1  }{(1+r )^{ \mbox{\tiny $\frac{T}{k}$} } -1} \, .
  \]
  As in the previous cases, our aim is to determine the $(c_1, c_2,c_3)$-fair profit sharing ratios in a viable {\em PL sharing - musharakah} contract. In other terms, we want to compute the periodic remuneration $p$ up to maturity and the profit sharing ratios $\gamma_1$ and  $\gamma_2$ satisfying
  \begin{equation}  \label{Eq-example-mush-c1c2c3}
    \left\{
	 \begin{array}{lcl}
	 	 c_1 \,  \mbox{Pay}_1 (\gamma_1,\kappa_1,p)  = c_2\,  \mbox{Pay}_2 (\gamma_2,\kappa_2,p)  = c_3\,  \mbox{Pay}_3 (p,k) \\
		\gamma_1 + \gamma_2 =1.
	\end{array}
	\right.  
  \end{equation}
  If we want  to determine first $p$, it is suitable to rewrite the system \eqref{Eq-example-mush-c1c2c3} as a system of three equations with three unknown: 
  \begin{equation}  \label{Eq-example-mush-c1c2c3-rewrite}
   \left\{
	 \begin{array}{lcl}
	 	 c_1\, c_2 \,  \mbox{Pay}_1 (\gamma_1,\kappa_1,p)  = c_2\,c_3\,  \mbox{Pay}_3 (p,k) \\
		  c_1\, c_2 \,  \mbox{Pay}_2 (\gamma_2,\kappa_2,p)  = c_1\,c_3\,  \mbox{Pay}_3 (p,k) \\
		\gamma_1 + \gamma_2 =1.
	\end{array}
	\right.  
  \end{equation}
Solving the system \eqref{Eq-example-mush-c1c2c3-rewrite} leads to (setting $  \varpi(r,T,k) =  ( (1+r )^{ \mbox{\tiny $\frac{T}{k}$} } -1) \, / \, ( (1+r )^{ \mbox{\tiny $T$} } - 1) $)
\begin{equation}
 \varpi(r,T,k)\,  p =    c_{3, \bar {3}} \, \Delta(R_T,L) ,
 % &=& \frac{c_1 c_2}{(c_1 c_2 + c_1 c_3 + c_2 c_3) k}   \,   \big( \mathbb E (R_T)-L\big), 
  \end{equation}
  with  $  c_{3, \bar {3}}  = c_1 c_2 \, / \, (c_1 c_2 + c_1 c_3 + c_2 c_3) $. Then, replacing $p$ by its value in the two first equations gives,  for $\ell \in {\{1,2 \} }$,
  \begin{equation} 
 % \gamma_{\ell}  = c_{\ell, \bar {\ell}}  - \big (c_{\ell, \bar {\ell}}  - \kappa_{\ell}  \big)  \rho(R_T,L)
  \gamma_{\ell}  = \Big( \frac{c_{3, \bar 3}}{2} + c_{\ell,\bar{\ell}}\Big) \,  \big(1-\rho(R_T,L) \big)   + \kappa_{\ell} \, \rho(R_T,L), 
\end{equation}
  where
  \begin{equation}  
 \big(c_{1, \bar {1}},\, c_{2, \bar {2}}\big) = \frac{1}{ c_1 c_2 + c_1 c_3 + c_2 c_3} \, \big(c_{2} c_3, \,c_1 c_3 \big).
\end{equation}
%   $\bar{\ell} = \{1,2\} \backslash \{ \ell \}$ and 
%  \[
%  c_{\ell,\bar {\ell}}  =   \frac{c_{ \bar{\ell} }}{c_1 c_2 + c_1 c_3 + c_2 c_3}  \Big( \frac{c_{\ell}}{2}  + c_3\Big).
%  \]
 % \textcolor{red}{with the constraint that $c_{\ell} > 2 c_{3}$, for $\ell \in \{1, 2\}$.}
   \item[$\diamond$]   They may also be bounded to the manager (the partner 3) by a {\em PL-sharing mudharabah} contract. In this case, the expected payoffs  $\mbox{Pay}_{1} (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)$, $\mbox{Pay}_2(\gamma_2,\kappa_2)$ and $\mbox{Pay}_3(\gamma_3)$ of the partners are given respectively by:
   \begin{eqnarray*}
&& \mbox{Pay}_1 (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)  \, = \,  \gamma_{1} \,  \mathbb E (R_T-L)^{+}  - \kappa_{1}\, \mathbb E (L-R_T)^{+}   \\
 &&  \mbox{Pay}_2 (\gamma_2,\kappa_2)  \,  = \,   \gamma_{2} \,  \mathbb E (R_T-L)^{+}  - \kappa_{2}\, \mathbb E (L-R_T)^{+}   \\
 && \quad \, \  \mbox{Pay}_3 (\gamma_3)  \,  = \,   \gamma_{3} \,  \mathbb E (R_T-L)^{+}. 
   \end{eqnarray*}
 Finding the $(c_1,c_2,c_3)$-fair profit sharing ratio amounts to solve the system
  \begin{equation}  \label{Eq-example-mush-mudharabah-c1c2c3}
    \left\{
	 \begin{array}{lcl}
	 	 c_1 \,  \mbox{Pay}_1 (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)  = c_2\,  \mbox{Pay}_2 (\gamma_2,\kappa_2)  = c_3 \,  \mbox{Pay}_1 (\gamma_3,\kappa_3)  \\
		 \hspace{0.32cm}   \gamma_1 + \gamma_2 + \gamma_3=1.
	\end{array}
	\right.  
  \end{equation}
  To compute $\gamma_1$,  we consider the following equivalent system:
   \begin{equation*}  \label{Eq-example-mush-mudaraba-c1c2c3gamma1}
   \left\{
	 \begin{array}{lcl}
	 	 c_1 \,  c_2\, c_3\, \mbox{Pay}_1 (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)   = c_1 \,  c_2\, c_3\,  \mbox{Pay}_1 (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)  \\
		  c_1^2 \,  c_3\, \mbox{Pay}_1 (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)   = c_1 \,  c_2\, c_3\,  \mbox{Pay}_2 (\gamma_2,\kappa_2) \\
		 c_1^2 \,  c_2\,  \mbox{Pay}_1 (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)    = c_1 \,  c_2\, c_3\, \mbox{Pay}_3 (\gamma_3) \\
		   \gamma_1 + \gamma_2 +\gamma_3=1.
	\end{array}
	\right.  
  \end{equation*}
 Now, adding the left hand side and then, the right hand side of the three first equations of the previous system gives 
 \begin{equation*}
 (c_2 c_3 + c_1 c_3 + c_1c_2)\, \mbox{Pay}_1 (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)   = c_2\, c_3 \big(\mathbb E (R_T-L)^{+}   - \mathbb E (L-R_T)^{+}\big).
 \end{equation*}
 It follows that
\begin{equation}
     \gamma_1  = \frac{c_2c_3}{c_2 c_3 + c_1 c_3 + c_1c_2 }  -  \left ( \frac{c_2c_3}{c_2 c_3 + c_1 c_3 + c_1c_2 }   - \kappa_{1}  \right)  \rho(R_T,L).
\end{equation}
The ratio $\gamma_2$ is obtained by solving the equivalent system
  \begin{equation*}  \label{Eq-example-mush-mudaraba-c1c2c3gamma1}
   \left\{
	 \begin{array}{lcl}
	 	 c_2^2 \,   c_3\, \mbox{Pay}_2 (\gamma_2,\kappa_2)   = c_1 \,  c_2\, c_3\,  \mbox{Pay}_1 (\gamma_1,\kappa_1)  \\
		  c_1 \, c_2 \,  c_3\, \mbox{Pay}_2 (\gamma_1,\kappa_2)   = c_1 \,  c_2\, c_3\,  \mbox{Pay}_2 (\gamma_2,\kappa_2) \\
		 c_2^2 \,  c_2\,  \mbox{Pay}_2 (\gamma_2,\kappa_2)    = c_1 \,  c_2\, c_3\, \mbox{Pay}_3 (\gamma_3) \\
		   \gamma_1 + \gamma_2 +\gamma_3=1, 
	\end{array}
	\right.  
  \end{equation*}
which solution is given by
\begin{equation}
     \gamma_2  = \frac{c_1c_3}{c_2 c_3 + c_1 c_3 + c_1c_2 }  -  \left ( \frac{c_1c_3}{c_2 c_3 + c_1 c_3 + c_1c_2 }   - \kappa_{2}  \right)  \rho(R_T,L).
\end{equation}
Using the condition  $  \gamma_1 + \gamma_2 +\gamma_3=1$ yields 
\begin{equation}
     \gamma_3  = \frac{c_1c_2}{c_2 c_3 + c_1 c_3 + c_1c_2 }  \Big( 1 -    \rho(R_T,L) \Big).
\end{equation}

 \end{enumerate}
 \end{enumerate}
 All the results of the previous discussions is summarised in the following proposition.

 \begin{prop}  \label{Prop-Musharakah-d-Partners2Parts} Consider  a  viable   $T$-term and $R$-asset based  {\em  PL - musharakah} contract of  $2$ partners. Then:
 \begin{enumerate} [leftmargin=*,itemsep=0cm]
 \item If they manage themselves the project  and agree, according to their contribution to the funding and the management of the project,  to rate their respective payoff by the coefficients $c_1$ and $c_2$, then, the $(c_1,c_2)$-fair profit sharing ratios are given by (we set  $\bar{\ell} = \{1,2\} \backslash \{ \ell \}$)
 \begin{eqnarray}
\gamma_{\ell} &=&  c_{\ell, \bar {\ell}} \,  ( 1- \rho(R_T,L) ) +  \kappa_{\ell} \,  \rho(R_T,L),\\
&=&   c_{\ell, \bar {\ell}}   \, \beta(R_T,L) +  \kappa_{\ell} \,  \rho(R_T,L), \qquad \ell =1, 2,  \label{EqPart1-2-Prop}
\end{eqnarray}
where, for every $\ell \in \{1, 2\}$, \,  $c_{\ell, \bar {\ell}} =  c_{\bar{\ell}} \, / \,  (c_1+c_2).$   Furthermore, the expected payoff of each partner $\ell$  is given by
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Pay}_{\ell} (\gamma_{\ell},\kappa_{\ell})  = c_{\ell, \bar {\ell}}\, \Delta(R_T,L),  \qquad \ell =1, 2.
\end{equation}
\item  If they appoint a manager outside of them and are bounded with him by a {\em Wakalah} contract which arranges a fixed remuneration (a amount $p$ that is paid out  $k$ times up to the maturity $T$) and if they agree (Partner 1, Partner 2 and the manager) to rate their respective payoff by the coefficients $c_1$, $c_2$ and $c_3$, then, the $(c_1,c_2,c_3)$-fair profit sharing  ratios and periodic remuneration $p$ satisfy:
\begin{eqnarray}  
  \varpi(r,T,k) \, p &=&    c_{3,\bar {3}}    \, \Delta(R_T,L)\\
 \gamma_{\ell}  &=& \Big( \frac{c_{3, \bar 3}}{2} + c_{\ell,\bar{\ell}}\Big) \,  \big(1-\rho(R_T,L) \big)   + \kappa_{\ell} \, \rho(R_T,L), \,  \ell \in \{1, 2\},   
\end{eqnarray}
where $\varpi(r,T,k) =  ((1+r )^{ \mbox{\tiny $\frac{T}{k}$} } -1 ) \, / \, ( (1+r )^{ \mbox{\tiny $T$} } - 1)$ and  the weights  $(c_{\ell, \bar {\ell}})_{\ell \in \{1, 2, 3 \}}$ are given by
\begin{equation}  \label{Eq-Def-c-ell-bar-ell}
 \big(c_{1, \bar {1}},\, c_{2, \bar {2}},\, c_{3, \bar {3}} \big) = \frac{1}{ c_1 c_2 + c_1 c_3 + c_2 c_3} \, \big(c_{2} c_3, \,c_1 c_3, \,c_1 c_2 \big).
\end{equation}
 Moreover, the expected payoff of each partner $\ell$ (even the partner $3$ who has a fixed $k$-periodic remuneration up to maturity) is given by
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Pay}_{\ell} (\gamma_{\ell},\kappa_{\ell})  = c_{\ell, \bar {\ell}}\, \Delta(R_T,L),  \qquad \ell \in \{1, 2,3\}.
\end{equation}
\item   If they appoint a manager (Partner  3) outside of them and  are bounded to him by a {\em PL-sharing mudharabah} contract, the $(c_1,c_2,c_3)$-fair profit sharing ratios are given  
\begin{enumerate} [leftmargin=*,itemsep=0cm]
\item[$\diamond$] for the funding owners (Partner 1 and Partner 2), by
\begin{equation}
     \gamma_{\ell}  = \frac{c_3 c_{\bar {\ell}}}{c_2 c_3 + c_1 c_3 + c_1c_2 } \big(1  - \rho(R_T,L) \big)  + \kappa_{\ell}  \, \rho(R_T,L) ,
\end{equation}
for  any $\ell \in \{1,2\}$ and where $\bar{\ell} = \{1,2\} \backslash \{ \ell \}$,
\item[$\diamond$] for the manager, by
\begin{equation}  \label{EqMush-Mudhar}
     \gamma_3  = \frac{c_1c_2}{c_2 c_3 + c_1 c_3 + c_1c_2 }  \big( 1 -    \rho(R_T,L) \big).
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
Still, the expected payoff of each partner $\ell$  is given by
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Pay}_{\ell} (\gamma_{\ell},\kappa_{\ell})  = c_{\ell, \bar {\ell}}\, \Delta(R_T,L),  \qquad \ell \in \{1, 2,3\}.
\end{equation}
where the $c_{\ell,\bar{\ell}}$'s are defined in \eqref{Eq-Def-c-ell-bar-ell}.
 \end{enumerate}
\end{prop}

\begin{rem}   It is important to remark that putting $\kappa_1=1$ and $\kappa_2=0$ in Equation \eqref{EqPart1-2-Prop} leads to the $(c_1,c_2)$-fair profit sharing ratios obtained in Equation \eqref{Eq-fair-ratio-c-fair1} and \eqref{Eq-fair-ratio-c-fair2} for a {\em PL-sharing mudharabah } contract between two partners. Consequently, a  {\em PL-sharing mudharabah} contract between two partners is a {\em PL-sharing musharakah} contract where  of the owner of the capital (the partner 1) brings the whole capital (so that $\kappa_1=1$) and the other, the labor ($\kappa_2=0$). Remark also that   $\gamma_3$ in Equation \eqref{EqMush-Mudhar} may be written as
\begin{equation}
     \gamma_{3}  = \frac{c_1 c_2}{c_2 c_3 + c_1 c_3 + c_1c_2 }  \big(1  - \rho(R_T,L) \big)  +  \kappa_{3}  \,  \rho(R_T,L),
\end{equation}
with $\kappa_3=0$. Additional discussions on the results of Proposition \ref{Prop-Musharakah-d-Partners2Parts} are made in the discussions that follow the proposition   \ref{Prop-Musharakah-d-PartnersdParts}. 
%We may also seen that in the case where
