\begin{rem}[{\em on perspectives}]  \label{Rem-Perspectives} We may note that all the challenge in  our approach  is the computation of the quantities $\rho(R_T,L)$ and $\Delta(R_T,L)$ given the dynamics of $R$. For example, we may model the dynamics of $R$ as  (the Black-Scholes model)
\begin{equation} \label{EqB-S}
dR_{t}  = \mu R_t \,dt  + \sigma  R_t \,dW_t, \qquad  R_0 = L, \quad t \in [0,T],
\end{equation}
where $W$ is a brownian motion defined on $(\Omega,{\cal F}, \mathbb P)$, $\mu \in \mathbb R$ and $\sigma (>0)$ are resp. the instantaneous return and  the instantaneous volatility parameters. In this case,  the solution of the stochastic differential equation \eqref{EqB-S} reads, for any $t \in [0,T]$ and  especially  for $t=T$,
\[
R_t  = L \exp\big ( (\mu - \sigma^2/2)t  + \sigma W_t \big),
\]
meaning in particular that the distribution of $R$ is log-normal.  Furthermore, we may show that an $R$ asset-based  investment  is viable if and only if $\mu > 0$ and that  $\rho(R_T,L)$ and $\Delta (R_T,L)$ are explicitly given by (setting  $ \vartheta(\mu, \sigma)  = (2\mu -\sigma^2)T\, / \, (2 \sigma \sqrt{T})$ and denoting $\Phi(\cdot)$ the c.d.f. of the standard normal distribution)
 \begin{eqnarray}
&&  \rho(R_T,L)  = -  \frac{\Phi(- \vartheta(\mu, \sigma)) - e^{\mu T} \Phi(- \vartheta(\mu, \sigma) - \sigma \sqrt{T})}{\Phi(\vartheta(\mu, \sigma)) - e^{\mu T} \Phi(\vartheta(\mu, \sigma) + \sigma \sqrt{T})} \\
 &\mbox{ and }& \Delta (R_T,L)  = L (e^{\mu T} -1).
 \end{eqnarray}
 Even if these previous quantities may somewhere be considered as some benchmarks values (that typically must be closed to  what we may expect from the use of  some more general models in a lower volatility framework),  it is however  well known that most of the financial data set distribution are not log-normal. Then, one of the challenging tasks is to use all the range of econometrics  models (like the  $\mbox{ARIMA}$ or the $\mbox{(G)ARCH}$ model) or more general stochastic diffusion  models (like  local volatility or stochastic volatility models) to find the appropriate model  for $R$,  given an asset data set.  
 \end{rem}
%\bigskip

\noindent \textbf{\em Conclusion remark}. The present  paper  introduces and studies  the notion of $c$-fair profit sharing ratios for islamic profit and loss  sharing contracts, possibly combined   with an agency contract.  We show that the $c$-fair profit sharing ratio is decomposed as a reword from the funding of the capital and a reward from the labor or from the management of the project. Although the  profit sharing ratios is determined with a common agreement between the partners of PL contracts, this work may help the islamic finance institutions, first,  understanding its link with several key economic factors  as: the expected investment profit, the investment risk, the investment opportunity, etc, and, secondly,  the present results may also  help them taking informed decisions about the profit sharing ratios and the  related parameters.  Furthermore,  the work open  new perspectives in the  (dynamic) stochastic  modelling and  their  applications to   islamic finance engineering. 

