\section{Mean displacement per unit time} 
\label{app:u}

In Fig. \ref{fig:v100} we have defined the chemotactic drift velocity as mean displacement in a run, divided by mean run duration. Another simpler definition of drift velocity is mean displacement per unit time. In steady state we observe the system for a time interval $\cal T$ (much longer than typical run duration) and measure the displacement in that interval. We average over many such intervals to calculate the mean displacement. After dividing this mean displacement by $\cal T$ we get another measure of drift velocity, which we denote as $U$. In Fig. \ref{fig:u} we plot $U$ as a function of wave speed and find the same behavior as $V$. These data are for $\lambda = 200 \mu m$. 
