%
\subsection{Propagation of chaos}
\label{subsec:prop_chaos}

The well-posedness, i.e. existence and uniqueness of a strong solution, together with moment bounds, of the multi-species interacting particle system follows from standard results on SDEs, e.g.\cite[Thm. 10.2.2]{SV79} or~\cite[Thm. 3.1.1]{Rockner2007}. We note that Eqn~\eqref{eq: many SDEs} is an Itô diffusion on $\bRb^{dN_n}$, with appropriate growth conditions on the drift and, therefore, standard SDE theory applies.

We now state a standard propagation of chaos result that provides us with the link between the interacting particle system~\eqref{eq: many SDEs} and the multi-species McKean SDE~\eqref{eq: mean-field SDE}.

\begin{theorem}[Propagation of chaos]
    \label{theo: PoC}
Under the same assumption as in Theorem \ref{thm: well-posedness}, if moreover $X_0^{i,1}=X_0^i$ and the two SDEs are driven by the same Brownian motion, then for $T<\infty$, we have
\begin{equation}
\label{eq: E-sup}
\lim\limits_{n\to\infty}\E\Big[\sup\limits_{t\in[0,T]}\big(X_t^{i,1}-\overline{X_t^{i}}\big)^2\Big]=0\quad \text{for}\quad i=1,\ldots, M.
\end{equation}
%where $\widehat{X}_t^{i}$ $i=1,\ldots, M$ is a solution to the following system
%\begin{equation}
% \dd\widehat{X}^{i}_t=-\nabla V_i(\widehat{X}^{i}_t)\,\ddt-\sum_{j=1}^M (a_\ell\nabla F_{ij}\ast \mu^{j}_t)(\widehat{X}^{i}_t) \,\ddt+\sigma_i \dd W^i_t.    
%\end{equation}
%with $(W^i_t)$ $i=1,\ldots, M$ being independent Wiener processes. Note that $\{\widehat{X}_t^i\}_{i=1}^{N_n}$ are identically independent copies of $X^i_t$ .
\end{theorem}

The proof of the latter theorem is similar to~\cite[Thm. 1.2]{DuongTugaut2020}. This result can be extended to the setting considered in this paper, due to Assumptions \ref{asp: assumption} and \ref{asp: assumptionbis}. %The We point out that this theorem is applicable due to the assumptions taken in force in our work. 
It is also possible to obtain quantitative propagation of chaos estimates, along the lines of what is done in~\cite{cattiaux2008,lacker2023sharp}. This will be presented elsewhere.

