\subsection{Connections to multi-player game theory}
We can explain why we need the symmetry condition on the interaction energies by connecting to the theory of multi-player (differentiable) potential games \cite{monderer1996potential}. We first briefly review this theory. A $M$-player differentiable game consists of $M$ twice continuously differentiable loss (unity) functions
\begin{align*}
u_i: D_1\times\ldots D_M&\rightarrow \mathbb{R}\\
    \mathbf{s}=(s_1,\ldots, s_M)&\mapsto u_i(\mathbf{s}).
\end{align*}
where $s_i\in D_i$, which is the set of possible strategies of $i$-th player, $i=1,\ldots, M$, whose aims to minimise their own unity (loss) function $u_i$, which depends not only on their own strategy $s_i$, but also on the strategies of other players:
\[
s_i^*=\mathrm{argmin}_{s_i\in D_i} u_i(\mathbf{s}).
\]
The simultaneous gradient is defined as
\[
\xi(\mathbf{s})=(\nabla_{s_i}u_i(\mathbf{s}),\ldots, \nabla_{s_M}u_M(\mathbf{s})).
\]
A fixed point (Nash stationary point) $\mathbf{s^*}=(s_1^*,\ldots, s_M^*)$ satisfies
\[
\xi(\mathbf{s}^*)=0.
\]
A game is called a potential game if and only if there exist a (potential) function $u: D_1\times \ldots\times D_M$ and positive numbers $\{\pi_i\}_{i=1}^M$ such that
\[
\pi_i \nabla_{s_i}u_i(\mathbf{s})=\nabla_{s_i} u(\mathbf{s})\quad\text{for all}\quad i=1,\ldots, M.
\]
Under this condition, the simultaneous gradient can be expressed via the gradient of the potential 
\[
\xi(\mathbf{s})=\Pi^{-1}\nabla u(\mathbf{s})\quad\text{where}\quad \Pi=\mathrm{diag}(\pi_1,\ldots, \pi_M).
\]
Thus in potential games, a fixed point is a critical point of the potential function; thus instead of minimising $M$ different functions, one just needs to minimise the potential function.
\begin{theorem}[\cite{monderer1996potential}]
A differentiable game is potential if and only if there
exist positive numbers (weights) $\{\pi_i\}_{i=1}^M\}$ such that
\[
\pi_i \nabla^2_{s_is_j} u_i(\mathbf{s})= \pi_j \nabla^2_{s_is_j} u_j(\mathbf{s}) \quad\text{for all}\quad i,j=1,\ldots, M.
\]
\end{theorem}
Going back to our problem. In a single-species model
\[
\partial_t \mu=\div[(\nabla U+\nabla F\ast \mu)\mu]+\beta^{-1}\nabla \mu,
\]
a stationary measure minimises the free energy functional
\[
F(\mu)=\int U \dd\mu+\frac{1}{2}\int F(x-y)\dd\mu(y)\dd\mu(x)+\beta^{-1}\int \mu\log\mu.
\]
In the multi-species model, the stationary conditions can be reformulated as
\[
\frac{\delta \mathcal{F}_i}{\delta \mu_i}(\boldsymbol{\mu})=0,\quad \text{for}\quad i=1,\ldots, M,
\]
where
\[
\mathcal{F}_i(\boldsymbol{\mu})=\int U_i \dd\mu_i+\sum_{j}a_j\int F_{ij}(x-y)\dd\mu_i(y)\dd\mu_j(x)+\beta^{-1}\int \mu_i\log\mu_i.
\]
We can view the multi-species at equilibrium as a multi-player game, where each species as a player with the loss functions $\{\mathcal{F}_i\}_{i=1}^M$. The above condition resembles the stationary condition for a fixed point in a multi-player game.  The condition for the existence of a single free energy functional is equivalent that the corresponding game is potential, that is there exist positive numbers $\{\pi_i\}_{i=1}^M$ such that
\[
\pi_i a_j\frac{\delta^2 \mathcal{F}_i}{\delta \mu_i\delta \mu_j}=\pi_j a_i\frac{\delta^2 \mathcal{F}_j}{\delta \mu_j\delta \mu_i},
\]
which is
\[
\pi_i a_j F_{ij}(x-y)=\pi_j a_i F_{ji}(y-x) \quad\text{for all}\quad i,j=1,\ldots, M.
\]
