\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
As scientific discovery increasingly relies on advanced technologies and instruments, vast amounts of data are being generated at an unprecedented rate. For example, the International Thermonuclear Experimental Reactor (ITER)~\cite{Iter}, a project designed to demonstrate fusion energy as a large-scale, carbon-free energy source, is expected to produce 2 petabytes of data daily starting in 2035. Similarly, large-scale simulations such as the Nyx cosmology code~\cite{Nyx}, used to simulate the evolution of matter in the universe, can generate several terabytes of data every hour on the fastest U.S. supercomputers. While much of this data is analyzed locally, it must often be transferred across wide-area networks (WANs) to facilitate global collaborations. This is particularly true for ITER, which, despite being located in France, enables scientists from around the world to conduct fusion experiments remotely. Afterward, the resulting data needs to be sent to distant researchers for further analysis, sometimes across continents. In cases requiring significant computational power, the data may need to be migrated to HPC systems, such as the U.S. Department of Energy’s supercomputers, to accelerate processing. These cross-facility workflows are not only vital for solving complex scientific problems but also contribute to the movement toward data democratization—the principle that scientific data should be accessible to a wide array of researchers, regardless of their geographic location, institutional affiliation, or available resources, fostering innovation through diverse perspectives. This underscores the critical importance of resilient, efficient, and timely data transmissions. 
%As more advanced technologies and equipment are heavily involved in scientific discovery, massive volume of scientific data is being generated at enormous speed in modern science. For instance, the International Thermonuclear Experimental Reactor (ITER) is an ambitious project designed to demonstrate the feasibility of fusion energy as a large-scale, carbon-free energy source. Upon entering the full-scale production phase in 2035, ITER is expected to produce 2 petabytes of experimental data on a daily basis~\cite{Iter}. In addition to experiments conducted on advanced  instruments like ITER, large-scale scientific simulations running on high-performance computing (HPC) systems also have the capability to produce huge amount of data within a short time frame. For example, the Nyx cosmology simulation code~\cite{Nyx}, which is used for simulating the evolution of baryonic matter and dark matter in the universe, can easily generate a few terabytes of simulation data every hour when running on the fastest supercomputers in the U.S.

