We develop an optimization model for \sys to minimize overall transmission time. The total size of the first $l$ levels is $S = S_1 + S_2 + \dots + S_l$, and the number of FTGs is $N = \frac{S}{(n - m) s}$. The time for initial transmission is $t + \frac{nN - 1}{r}$, and the expected retransmission time depends on the number of FTGs needing retransmission in each round.
When the packet loss rate is relatively low, 
%the likelihood of multiple fault-tolerant groups experiencing unrecoverable data losses during transmission is low. Consequently, 
unrecoverable data losses across FTGs can be modeled as independent events. Under this assumption, the number of FTGs with unrecoverable losses in a given round follows a binomial distribution. %This model enables the estimation of the number of fault-tolerant groups requiring retransmission in subsequent rounds, forming the basis for calculating the retransmission time for each round. 
For instance, if the number of FTGs transmitted in the initial round of transmission is $N$, and the probability of a single FTG experiencing unrecoverable data loss is $p$, the probability that exactly $K$ out of $N$ FTGs experience such losses is given by $\binom{N}{K}p^K(1-p)^{N-K}$. The expected number of FTGs that will need retransmission in the first round of retransmission is $\sum\limits_{K=0}^{N}K\binom{N}{K}p^K(1-p)^{N-K}=Np$. In general, the expected number of FTGs requiring retransmission in the $i$-th round of retransmission can be derived as $Np^i$. The likelihood of requiring 
%a round of retransmission expressed as the probability that at least one fault-tolerant group transmitted during that round experiences unrecoverable data loss. For 
the $i$-th round of retransmission corresponds to the probability that at least one FTG transmitted in that round experiences unrecoverable loss, which is given by $1-(1-p)^{Np^i}$. Using this, the expected total time for receiving all the data fragments across the required levels can be calculated as follows:
\begingroup
\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{3pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{3pt}
\begin{equation}
%\begin{split}
\label{ETtotal}
    E[T_{total}] = t+\frac{nN-1}{r}+\sum\limits_{i=1}^{\infty}[1-(1-p)^{Np^{i-1}}](t+\frac{nNp^i-1}{r}) %\\
    %=& t+\frac{\frac{nS}{(n-m)s}-1}{r}+\sum\limits_{i=1}^{\infty}[1-(1-p)^{\frac{Sp^{i-1}}{(n-m)s}}](t+\frac{\frac{nSp^i}{(n-m)s}-1}{r})
%\end{split}
\end{equation}
\endgroup
Since we always have $0<p<1$, when $i\to \infty$, $[1-(1-p)^{Np^{i-1}}] \to 0$ and $(t+\frac{nNp^i-1}{r}) \to t$. Consequently, the summation term in $E[T_{total}]$ diminishes, ensuring that $E[T_{total}]$ converges as $i\to \infty$.

