Now the key is to estimate $p$, the probability that the data fragments of an FTG cannot be recovered from the losses. %As shown in~\autoref{fig:transmission}, 
If each FTG has $n$ fragments and the transmission rate is $r$ fragments per second, sending $n$ fragments takes $n/r$ seconds. If the network latency for transmitting a single fragment from the sender to the receiver is $t$, the total time from when the first fragment of the FTG is sent to when the last fragment is received can be expressed as $T=t+(n-1)/r$. %During this period, $T$, all the $n$ fragments of this fault-tolerant group are in transit, and any of them may be lost due to packet loss. 
The total number of fragments sent during $T$ is 
\begingroup
\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{3pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{3pt}
\begin{equation}
    u = \frac{T}{1/r}=\frac{t+(n-1)/r}{1/r} = rt+n-1
\end{equation}
\endgroup
Assume the packet loss rate remains constant or exhibits minimal variation over short time intervals. Under this assumption, packet losses can be effectively modeled using a Poisson distribution. Specifically, the probability of exactly $j$ out of the $u$ fragments being lost within $T$ is given by:
\begingroup
\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{3pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{3pt}
\begin{equation}
Pr(v = j) = \frac{(\lambda T)^j e^{-\lambda T}}{j!} =\frac{{\lambda (t+\frac{n-1}{r})}^j e^{-\lambda (t+\frac{n-1}{r})}}{j!} 
\end{equation}
\endgroup
These $j$ lost fragments may belong to different FTGs.
If each FTG has $m$ parity fragments, given that there are $j$ ($j > m$) fragment losses during $T$, the probability that the lost data fragments in an FTG cannot be recovered is given by:
\begingroup
\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{3pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{3pt}
\begin{equation}
Pr(\text{unrecoverable}|v = j) = \frac{\sum\limits_{w=m+1}^{n}\binom{n}{w}\binom{u-n}{j-w}}{\binom{u}{j}}
\end{equation}
\endgroup
Therefore, the overall probability that the lost data fragments in an FTG cannot be recovered is given by:
\begingroup
\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{3pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{3pt}
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\label{p}
p=&\sum\limits_{j=m+1}^{u}Pr(\text{unrecoverable}|v = j)Pr(v = j) \\
=&\sum\limits_{j=m+1}^{u}\frac{\sum\limits_{w=m+1}^{n}\binom{n}{w}\binom{u-n}{j-w}}{\binom{u}{j}}\frac{{\lambda (t+\frac{n-1}{r})}^j e^{-\lambda (t+\frac{n-1}{r})}}{j!} \\
=&\sum\limits_{j=m+1}^{rt+n-1}\frac{\sum\limits_{w=m+1}^{n}\binom{n}{w}\binom{rt-1}{j-w}}{\binom{rt+n-1}{j}}\frac{{\lambda (t+\frac{n-1}{r})}^j e^{-\lambda (t+\frac{n-1}{r})}}{j!} 
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\endgroup

However, when the packet loss rate is high (e.g., when each FTG experiences more than one fragment loss on average) and number of parity fragments per FTG is small (e.g., $m=0$ or $m=1$), 
%the likelihood of multiple fault-tolerant groups within the same transmission or retransmission round simultaneously experiencing unrecoverable data losses increases significantly. This breaks the assumption of independence between fault-tolerant groups, meaning that 
unrecoverable data losses across groups become correlated, meaning multiple FTGs within $T$ are likely to experience unrecoverable losses simultaneously. This correlation invalidates the probability $p$ calculated using~\autoref{p}, rendering it unsuitable for use in~\autoref{ETtotal} to compute $E[T_{total}]$. To address this issue, we adopt an alternative approach to calculate $p$ that accounts for the correlations introduced by high packet loss rates. This refined calculation ensures that~\autoref{ETtotal} remains a reliable approximation for the total transmission time, even under conditions of elevated packet loss. %As shown in~\autoref{fig:transmission}, 
Given that $\frac{t+(n-1)/r}{n/r}$ FTGs are in transit during $T$, the average fragment losses each of these FTGs experiences is $\lambda T/(\frac{t+(n-1)/r}{n/r})=\frac{\lambda n}{r}$. An FTG experiences unrecoverable data loss only when more than $m$ fragments within it are lost. Therefore, 
%the assumption of independence between FTGs no longer holds. In such scenarios, setting $m=0$ or $m=1$ is not feasible, as nearly all FTGs would incur unrecoverable data fragment losses, leading to a large number of retransmissions. Consequently, only values of 
%$m>1$ are considered.
%If we assume the total fragment losses during $T$ are randomly distributed across these FTGs, 
the probability $p$ that an FTG experiences unrecoverable data losses can be calculated as follows:
\begingroup
\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{3pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{3pt}
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\label{p_big_lambda}
p =&Pr(\text{more than} \ m \ \text{fragment losses in an FTG}) \\
=&1-\sum\limits_{j=0}^{m}\frac{{(\frac{\lambda n}{r})}^j e^{-(\frac{\lambda n}{r})}}{j!} 
\end{split}
\end{equation} 
\endgroup

In summary, the following optimization problem needs to be solved:
\begingroup
\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{3pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{3pt}
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\label{ETotal_opt}
\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{ m \in \{0,\ldots,n/2\} } &\quad  t+\frac{nN-1}{r}+\sum\limits_{i=1}^{\infty}[1-(1-p)^{Np^{i-1}}](t+\frac{nNp^i-1}{r}) \\
\text{s.t.}& \quad \text{Calculate $p$ using~\autoref{p_big_lambda}, if $\frac{\lambda n}{r}>1$}   \\
& \quad \text{Calculate $p$ using~\autoref{p}, otherwise}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\endgroup
No matter which equation is used to calculate $p$, increasing $m$ has two opposing effects on $E[T_{total}]$: 1) As $N=\frac{S}{(n-m)s}$, a larger $m$ increases the number of FTGs, leading to more fragments transmitted during both the initial transfer and potential retransmissions, thus increasing  $E[T_{total}]$. 2) A larger $m$ reduces the probability $p$ of unrecoverable data loss within an FTG, decreasing the likelihood of retransmissions and mitigating the increase in $E[T_{total}]$ caused by a higher $N$. As a result, an optimal value of $m$ exists that minimizes the expected total transmission time $E[T_{total}]$. In practice, since $p$ is relatively small, $E[T_{total}]$ usually converges for $i > 50$. Given that $m$ is an integer and $m \leq n/2$ (as the number of parity fragments must not exceed the number of data fragments), finding the optimal $m$ that minimizes $E[T_{total}]$ is computationally straightforward. This can be achieved through an exhaustive search or a lightweight numerical optimization approach.

%Furthermore, increasing $m$ (the number of parity fragments) has two opposing effects on $E[T_{total}]$: 1) Since $N=\frac{S}{(n-m)s}$, increasing $m$ results in a larger number of fault-tolerant groups,  which increases the total number of fragments to be transmitted during both the initial transmission and potential retransmissions, thereby increasing  $E[T_{total}]$. 2) A larger $m$ reduces the probability $p$ of unrecoverable data loss within a fault-tolerant group, decreasing the likelihood and frequency of retransmissions. This reduction in retransmissions mitigates the increase in $E[T_{total}]$ caused by a higher $N$. As a result, an optimal value of $m$ exists that minimizes the expected total transmission time $E[T_{total}]$. In practice, since $p$ is relatively small, $E[T_{total}]$ usually converges for $i > 20$. Given that $m$ and its valid range is $[0,31]$, finding the optimal $m$ that minimizes $E[T_{total}]$ is computationally straightforward. This can be achieved through an exhaustive search over the discrete range or a lightweight numerical optimization approach tailored to the specific function.







\subsubsection{Minimizing Errors in the Received Data with a Guaranteed Data Transmission Time}

In certain scenarios, scientists prioritize receiving data rapidly within a deterministic timeframe, even if it contains some level of error, rather than waiting for a prolonged and unpredictable period to obtain highly accurate data. 
%This is particularly common in exploratory research or the early stages of experimentation, where approximate results are sufficient to guide immediate actions. 
%For example, in scientific data visualization, researchers often aim to quickly render preliminary visual representations of large datasets. These visualizations, even if imperfect, can help scientists identify trends, spot anomalies, or formulate hypotheses without needing fully precise data. 
For example, in scientific visualization, quickly rendering preliminary representations of large datasets allows researchers to identify trends, detect anomalies, or formulate hypotheses, even if the data is imperfect.
%This fast feedback loop accelerates the decision-making process, allowing researchers to refine their approaches or focus on specific areas of interest while awaiting more accurate data for in-depth analyses. 

%To support this type of requirement, retransmissions must be disabled, as they introduce non-deterministic delays that conflict with the need for predictable transmission times. In this case, fault tolerance is ensured solely through parity fragments generated via erasure coding. Consequently, the key challenge lies in determining the optimal number of refactored data levels to transmit and the appropriate number of parity fragments to generate for each fault-tolerant group at each level, so that the total transmission time is within the user-specified timeframe while at the same time the error in the received data is minimized. To address this challenge, the following optimization model is formulated.
To ensure predictable transmission times, retransmissions must be minimized to avoid introducing non-deterministic delays. As a result, fault tolerance relies primarily on parity fragments generated by erasure coding. The key challenge is to determine which levels to transmit and the appropriate number of parity fragments created for the FTG at each level, so that the total transmission time stays within the user-specified timeframe while minimizing errors in the received data. The optimization model presented below is developed to address this challenge.

Assume the size of the $j$-th level of the refactored data is $S_j$, and $m_j$ parity fragments are generated for this level. Consequently, the number of FTGs required for the first level can be expressed as: $N_j = \frac{S_j}{(n-m_j)s}$. 
If the first $l$ out of $L$ levels of the refactored data are sent without retransmission, the total transmission time can be calculated as:
\begingroup
\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{3pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{3pt}
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
T_{total} =& t+\frac{n(N_1+N_2+\ldots+N_l)-1}{r} \\
=& t+\frac{n\sum\limits_{j=1}^{l}\frac{S_j}{(n-m_j)s}-1}{r}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\endgroup
Let the user-specified constraint on total transmission time be $\tau$. Given that $0 \leq m_j \leq n/2$, the range of $l$ 
can be roughly estimated by solving the following inequality: 
\begingroup
\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{3pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{3pt}
\begin{equation}
\label{l_range}
  t+\frac{n\sum\limits_{j=1}^{l}\frac{S_j}{(n-0)s}-1}{r} \leq T_{total} \leq t+\frac{n\sum\limits_{j=1}^{l}\frac{S_j}{(n-\frac{n}{2})s}-1}{r} \leq \tau
\end{equation}
\endgroup
%The value of $l$ obtained this way represents a lower bound. In practice, the number of parity fragments for each level can be reduced based on the packet loss rate to enable the transmission of more levels while still meeting the time constraint.

To estimate errors in the received data, let $p_1$ denote the probability that an FTG in the first level experiences unrecoverable data loss. Assuming such losses are independent across groups, the probability that the first level cannot be recovered is $1-(1-p_1)^{N_1}$. Since the first level is essential for data reconstruction, its loss results in a relative error of $\varepsilon_0 = 1$. If the first level is successfully recovered but the second level is not, the data can still be reconstructed with an error of $\varepsilon_1$, occurring with probability $(1-p_1)^{N_1}[1-(1-p_2)^{N_2}]$. Similarly, if the first two levels are recovered but the third fails, the error is $\varepsilon_2$, with probability $(1-p_1)^{N_1}(1-p_2)^{N_2}[1-(1-p_3)^{N_3}]$. Following this pattern, for  $l$ transmitted levels, the expected error of the reconstructed data is:
\begingroup
\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{3pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{3pt}
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\label{epsilon}
    E[\varepsilon]=&[1-(1-p_1)^{\frac{S_1}{(n-m_1)s}}]\varepsilon_0+ \\
                   &\sum\limits_{i=2}^{l-1}\prod\limits_{j=1}^{i-1}(1-p_j)^{\frac{S_j}{(n-m_j)s}}[1-(1-p_i)^{\frac{S_i}{(n-m_i)s}}]\varepsilon_{i-1}+ \\
                   &\prod\limits_{j=1}^{l}(1-p_j)^{\frac{S_j}{(n-m_j)s}}\varepsilon_{l}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\endgroup
As previously discussed, the method for calculating $p_j$ depends on the packet loss rate. When the packet loss rate is relatively low, $p_j$ is calculated using~\autoref{p}. Conversely, when the packet loss rate is high and correlations between FTGs become significant, $p_j$ is determined using~\autoref{p_big_lambda}. 

%The overarching objective of this optimization problem is to determine the number of levels $l$ and the optimal fault tolerance configuration for each level ($m_{1}, m_{2}, \ldots, m_{l}$) that minimizes the expected relative L-infinity error while satisfying the user-specified constraint on total transmission time denoted by $\tau$. 
In summary, the complete optimization model, combining all components, is formulated as a nonlinear integer programming problem as follows:
\begingroup
\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{3pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{3pt}
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\label{epsilon_opt}
\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{m_{j}, \forall j \in \{1,\ldots,l\}} & \quad [1-(1-p_1)^{\frac{S_1}{(n-m_1)s}}]\varepsilon_0+ \\
                   &\quad \sum\limits_{i=2}^{l-1}\prod\limits_{j=1}^{i-1}(1-p_j)^{\frac{S_j}{(n-m_j)s}}[1-(1-p_i)^{\frac{S_i}{(n-m_i)s}}]\varepsilon_{i-1}+ \\
                   &\quad \prod\limits_{j=1}^{l}(1-p_j)^{\frac{S_j}{(n-m_j)s}}\varepsilon_{l} \\
\text{s.t.}& \quad  t+\frac{n\sum\limits_{j=1}^{l}\frac{S_j}{(n-m_j)s}-1}{r} \leq \tau 
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\endgroup
This model can be solved using numerical solvers such as SCIP~\cite{BolusaniEtal2024OO} or other optimization tools capable of handling nonlinear integer programming.



\section{Adaptive Data Transfer Protocols}
\label{sec:algorithm}

In real-world networks, packet loss rates often fluctuate over time, introducing variability that can impact data transfer performance. Consequently, the fault-tolerance configurations derived from optimization models based on constant packet loss rates may become suboptimal, potentially degrading transmission efficiency or violating the user-specified constraints on total transmission time. To address this challenge, we integrate adaptive mechanisms into the data transfer protocols, which can dynamically adjust fault-tolerance configurations in response to changing packet loss rates, ensuring that the two user requirements outlined in~\autoref{sec:optimization_models} are consistently satisfied.

\subsection{Data Transfer with Guaranteed Error Bound}

The protocol for transferring data between sender and receiver with guaranteed error bound is shown in~\autoref{alg:transfer_with_err_bound}.


{\footnotesize
\begin{algorithm}[h]
\caption{Data Transfer with Guaranteed Error Bound}
\label{alg:transfer_with_err_bound}
\SetKwInput{Input}{Input}
\SetKwBlock{Sender}{Sender}{}
\SetKwBlock{ThreadOne}{Parity Generation Thread}{}
\SetKwBlock{ThreadTwo}{Transmission Thread}{}
\SetKwBlock{Receiver}{Receiver}{}
\SetKw{Break}{break}

\Sender{
\Input{$\varepsilon$, $s$, $t$, $n$, $r_{ec}$, $r_{link}$, $\lambda$}
\ThreadOne{
Determine $l$ such that $\varepsilon_{l}\leq \varepsilon< \varepsilon_{l-1}$\;
Divide $l$ levels into $d=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{l}S_i}{s}$ data fragments\;
Compute $r=min\{r_{ec}, r_{link}\}$\;
%Calculate $p = \begin{cases} \text{\autoref{p}},& \text{if $\frac{\lambda r}{n} \leq 1$} \\ \text{\autoref{p_big_lambda}},& \text{otherwise}\end{cases}$\;
Solve~\autoref{ETotal_opt} to obtain $m$\;
\ForEach{$k$ ($k=n-m$) data fragments}{
Generate $m$ parity fragments to form an FTG\;
Store data and parity fragments of this FTG to $buf$\;
\If{updated $\lambda$ notified by Receiver}{
Update $m$ by solving~\autoref{ETotal_opt} again\;
}
}
}
\ThreadTwo{
%Obtain $N \gets \frac{S}{(n-m)s}$\;
\While{$d>0$}{
Transmit fragments of an FTG in $buf$\;
$d \gets d-k$\;
}
Notify Receiver transmission ended\;
\While{True}{
Wait for $lost\_FTGs\_list$ from Receiver\;
\If{$lost\_FTGs\_list$ is empty}{
\Break
}
\ForEach{$lost\_FTG$ in $lost\_FTGs\_list$}{
Obtain fragments of $lost\_FTG$ from $buf$ and transmit\;
}
Notify Receiver transmission ended\;
}

}
}
\Receiver{
\Input{$T_W$}
Initialize $t_{start} \gets t_0$, $lost \gets 0$\;
\While{True}{
Receive fragments from Sender\;
\ForEach{$FTG$} {
\If{fragment loss occurs}{
Increment $lost$ by number of lost fragments\;
\If{data fragment loss occurs}{
\If{number of lost fragments $\leq m$}{
Recover lost data fragments\;
}
\Else{
Add $FTG$ to $lost\_FTGs\_list$\;
}
}
}
}
\If{$t_{now}-t_{start}\geq T_W$}{
Update $\lambda \gets \frac{lost}{T_W}$ and notify Sender\;
$t_{start} \gets t_{now}$, $lost \gets 0$
}
\If{transmission ended notified by Sender}{
Send $lost\_FTGs\_list$ to Sender\;
\If{$lost\_FTGs\_list$ is empty}{
\Break
}
}
}
}
\end{algorithm}
}

The sender is designed with multithreading capabilities to manage parity generation and data transmission simultaneously. In particular, the parity generation thread begins by analyzing the user-specified error bound to determine the appropriate number of levels for transmission. It then partitions the data into fragments and calculates the required number of parity fragments ($m$) needed for each FTG. This calculation involves solving the optimization problem defined in~\autoref{ETotal_opt}, which depends on parameters such as the actual packet transmission rate ($r$) and the packet loss rate ($\lambda$) in lost packets per second. Notably, the actual packet transmission rate is determined as the minimum of the parity fragment generation rate ($r_{ec}$) and the network link transmission rate ($r_{link}$) for UDP packet of size $s$. The parity generation thread continuously generates $m$ parity fragments for every $k$ data fragments to form an FTG while monitoring for updates to $\lambda$ from the receiver. If the packet loss rate changes, the thread dynamically recalculates $m$, ensuring the system adapts to current network conditions. Simultaneously, the transmission thread handles the actual sending of fragments in FTGs. Upon completing the transmission of all data fragments, it notifies the receiver and waits for feedback on any lost FTGs. The thread then retransmits the lost FTGs iteratively until list of lost FTGs returned by the receiver is empty.

The receiver operates continuously to handle incoming fragments from the sender. For each FTG, it verifies if any fragment loss has occurred and counts the number of lost fragments within a user-specified time window, $T_W$. If data fragment loss is detected and the number of lost fragments in the FTG does not exceed $m$, the receiver recovers the missing data fragments using the remaining data and parity fragments. If recovery is not possible, the FTG is marked as unrecoverable and added to a list of lost FTGs. Once $T_W$ has elapsed, the receiver calculates the updated packet loss rate, $\lambda$ based on the total fragment losses within the time window and notifies the sender with the new $\lambda$. Additionally, when notified by the sender that the transmission has concluded, the receiver provides the sender with the list of lost FTGs for retransmission.

