}

Similar to~\autoref{alg:transfer_with_err_bound}, the sender in~\autoref{alg:transfer_with_time} leverages multithreading to manage parity generation and data transmission concurrently. The parity generation thread begins by calculating the effective packet transmission rate ($r$), determined as the minimum of the parity fragment generation rate ($r_{ec}$) and the network link transmission rate ($r_{link}$). Using this transmission rate, it solves~\autoref{l_range} to identify all feasible values for number of levels that can be transmitted within the given deadline $\tau$.  If no feasible value exists, the protocol raises an exception, indicating that the specified deadline is too stringent for the current system configuration. Once all feasible values are identified, the parity generation thread refines the selection by determining the optimal number of levels $l$ to transmit and calculating the most effective combination of redundancy parameters $[m_1,m_2,\ldots,m_l]$ for each level. This is achieved by solving the optimization problem defined in~\autoref{epsilon_opt}, which minimizes the overall error bound ($\varepsilon$) while satisfying the timing and transmission constraints. Each level is then divided into data fragments of size $s$. For the $i$-th level, the parity generation thread generates $m_i$ parity fragments for every $k_i$ ($k_i=n-m_i$) data fragments to form an FTG. The parity generation thread is designed to dynamically adapt to changes in network conditions. If the receiver notifies the sender of an updated packet loss rate ($\lambda$), the thread recalculates the redundancy parameters $m_i$ by solving~\autoref{epsilon_opt}. However, FTGs that have already been transmitted are excluded from consideration, and the time elapsed since the transmission began is deducted from the remaining deadline $\tau$ when solving~\autoref{epsilon_opt}.

The receiver continuously processes incoming fragments and monitors fragment losses. For each FTG, it extracts the level-specific redundancy parameter ($m_i$) from the fragments' metadata to determine recoverability. If data fragments are lost and the total number of lost fragments does not exceed $m_i$, the receiver restores the missing fragments. Otherwise, the affected level is marked as unrecoverable. The receiver also maintains a time window ($T_W$) to calculate the packet loss rate ($\lambda$) and periodically notifies the sender of updated $\lambda$ values. Once the sender signals the end of transmission, the receiver concludes its operations.

\section{Evaluation}
\label{sec:evaluation}

Simulation serves as the primary evaluation method for the proposed adaptive data transfer protocols due to its ability to provide a controlled and flexible environment. Unlike real networks, where packet loss rates are unpredictable and difficult to control, simulation allows systematic exploration of protocol behavior under diverse and extreme packet loss conditions. %To ensure the fidelity of the simulation results, the selection of simulation parameters is grounded in measurements obtained from real-world networks. 
To complement the simulation-based evaluation, we also conducted real network testing focused on validating the functionality of the proposed protocols in practical scenarios. %This dual approach ensures both a thorough analysis of performance under controlled conditions and confirmation of operational feasibility in real-world networks. 

\subsection{Testing Data and Network}
The scientific data used in our evaluation was generated by the Nyx cosmology simulation~\cite{Nyx} and refactored into a hierarchical representation with four levels using the pMGARD~\cite{liang2021error} library. The sizes of the four levels were $S_1=668$~MB, $S_2=2.67$~GB, $S_3=5.42$~GB, and $S_4=17.99$~GB. The relative L-infinity errors for reconstructing the data using the first $i$ levels of the hierarchy, denoted as $\varepsilon_i$, were set as follows: $\varepsilon_1=0.004$, $\varepsilon_2=0.0005$, $\varepsilon_3=0.00006$, and $\varepsilon_4=0.0000001$. Note that we do not evaluate data refactoring in this work, as it is typically performed during the data generation process and stored on the system where it is produced before any transfer occurs. Evaluations of data refactoring techniques can be found in existing works, such as~\cite{liang2021error, wan2023rapids}.
The testing network was configured with the sender deployed on a workstation at our university and the receiver operating on a virtual machine hosted by CloudLab~\cite{cloudlab}, a cloud infrastructure that is part of the National Science Foundation's NSFCloud program.

