Second, we evaluated the data transfer process under a guaranteed transmission time constraint, which enables users to receive data within a specified time frame while accepting the trade-off that the error bound of the received data may not be strictly guaranteed. As shown in~\autoref{fig:constant_lambda_guaranteed_error}, the minimum transmission times required to transfer all levels of the refactored data under different packet loss rates were 378.03 seconds for $\lambda=19$, 401.11 seconds for $\lambda=383$, and 429.75 seconds for $\lambda=957$. These values were subsequently used as the transmission time constraint $\tau$ in solving the optimization model presented in~\autoref{epsilon_opt}. The optimization results indicate that to minimize the error bound of the received data, the optimal number of parity fragments per FTG at all four levels should be set as follows: for $\lambda=19$, the values are $m_1=5$, $m_2=4$, $m_3=2$, and $m_4=0$; for $\lambda=383$, the values are $m_1=8$, $m_2=7$, $m_3=7$, and $m_4=0$; and for $\lambda=957$, the values are $m_1=12$, $m_2=11$, $m_3=11$, and $m_4=0$. To evaluate the effectiveness of these fault-tolerance configurations, we conducted simulations using the derived configurations, running each scenario 100 times to account for the nondeterministic nature of data errors. We recorded the number of instances where the received data satisfied different error bounds, such as $\varepsilon_{0}$, $\varepsilon_{1}$, and so on. This approach allowed us to quantify the frequency of achieving varying levels of data accuracy under the given configurations. Additionally, to highlight the advantages of the optimized configurations, we performed simulations with alternative configurations where the number of parity fragments per FTG was set uniformly across all four levels. As illustrated in~\autoref{fig:constant_lambda_guaranteed_time}, the optimized configurations consistently ensured that the transmission time remained within the specified constraint while achieving a high likelihood of maintaining a low error bound ($\varepsilon_{3}$) in the received data. In contrast, the alternative configurations either met the time constraint but failed to recover the data entirely (resulting in an error bound of $\varepsilon_{0}$), or exceeded the specified transmission time constraint.

\subsubsection{Data Transfer under Time-Varying Packet Loss Rates}
When the packet loss rate changes over time, a static fault-tolerance configuration fails to achieve optimal transmission performance. We conducted simulation-based evaluations to assess the effectiveness of the proposed data transfer protocols in dynamically adjusting fault-tolerance configurations in response to time-varying packet loss rates.

First, we conducted simulations to evaluate the performance of three data transfer approaches under a guaranteed error bound: the TCP protocol, a UDP-based protocol incorporating erasure coding with a static number of parity fragments per FTG, and the proposed adaptive protocol shown in~\autoref{alg:transfer_with_err_bound}. As illustrated in~\autoref{fig:lambda_time_varying}, the adaptive protocol consistently outperforms the other two approaches under time-varying packet loss rates. Notably, the adaptive protocol completes the data transmission in 388.8 seconds, reducing the transmission time by 30 seconds compared to the minimum time achieved by the static fault-tolerance configuration.

