\subsection{Minimal vs. non-minimal coupling
\label{non-minimal}}

So far, our analysis has been based on the assumption of minimal coupling between gravity and fermionic fields. However, some authors have pointed out that this procedure may not be adequate in curved spacetime \cite{Bojowald:2010qpa, PhysRevD.73.084016, Alexandrov:2008iy}. In particular, they found that employing minimal coupling can result in a system that lacks parity invariance. As noted in \cite{Das:2008r}, this issue may stem from sign inconsistencies or the choice of representation for the gamma matrices. Although, in our work we did not encounter this problem, nevertheless, for the sake of completeness, we now examine how our earlier results are affected when non-minimal coupling is assumed, focusing specifically on the case of Weyl fermions. Generalization to Dirac spinors is straightforward.



% However, some authors have pointed out that this procedure may not be adequate in curved spacetime \cite{Bojowald:2010qpa, Das:2008r, PhysRevD.73.084016, Alexandrov:2008iy}. 

