%&-\frac{i}{2}\sqrt{\text{det}\,h}\left(\overline{\Psi}\gamma^{0}(1-i\alpha\gamma_5)\mathfrak{D}_{a}\Psi+\overline{\mathfrak{D}_{a}\Psi}(1-i\alpha\gamma_5)\gamma^{0}\Psi\right).
%\end{align}
%Now the compatibility condition is
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
    \mathcal{D}_{b}(|e|e_{K}^{[a}e_{L}^{b]})=&\frac{\kappa}{4}\frac{\beta^{2}}{1+\beta^{2}}|e|\Bigl[\left(1-\frac{\alpha}{\beta}\right)\epsilon_{KL}{}^{MN}e_{M}^{a}J_{N}-\left(\frac{1}{\beta} + \alpha\right)\left(e_{K}^{a}J_{L}-e_{L}^{a}J_{K}\right)\Bigr].
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Consequently, the spin connection is modified as follow
\begin{equation}
    \Gamma_a^i = \tilde{\Gamma}_a^i + \frac{\kappa}{4}\frac{\beta^2}{1+\beta^2}\Bigl[\left(1-\frac{\alpha}{\beta}\right)e_a^i J^0 - \left(\frac{1}{\beta}+\alpha\right)\epsilon^i\,_{jk}e_a^jJ^k \Bigr].
\end{equation}
Finally, imposing the new compatibility condition on the Gauss constraint leads to
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{G}_i^{\text{G+F}} = \frac{1}{\kappa}\epsilon_{ij}\,^k K_a^jE_k^a + \frac{1}{2}\frac{\beta^2}{1+\beta^2}\left(1-\frac{\alpha}{\beta}\right)\sqrt{\text{det}\,h}J_{i}
\end{equation}


\section{Discussion and conclusions \label{sec:Conclusion}}

In this work we have provided the full analysis of the canonical formulation of the system of gravity, fermions, and photons, and their interaction in a background-independent way. In particular, we have considered the interaction term of the QED, which to our knowledge, has not been considered in previous studies. 

We have analyzed the constraint structure of the system in the Ashtekar-Barbero variables, with either Weyl or Dirac fermions, and have shown how the connection variables are modified due to the presence of fermions. Furthermore, the expression for the Hamiltonian, spatial diffeomorphism and Guass constraints are given in a way that the contribution of each of the sectors, i.e., gravity, fermions, and photons, are clear.  
We have also derived the EOM of the matter sector, which reduce to the standard EOM of QFT on fixed curved or flat spacetime in the appropriate limits. The behavior of the system under parity transformation is studied in detail. Finally, we presented the modifications to the constraints arising from the introduction of non-minimal coupling between gravity and fermions.

This analysis and the results pave the way for further canonical analysis of realistic matter in the universe involved in electromagnetic interactions in strong and dynamical (non-fixed) gravity regimes. Most importantly, this work opens the door for further detailed studies of an interactive system (QED in a dynamical background) using loop quantum gravity, polymer quantization, generalized uncertainty principle, or other theory that uses canonical quantization techniques. A rather immediate application of this analysis is in symmetry-reduced systems such as cosmological models involving electromagnetic matter, or collapse scenarios in spherical symmetric or rotating black holes, which up to now, have been done almost exclusively only with scalar fields.


