\section{Complementary information on fermions}
\label{App_fermions}
Appendix \ref{App_fermions} serves to clarify the notation adopted for the fermionic field, the gamma matrices, and their associated identities. Given the ambiguity that exists in the literature, this appendix establishes the conventions used throughout the paper to ensure consistency. 


\subsection{Notes on helicity and chirality\label{chirality-helicity}}

In this subsection, we introduce some key concepts related to helicity
and chirality \cite{Pal:2011pb, Schwartz:2014sze}, which
will help clarify the discussions that follow.

 A general definition of ``handedness'' applicable to any particle
involves the relative orientation between its momentum and angular
momentum. For a particle with three-momentum $\mathbf{p}$, \textit{helicity}
is defined as 
\begin{equation}
h_{\mathbf{p}}\coloneqq\frac{2\mathbf{J}\cdot\mathbf{p}}{|\mathbf{p}|}\,,
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{J}$ is the total angular momentum of the particle.
Since the orbital angular momentum is always perpendicular to the
direction of motion, it does not contribute to helicity. Therefore,
helicity effectively measures twice the projection of the spin along
the direction of momentum. Defining the spin matrices as $\frac{1}{2}\Sigma$,
the helicity operator can be rewritten as
\begin{equation}
h_{\mathbf{p}}\coloneqq\frac{\Sigma\cdot\mathbf{p}}{|\mathbf{p}|}\,.
\end{equation}
It follows that the eigenvalues of $h_{\mathbf{p}}$ are $\pm1$.
An eigenstate with eigenvalue $-1$ is referred to as ``left-handed'',
while one with eigenvalue $+1$ is called ``right-handed''.

A key property of helicity is that it commutes with the Dirac Hamiltonian,
which means it is conserved in coordinate time. Moreover, helicity
is invariant under rotations, as evident from the rotational invariance
of the dot product in its definition. In other words, different observers
related by a spatial rotation will agree on the helicity of a given
particle. However, helicity is not invariant under Lorentz boosts.
As a result, a massive fermion\footnote{The change in helicity can only occur in frames moving faster than
the particle. Since such a frame cannot exist for a massless particle,
helicity remains invariant in that case.} cannot be strictly left- or right-helical in an absolute sense; the
helicity depends on the observer's frame of reference.

With regard to the definition of \textit{chirality}, it is associated
with the matrix $\gamma_{5}$ (see Appendix \ref{qft} for its properties).
This matrix allows us to construct projection operators that act on
fermion fields as follows
\begin{align}
\mathds{L}= & \frac{1}{2}\left(1-\gamma_{5}\right), & \mathds{R}= & \frac{1}{2}\left(1+\gamma_{5}\right)\,.
\end{align}
These projection operators are commonly referred to as ``left-chiral''
and ``right-chiral'', respectively. Given any Dirac spinor $\Psi(x)$
that satisfies the Dirac equation, we can decompose it into its left-
and right-chiral components as
\begin{equation}
\Psi=\Psi_{L}+\Psi_{R},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{align}
\Psi_{L}= & \mathds{L}\Psi, & \Psi_{R}= & \mathds{R}\Psi\,.
\end{align}
An important feature of chirality is that a left-chiral solution to
the Dirac equation remains left-chiral under Lorentz transformations.
This is ensured by the fact that $\gamma_{5}$ anticommutes with all
gamma matrices, allowing the chiral projections to be defined in a
Lorentz covariant manner. However, chirality is not conserved for
a massive free particle, as $\gamma_{5}$ does not commute with the
mass term in the Dirac Hamiltonian. Since the mass term involves only
one gamma matrix, it anticommutes with $\gamma_{5}$, leading to a
mixing of chiral components. In contrast, there is no such issue with
the kinetic (derivative) term, which preserves chirality.

In this sense, chirality and helicity exhibit complementary behaviors:
helicity is conserved for a free particle but is not Lorentz invariant,
whereas chirality is Lorentz invariant but not conserved when the
particle has mass.

In the massless case, applying the Dirac equation to a spinor eigenstate
of 3-momentum $\mathbf{p}$, we find 
\begin{equation}
\gamma_{5}w_{\mathbf{p}}=\frac{\Sigma\cdot\mathbf{p}}{|\mathbf{p}|}w_{\mathbf{p}}\,,
\end{equation}
where $w_{\mathbf{p}}$ denotes a general spinor. This relation shows
that chirality and helicity coincide for massless spinors: the action
of $\gamma_{5}$ on the spinor yields its helicity. In this limit,
chirality becomes a physical observable and Lorentz-invariant quantity.

When mass is introduced, the equations of motion cause mixing between
left- and right-chiral components. However, since momentum and spin
remain good quantum numbers in the free theory ---even for massive
particles--- helicity is still conserved. As a result, helicity remains
a useful and meaningful concept in the massive case. The crucial distinction
is that, in the presence of mass, helicity eigenstates no longer coincide
with chirality eigenstates.




\subsection{Weyl fermions in flat spacetime\label{qft}}

In order to maintain consistency with the majority of the literature
regarding fermionic matter \cite{Schwartz:2014sze,Weinberg:1995s,Peskin:1995ev},
we will switch to the standard QFT signature convention, $(+\,-\,-\,-)$,
\textbf{for Section \ref{qft} only}.

In general, elements of the vector space on which the spin-$\frac{1}{2}$
representations act are known as spinors. When we consider massless
fermions, we can talk of positive or negative helicity fermion or
of left or right chiral fermion \footnote{In the massless case, helicity are chiraly are related to each other
(see Appendix \ref{chirality-helicity}).}. It is well known that the proper Lorentz algebra $\mathfrak{so}(1,3)$ is isomorphic to the Lie algebra of
$SU(2)\times SU(2)$, meaning that any
representation of the Lorentz algebra can be characterized by how
it transforms under each of the two $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ factors. In this framework, a left-chiral
fermion transforms as a doublet under one $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ and as a singlet under the other, denoted
by the representation $(\frac{1}{2},0)$. Conversely, a right-chiral
fermion transforms as $(0,\frac{1}{2})$. These are known as Weyl
fermions \cite{Pal:2011pb,Schwartz:2014sze}
\begin{align}
\psi_{R}= & \begin{pmatrix}\psi_{1}(x)\\
\psi_{2}(x)
\end{pmatrix}, & \text{for the \ensuremath{\left(0,\frac{1}{2}\right)} representation}\,,\\
\psi_{L}= & \begin{pmatrix}\psi_{1}(x)\\
\psi_{2}(x)
\end{pmatrix}, & \text{for the \ensuremath{\left(\frac{1}{2},0\right)} representation}\,.
\end{align}
Under rotational angles $\theta_{i}$ and boost angles $\omega_{i}$,
they transform as 
\begin{align}
\psi_{L}\rightarrow\Lambda_{L}\psi_{L}= & e^{i\frac{\bar{\sigma}}{2}\left(\bar{\theta}+i\bar{\omega}\right)}\psi_{L}, & \psi_{R}\rightarrow\Lambda_{R}\psi_{R}= & e^{i\frac{\bar{\sigma}}{2}\left(\bar{\theta}-i\bar{\omega}\right)}\psi_{R}\,.
\end{align}
Since $\Lambda_{L}$ and $\Lambda_{R}$ are complex, so are $\psi_{L}$
and $\psi_{R}$ . Similarly, 
\begin{align}
\psi_{L}^{\dagger}\rightarrow & \psi_{L}^{\dagger}e^{-i\frac{\bar{\sigma}}{2}\left(\bar{\theta}-i\bar{\omega}\right)}, & \psi_{R}^{\dagger}\rightarrow & \psi_{R}^{\dagger}e^{-i\frac{\bar{\sigma}}{2}\left(\bar{\theta}+i\bar{\omega}\right)}\,.
\end{align}
By definition, Weyl fermions are massless, so we are only concerned
with the kinetic term of their Lagrangian. To derive this term, consider
the vector quantities $v^{i}=\psi_{R}^{\dagger}\sigma^{i}\psi_{R}$
and $\tilde{v}^{i}=\psi_{L}^{\dagger}\sigma^{i}\psi_{L}$, where $\sigma^{i}$
denotes the $i$th Pauli matrix. Using the well-known identities of
the Pauli matrices, 
\begin{align}
\left[\sigma^{i},\sigma^{j}\right]= & 2i\epsilon^{ij}\,_{k}\sigma^{k}\,, & \left\{ \sigma^{i},\sigma^{j}\right\} = & 2\delta^{ij}\mathbb{1}\,,
\end{align}
we can compute the infinitesimal transformations of $v^{i}$ and $\tilde{v}^{i}$
under Lorentz transformations. This yields 
\begin{align}
\delta v^{i}= & -\epsilon^{ij}\,_{k}\theta_{j}\psi_{R}^{\dagger}\sigma^{k}\psi_{R}+\omega^{i}\psi_{R}^{\dagger}\psi_{R}\,, & \delta\tilde{v}^{i}= & -\epsilon^{ij}\,_{k}\theta_{j}\psi_{L}^{\dagger}\sigma^{k}\psi_{L}-\omega^{i}\psi_{L}^{\dagger}\psi_{L}\,.
\end{align}
Thus, both $v^{i}$ and $\tilde{v}^{i}$ transform as the spatial
parts of a Lorentz 4-vector $\delta V^{\mu}=\Lambda_{\nu}^{\mu}V^{\nu}$
with $\Lambda_{i}^{0}=\omega^{i}$ and $\Lambda_{j}^{i}=\epsilon^{i}\,_{jk}\theta^{k}$.
We can define 
\begin{align}
v^{\mu}= & (\psi_{R}^{\dagger}\psi_{R},\psi_{R}^{\dagger}\sigma^{i}\psi_{R})\,, & \tilde{v}^{\mu}= & (\psi_{L}^{\dagger}\psi_{L},-\psi_{L}^{\dagger}\sigma^{i}\psi_{L})\,,
\end{align}
so that the combinations $\psi_{R,L}^{\dagger}\partial_{t}\psi_{R,L}$
transform as Lorentz invariants.

Defining the matrices 
\begin{align}
\sigma^{\mu}= & (\mathbb{1},\sigma^{i})\,, & \tilde{\sigma}^{\mu}= & (\mathbb{1},-\sigma^{i})\,,
\end{align}
we can write the previous vectors in the following form 
\begin{align}
v^{\mu}= & \psi_{R}^{\dagger}\sigma^{\mu}\psi_{R}\,, & \tilde{v}^{\mu}= & \psi_{L}^{\dagger}\tilde{\sigma}^{\mu}\psi_{L}\,,
\end{align}
and the kinetic terms of the Lagrangian become 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{L}_{R}= & i\psi_{R}^{\dagger}\sigma^{\mu}\partial_{\mu}\psi_{R}\,, & \mathcal{L}_{L}= & i\psi_{L}^{\dagger}\tilde{\sigma}^{\mu}\partial_{\mu}\psi_{L}\,.
\end{align}
Hence, the Dirac Lagrangian for Weyl fermions can be expressed as
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{Dirac}}=i\psi_{R}^{\dagger}\sigma^{\mu}\partial_{\mu}\psi_{R}+i\psi_{L}^{\dagger}\tilde{\sigma}^{\mu}\partial_{\mu}\psi_{L}\,.
\end{equation}
It is now possible to introduce a notation that can be used for arbitrary
fermionic field combining the two spinors into a four-component object
\begin{align}
\Psi= & \begin{pmatrix}\psi_{L}\\
\psi_{R}
\end{pmatrix}, & \Psi^{\dagger}= & \begin{pmatrix}\psi_{L}^{\dagger} & \psi_{R}^{\dagger}\end{pmatrix}.\label{4compontents}
\end{align}
In this basis, known as the Weyl (or chiral) representation, the gamma
matrices take the form 
\begin{equation}
\gamma^{\mu}=\begin{pmatrix}0 & \sigma^{\mu}\\
\tilde{\sigma}^{\mu} & 0
\end{pmatrix}\,,
\end{equation}
with components 
\begin{align}
\gamma^{0}= & \begin{pmatrix}0 & \mathbb{1}\\
\mathbb{1} & 0
\end{pmatrix}\,, & \gamma^{i}= & \begin{pmatrix}0 & \sigma^{i}\\
-\sigma^{i} & 0
\end{pmatrix}\,.\label{gammamatrix}
\end{align}
These matrices satisfy the Clifford algebra $\{\gamma^{\mu},\gamma^{\nu}\}=2\eta^{\mu\nu}$.

Moreover, we need to identify a suitable way to combine fermionic
fields in order to construct a Lorentz scalar. This is achieved by
introducing the Dirac adjoint (or conjugate) of the fermionic field,
defined using the zeroth gamma matrix 
\begin{equation}
\overline{\Psi}=\Psi^{\dagger}\gamma^{0}=\begin{pmatrix}\psi_{L}^{\dagger} & \psi_{R}^{\dagger}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}0 & \mathbb{1}\\
\mathbb{1} & 0
\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}\psi_{R}^{\dagger} & \psi_{L}^{\dagger}\end{pmatrix}\,.
\end{equation}
This definition ensures that the bilinear form $\overline{\Psi}\Psi$
transforms as a Lorentz scalar, and that the kinetic term $\overline{\Psi}\gamma^{\mu}\partial_{\mu}\Psi$
transforms as a Lorentz scalar density, preserving the overall covariance
of the Lagrangian. Thus, we arrive at the conventional form of the
Dirac Lagrangian for a Weyl fermionic field
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{Dirac}}=i\overline{\Psi}\gamma^{\mu}\partial_{\mu}\Psi\,.
\end{equation}




\subsection{Weyl fermions in curved spacetime \label{fermions-curved}}
Consider the fermionic field in a curved spacetime. In this context,
it is necessary to introduce a suitable covariant derivative that
accounts for the coupling between fermions and gravity. This is achieved
by incorporating the generalized connection 1-form $\omega_{a}^{IJ}$.
Hence, The covariant derivatives acting on the spinor field $\Psi$
and its conjugate field $\overline{\Psi}$ are defined respectively
as
\begin{align}
\mathfrak{D}_{\mu}\Psi= & \partial_{\mu}\Psi+\frac{1}{2}\omega_{\mu}^{IJ}\sigma_{IJ}\Psi\,,\label{derferm}\\
\overline{\mathfrak{D}_{\mu}\Psi} & =\partial_{\mu}\overline{\Psi}+\frac{1}{2}\omega_{\mu}^{IJ}\Psi^{\dagger}\sigma_{IJ}^{\dagger}\gamma^{0}\,,\label{derantiferm}
\end{align}
where $\sigma^{\mu\nu}\coloneqq\frac{1}{4}[\gamma^{\mu},\gamma^{\nu}]$
are the generators of the Lorentz group in the spinor representation.
Using this formalism, the action for a Weyl fermion in curved spacetime
is given by 
\begin{equation}
S_{F}=\frac{i}{2}\int d^{4}x|e|\Bigl\{ e_{I}^{\mu}\overline{\Psi}\gamma^{I}\mathfrak{D}_{\mu}\Psi-e_{I}^{\mu}\overline{\mathfrak{D}_{\mu}\Psi}\gamma^{I}\Psi\Bigr\}\,.\label{dirac-action}
\end{equation}
However, in General Relativity we adopt the mostly-plus metric signature
$(-,+,+,+)$, which requires a modification of some algebraic relations
involving the gamma matrices. While the Pauli matrices and their commutation
and anticommutation relations remain unchanged, the Clifford algebra
for the gamma matrices must be adapted accordingly. We choose to retain
the explicit form of the gamma matrices as given in Eq.\eqref{gammamatrix}.
However, due to the change in metric signature, the anticommutation
relation now reads: 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \gamma^{I},\gamma^{J}\right\} =-2\eta^{IJ}\,,\label{algebra}
\end{equation}
where $\eta^{IJ}$ is the Minkoswki metric. This relation leads to
the following properties for the gamma matrices: 
\begin{align}
\left(\gamma^{0}\right)^{\dagger}=\gamma^{0} & \implies\left(\gamma^{0}\right)^{2}=\mathbb{1}\,, & \left(\gamma^{i}\right)^{\dagger}=-\gamma^{i} & \implies\left(\gamma^{i}\right)^{2}=-\mathbb{1}\,,
\end{align}
which imply that $\gamma^{0}$ is Hermitian and unitary, while $\gamma^{i}$
are anti-Hermitian and unitary.

Moreover, adopting the GR signature, the definition of the $\gamma^{5}$
matrix becomes 
\begin{equation}
\gamma^{5}=i\gamma^{0}\gamma^{1}\gamma^{2}\gamma^{3}=\begin{pmatrix}-\mathbb{1} & 0\\
0 & \mathbb{1}
\end{pmatrix}\,.
\end{equation}

Unlike other approaches found in the literature \cite{Bojowald:2010qpa,Das:2008r},
we do not modify the gamma matrices or the fermionic fields when transitioning
to the curved spacetime formulation of the Dirac action. The key differences
between the flat and curved spacetime cases arise from the introduction
of the tetrad fields and a sign change in the anticommutation relations
of the gamma matrices, as shown in Eq. \eqref{algebra}. As a consequence,
several familiar identities involving gamma matrices are altered.
Below, we present a list of these modified identities, which can be
verified straightforwardly
\begin{align}
\gamma^{I}\gamma_{I}= & -4\mathbb{1}\,,\\
\gamma^{I}\gamma^{J}\gamma_{I}= & 2\gamma^{I}\,,\\
\gamma^{I}\gamma^{J}\gamma^{K}\gamma_{I}= & 4\eta^{JK}\mathbb{1}\,,\\
\gamma^{I}\gamma^{J}\gamma^{K}\gamma^{L}\gamma_{I}= & 2\gamma^{L}\gamma^{K}\gamma^{J}\,,\\
\gamma^{I}\gamma^{J}\gamma^{K}= & -\eta^{IJ}\gamma^{K}-\eta^{JK}\gamma^{I}+\eta^{IK}\gamma^{J}+i\epsilon^{LIJK}\gamma_{L}\gamma^{5}\,,\\
\epsilon^{IJKL}\sigma_{IJ}= & -2i\gamma^{5}\sigma^{KL}\,,\\
\gamma^{I}\sigma_{JK}+\sigma_{JK}\gamma^{I}= & \,i\epsilon^{I}\,_{JKL}\gamma_{5}\gamma^{L}\,,\\
\sigma_{JK}\gamma^{I}-\gamma^{I}\sigma_{JK} & =\eta_{J}^{I}\gamma_{K}-\eta_{K}^{I}\gamma_{J}\,.
\end{align}

\subsection{Dirac fermions in flat and curved spacetime \label{App_Dirac}}

A general fermion field transforms according to a reducible representation\footnote{A representation is called reducible if it can be decomposed into
a direct sum of irreducible representations, each of which transforms
independently under the group.} $(\frac{1}{2},0)\oplus(0,\frac{1}{2})$. This means that it can be
described in terms of two irreducible Weyl components: one left-chiral
and one right-chiral. This decomposition underscores the fundamental
role of Weyl fields, which serve as the basic building blocks from
which any fermionic field can be constructed.

We begin by considering the case of flat spacetime. To construct a
massive fermionic field, we consider the Lorentz-invariant combinations
$\psi_{L}^{\dagger}\psi_{R}$ and $\psi_{R}^{\dagger}\psi_{L}$, which
can be used to introduce a mass term in the Dirac Lagrangian: 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{mass}}=-m(\psi_{L}^{\dagger}\psi_{R}+\psi_{R}^{\dagger}\psi_{L})\,.
\end{equation}
When combined with the kinetic term, the resulting theory can describe
Majorana fermions, provided the fields are real solutions of the Dirac
equation. This would require imaginary gamma matrices. However, this
scenario will not be explored further in this work. Instead, we will
focus on generic complex solutions of the Dirac equation, without
imposing any reality conditions. This leads us to the Dirac field,
whose Lagrangian in terms of the Weyl components is given by
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{Dirac}}=i\psi_{R}^{\dagger}\sigma^{\mu}\partial_{\mu}\psi_{R}+i\psi_{L}^{\dagger}\tilde{\sigma}^{\mu}\partial_{\mu}\psi_{L}-m(\psi_{L}^{\dagger}\psi_{R}+\psi_{R}^{\dagger}\psi_{L})\,.
\end{equation}
The Dirac field is particularly useful for studying QED, which does
not distinguish between left- and right-handed components, i.e., it
is not a chiral theory. Since we are not concerned with analyzing
chirality in our discussion, working in the standard (Dirac) representation
is more convenient.

One of the future goals of this study will be to examine propagators
in a modified version of QED. Importantly, the choice of representation
for the gamma matrices (whether Dirac, Weyl, or Majorana) does not
affect the physical structure or form of the propagators. Therefore,
when referring to the Dirac field, we will adopt the four-component
spinor formalism as defined in \eqref{4compontents}, and in this
case, the Dirac Lagrangian takes the form 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{Dirac}}=i\overline{\Psi}\gamma^{\mu}\partial_{\mu}\Psi-m\overline{\Psi}\Psi\,,
\end{equation}
where the gamma matrices are given in Eq.\eqref{gammamatrix}.

To extend the previously discussed Lagrangian to curved spacetime,
we follow the procedure outlined in Appendix \ref{fermions-curved}. The
fermionic covariant derivative is introduced as defined in Eqs. \eqref{derferm}-\eqref{derantiferm}.
It is important to note that the mass term remains unchanged in curved
spacetime, as it does not involve any explicit gamma matrix or derivative
operators. As a result, the action for Dirac fields in a curved background
takes the form 
\begin{equation}
S_{F}=\int d^{4}x|e|\left\{ \frac{i}{2}\left(e_{I}^{a}\overline{\Psi}\gamma^{I}\mathfrak{D}_{a}\Psi-e_{I}^{a}\overline{\mathfrak{D}_{a}\Psi}\gamma^{I}\Psi\right)-m\overline{\Psi}\Psi\right\} \,.
\end{equation}
where all symbols carry their usual meaning. 

